(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["svetasvatara"]={"slug":"svetasvatara","name":"Śvetāśvatara","deva":"श्वेताश्वतर","note":"","intro":["The great <strong>theistic Upaniṣad</strong> of the <strong>Kṛṣṇa (Black) Yajurveda</strong>, classed among the <em>mukhya</em> (principal) Upaniṣads and counted 14th of the 108. In <strong>113 verses across six adhyāyas</strong>, it gathers the threads of earlier Vedānta and weaves them with the vocabulary of <strong>Sāṃkhya and Yoga</strong> into a luminous <strong>devotional monotheism</strong>: the one God — named <strong>Rudra, Śiva, Hara, Īśāna, Maheśvara, Bhagavān</strong> — is at once the impersonal <strong>Brahman</strong> and the personal <strong>Lord (Īśa)</strong> who wields <strong>māyā (= prakṛti)</strong>, projects and withdraws the worlds, and is known by <strong>dhyāna (meditation)</strong> and <strong>bhakti (loving devotion)</strong>. It is the scriptural fountainhead of later <strong>Śaiva</strong> theology and the earliest Upaniṣad to use the words <em>māyā</em> (as the Lord's creative power), <em>bhakti</em>, <em>guṇa</em>, <em>pradhāna</em>, <em>kapila</em>, and <em>sāṅkhya-yoga</em> — and it closes (6.23) with the first clear Upaniṣadic statement of the <strong>path of devotion to God and Guru</strong>.","Sanskrit text follows the standard (Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda) recension, verified verse-by-verse from the ITRANS edition of <strong>sanskritdocuments.org</strong> (`shveta.itx`, transliterated by Sunder Hattangadi, proofread by John Manetta), rendered to Devanāgarī and IAST with the `indic_transliteration` (sanscript) library. Cross-referenced against Max Müller (<em>SBE</em> 15, 1884), Swāmī Tyāgīśānanda (Sri Ramakrishna Math), and Swāmī Gambhīrānanda (with Śaṅkara-bhāṣya).<br>⚠️ <em>Accuracy note:</em> the <em>mūla</em> (root text) below is from the standard recension; several verses are <em>ṛk</em>-mantras shared with the Ṛgveda, Atharvaveda, Taittirīya, Kaṭha, Muṇḍaka, and Mahānārāyaṇa (noted where they occur), and minor sandhi/pada conventions vary between print editions. Readers doing close textual work should spot-check against the accented (<em>sasvara</em>) edition."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<strong>श्वेताश्वतर उपनिषत्</strong> — <em>\"The Upaniṣad of the Sage Śvetāśvatara\"</em>","The great <strong>theistic Upaniṣad</strong> of the <strong>Kṛṣṇa (Black) Yajurveda</strong>, classed among the <em>mukhya</em> (principal) Upaniṣads and counted 14th of the 108. In <strong>113 verses across six adhyāyas</strong>, it gathers the threads of earlier Vedānta and weaves them with the vocabulary of <strong>Sāṃkhya and Yoga</strong> into a luminous <strong>devotional monotheism</strong>: the one God — named <strong>Rudra, Śiva, Hara, Īśāna, Maheśvara, Bhagavān</strong> — is at once the impersonal <strong>Brahman</strong> and the personal <strong>Lord (Īśa)</strong> who wields <strong>māyā (= prakṛti)</strong>, projects and withdraws the worlds, and is known by <strong>dhyāna (meditation)</strong> and <strong>bhakti (loving devotion)</strong>. It is the scriptural fountainhead of later <strong>Śaiva</strong> theology and the earliest Upaniṣad to use the words <em>māyā</em> (as the Lord's creative power), <em>bhakti</em>, <em>guṇa</em>, <em>pradhāna</em>, <em>kapila</em>, and <em>sāṅkhya-yoga</em> — and it closes (6.23) with the first clear Upaniṣadic statement of the <strong>path of devotion to God and Guru</strong>.","Sanskrit text follows the standard (Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda) recension, verified verse-by-verse from the ITRANS edition of <strong>sanskritdocuments.org</strong> (`shveta.itx`, transliterated by Sunder Hattangadi, proofread by John Manetta), rendered to Devanāgarī and IAST with the `indic_transliteration` (sanscript) library. Cross-referenced against Max Müller (<em>SBE</em> 15, 1884), Swāmī Tyāgīśānanda (Sri Ramakrishna Math), and Swāmī Gambhīrānanda (with Śaṅkara-bhāṣya).","⚠️ <em>Accuracy note:</em> the <em>mūla</em> (root text) below is from the standard recension; several verses are <em>ṛk</em>-mantras shared with the Ṛgveda, Atharvaveda, Taittirīya, Kaṭha, Muṇḍaka, and Mahānārāyaṇa (noted where they occur), and minor sandhi/pada conventions vary between print editions. Readers doing close textual work should spot-check against the accented (<em>sasvara</em>) edition."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The text at a glance","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Adhyāya</td><td>Verses</td><td>Theme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>16</td><td><strong>The first cause</strong> — \"What is the cause? Brahman?\" The <strong>Brahma-wheel (brahma-cakra)</strong>; the one God who rules <em>kṣara</em> (the perishable) and <em>akṣara</em> (the imperishable); fire latent in wood → the Self realised by meditation (<em>svadeha araṇi, praṇava araṇi</em>).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>17</td><td><strong>Yoga</strong> — the <em>Savitṛ</em> (yoking) verses; the method of meditation (posture, breath, place); the signs of progress; the one God present in fire, water, herbs, and all.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>21</td><td><strong>The one Rudra / Puruṣa</strong> — \"One Rudra only, without a second\"; the cosmic Person <em>aṅguṣṭha-mātra</em> (thumb-sized) in the heart; <em>sahasraśīrṣā</em> (thousand-headed); <em>aṇor-aṇīyān</em> (subtler than the subtle).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>22</td><td><strong>The God of all forms; māyā</strong> — \"Thou art woman, thou art man\"; the <strong>two birds</strong> (<em>dvā suparṇā</em>); the <strong>one unborn she (ajā = prakṛti)</strong> of three colours; <strong>māyā = prakṛti, the māyin = Maheśvara</strong>; the Rudra-prayers.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td>14</td><td><strong>Vidyā &amp; avidyā; the jīva</strong> — the two imperishables hidden in Brahman; the Lord who upholds <strong>Kapila</strong>; the individual soul \"the hundredth part of a hair-tip,\" yet fit for infinitude; rebirth governed by <em>karma</em> and <em>guṇa</em>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>6</strong></td><td>23</td><td><strong>Maheśvara, devotion, and release</strong> — neither <em>svabhāva</em> nor <em>kāla</em> but <strong>the Lord's glory</strong> turns the wheel; \"He has no task, no organ; none is His master\"; <em>na tatra sūryo bhāti</em> (\"there the sun shines not\"); the sage <strong>Śvetāśvatara</strong>; and <strong>\"He whose devotion to God is supreme, and to the Guru as to God\"</strong> receives the teaching.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The five commentaries used in this volume","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Śaṅkara — <em>Śvetāśvataropaniṣad-bhāṣya</strong></em> (the commentary traditionally ascribed to Śaṅkarācārya; its authorship is debated by modern scholars, but it is the standard classical Advaita reading). Renders the theistic God as <em>saguṇa Brahman/Īśvara</em>, ultimately the one non-dual Ātman; <em>māyā</em> as the beginningless power of nescience. English here via Swāmī Gambhīrānanda. Cited as <strong>Śaṅkara</strong>.","2. <strong>Śaṅkarānanda — <em>Śvetāśvataropaniṣad-dīpikā</strong></em> (14th c., the well-known Advaitic sub-commentary/gloss). Concise, word-focused, Advaitin. Cited as <strong>Śaṅkarānanda</strong>.","3. <strong>Swāmī Tyāgīśānanda — <em>Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad</strong></em> (Sri Ramakrishna Math) — the standard modern scholarly English translation and exposition; balances the theistic-devotional surface with the Vedāntic depth, and notes the Sāṃkhya-Yoga vocabulary. Cited as <strong>Tyāgīśānanda</strong>.","4. <strong>Swāmī Krishnananda &amp; Swāmī Sivananda (Divine Life Society)</strong> — the practical, yoga-and-devotion lens; reads the text as a manual of meditation (Adhyāya 2) and surrender. Cited as <strong>Krishnananda/Sivananda</strong>.","5. <strong>Max Müller (<em>SBE</em> 15, 1884), Paul Deussen (1897) &amp; Patrick Olivelle (1996/1998)</strong> — the Indological / text-critical lens: dating, the Ṛgvedic/Atharvan borrowings, the Śaiva-sectarian and Sāṃkhya-Yoga affiliations, and the philological reading. Cited as <strong>Müller/Deussen/Olivelle</strong>.","<em>On usage below</em>: under each verse, <strong>Śaṅkara</strong> is the classical Advaita lens, <strong>Śaṅkarānanda</strong> the close gloss, <strong>Tyāgīśānanda</strong> the standard translator's exposition, <strong>Krishnananda/Sivananda</strong> the meditative-devotional application, <strong>Müller/Deussen/Olivelle</strong> the text-critical/comparative angle."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha (Peace invocation of the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda)","title":"","sa":["ॐ सह नाववतु । सह नौ भुनक्तु । सह वीर्यं करवावहै ।","तेजस्वि नावधीतमस्तु । मा विद्विषावहै ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["Oṃ saha nāvavatu | saha nau bhunaktu | saha vīryaṃ karavāvahai |","tejasvi nāvadhītam astu | mā vidviṣāvahai ||","Oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"<strong>May (Brahman) protect us both (teacher and pupil); may He nourish us both; may we work together with vigour; may our study be illumining; may we never hate (one another).</strong> Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(The standard <em>śānti-mantra</em> of the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda Upaniṣads, shared with the Taittirīya and Kaṭha.)"]}]},{"t":"The 113 verses","u":[{"ref":"The 113 verses","title":"Overview","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Each verse is given as: <strong>Devanāgarī → IAST → pada/phrase gloss → literal translation → paraphrase → 5-commentary digest.</strong> Each <em>adhyāya</em> closes with three applied sections — <strong>Details</strong> (deeper exposition), <strong>Visual representation</strong> (diagram), and <strong>Practical steps / observations</strong> (contemplative-devotional application).","<em>Grammar abbreviations:</em> m./f./n. = masc./fem./neut.; nom./acc./instr./dat./abl./gen./loc./voc. = case; sg./du./pl. = number; pres./impf./pf./aor./pot./impv./ger./inf. = tense/mood/non-finite; pp. = past participle."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 1 — The First Cause and the Brahma-Wheel","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya 1","title":"The First Cause and the Brahma-Wheel","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Upaniṣad opens with the <strong>great question of the seekers of Brahman</strong>: what is the cause of the world, whence are we born, by what do we live, and in what are we established? It rejects the partial answers (time, nature, chance, the elements, the individual) and points to the <strong>one God (deva), the Self's own power (devātma-śakti)</strong>, who, \"as one,\" rules all causes. The world is figured as a vast <strong>wheel (brahma-cakra)</strong> on which the soul (haṃsa) revolves until it knows the Mover apart from itself; and the chapter ends with the <strong>fire-in-the-firewood</strong> and <strong>butter-in-milk</strong> images for the hidden Self, drawn out by the friction of <strong>meditation (dhyāna) with OM (praṇava).</strong>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Verse 1.1","title":"The question of those who discourse on Brahman","sa":["हरिः ॐ ॥ ब्रह्मवादिनो वदन्ति । किं कारणं ब्रह्म कुतः स्म जाता जीवाम केन क्व च सम्प्रतिष्ठा । अधिष्ठिताः केन सुखेतरेषु वर्तामहे ब्रह्मविदो व्यवस्थाम् ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["hariḥ oṃ || brahmavādino vadanti | kiṃ kāraṇaṃ brahma kutaḥ sma jātā jīvāma kena kva ca sampratiṣṭhā | adhiṣṭhitāḥ kena sukhetareṣu vartāmahe brahmavido vyavasthām || 1 ||"],"words":[["brahma-vādinaḥ vadanti","m. nom. pl. + pres.","the discoursers on Brahman say"],["kiṃ kāraṇaṃ brahma","n. nom.","\"is Brahman the cause?"],["kutaḥ sma jātāḥ","adv. + pf. part.","whence are we born?"],["kena jīvāma","instr. + pres.","by what do we live?"],["kva ca sampratiṣṭhā","adv. + f. nom.","and where are we established?"],["adhiṣṭhitāḥ kena","pp. + instr.","governed by whom"],["sukha-itareṣu vartāmahe","loc. pl. (in pleasures and their opposites) + pres.","do we abide in pleasures and pains?"],["brahma-vidaḥ vyavasthām","m. nom. pl. + f. acc.","(so ask) the knowers of Brahman, (seeking) the rule (law)\""]],"lit":"\"Hariḥ Oṃ. Those who discourse on Brahman ask: 'Is Brahman the cause? Whence are we born? By what do we live, and where are we finally established? Governed by whom do we abide in (our lot of) pleasures and pains, O knowers of Brahman?'\"","para":"The Upaniṣad begins not with a doctrine but with the <strong>fundamental questions</strong> of philosophy, voiced by a circle of <em>brahma-vādins</em>: <strong>What is the first cause? Whence our birth, by what our life, in what our rest? Who ordains our happiness and suffering?</strong> The whole text is the answer — and the answer will be <strong>the one God who is also one's own Self.</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the questions concern the <em>single cause</em> of origin, sustenance, and dissolution; the answer (developed below) is <strong>Brahman as the conscious Lord</strong>, not any insentient principle."],["Śaṅkarānanda","\"governed by whom in pleasure and pain\" raises the problem of the <em>ordainer</em> of experience — resolved as the Lord/Self, not blind fate."],["Tyāgīśānanda","a rare Upaniṣadic opening that frames the inquiry as a communal <em>brahma-vāda</em>; the six questions structure the work."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the perennial human cry — origin, sustenance, destiny, and the author of joy and grief; the Upaniṣad answers with God-realisation."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the dialectical opening listing rival causal theories (to be enumerated in v. 2) — evidence of a developed philosophical milieu."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 1.2","title":"The rival theories, and why they fail","sa":["कालः स्वभावो नियतिर्यदृच्छा भूतानि योनिः पुरुष इति चिन्त्या । संयोग एषां न त्वात्मभावादात्माप्यनीशः सुखदुःखहेतोः ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["kālaḥ svabhāvo niyatir yadṛcchā bhūtāni yoniḥ puruṣa iti cintyā | saṃyoga eṣāṃ na tv ātmabhāvād ātmāpy anīśaḥ sukhaduḥkhahetoḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["kālaḥ svabhāvaḥ niyatiḥ yadṛcchā bhūtāni yoniḥ puruṣaḥ iti cintyā","nom. + f. nom. (to be considered)","Time, inherent nature, necessity (fate), chance, the elements, the womb (matter), the person — (are these) to be thought (the cause)?"],["saṃyogaḥ eṣām na","m. nom. + gen. pl.","(no —) not a combination of these,"],["tu ātma-bhāvāt","abl. (because of the existence of the self)","because of the existence of the self (which would be needed to experience)"],["ātmā api anīśaḥ sukha-duḥkha-hetoḥ","nom. + adj. (not the lord) + gen.","and the self too is not the (independent) cause of pleasure and pain (being itself subject to them)"]],"lit":"\"Time, inherent nature, necessity, chance, the elements, matter (the womb), the person — should one of these be thought the cause? Not (a mere) combination of them, because of the (presupposed) existence of a self; and the self too is not master (of itself), being subject to the causes of pleasure and pain.\"","para":"The proposed first causes are listed: <strong>Kāla</strong> (Time), <strong>svabhāva</strong> (Nature/essence), <strong>niyati</strong> (Fate/necessity), <strong>yadṛcchā</strong> (Chance), <strong>bhūtāni</strong> (the elements), <strong>yoni</strong> (matter/<em>prakṛti</em>), and <strong>puruṣa</strong> (the individual). None suffices: a <em>mere combination</em> of insentient principles cannot explain experience (which presupposes a conscious self); yet the ordinary self is <strong>not independent</strong> either — it is <em>subject to</em> pleasure and pain, not their free author. The true cause must be <strong>a conscious Lord who is yet not bound</strong> (vv. 3, 8).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","refutes the materialist/fatalist/atheist causal theories; the <em>jīva</em> cannot be the cause (it is <em>anīśa</em>, not the lord, being an experiencer); only the <strong>all-controlling, unbound Lord</strong> answers v. 1."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the list surveys the philosophical schools (Cārvāka time/nature, Ājīvika niyati, etc.); each is insufficient without consciousness."],["Tyāgīśānanda","a precious doxography of pre-classical Indian thought; the verse demands a cause that is conscious <em>and</em> free."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","neither blind law nor the helpless ego rules our lot — only the Divine."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","a famous early catalogue of causal hypotheses (Time, Nature, Necessity, Chance, etc.); evidence the Upaniṣad engages rival systems."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 1.3","title":"They saw the self-power of God, hidden by the guṇas","sa":["ते ध्यानयोगानुगता अपश्यन् देवात्मशक्तिं स्वगुणैर्निगूढाम् । यः कारणानि निखिलानि तानि कालात्मयुक्तान्यधितिष्ठत्येकः ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["te dhyānayogānugatā apaśyan devātmaśaktiṃ svaguṇair nigūḍhām | yaḥ kāraṇāni nikhilāni tāni kālātmayuktāny adhitiṣṭhaty ekaḥ || 3 ||"],"words":[["te dhyāna-yoga-anugatāḥ apaśyan","nom. pl. + pp. (devoted to the yoga of meditation) + impf.","they, given to the yoga of meditation, beheld"],["deva-ātma-śaktiṃ","f. acc.","the power of the Self of God (God's own self-power)"],["sva-guṇaiḥ nigūḍhām","instr. pl. + f. acc. pp.","hidden by its own qualities (guṇas)"],["yaḥ kāraṇāni nikhilāni tāni","rel. + n. acc. pl.","who (rules) all those causes —"],["kāla-ātma-yuktāni","acc. pl. (joined with time and the self)","(the causes) together with time and the individual self —"],["adhitiṣṭhati ekaḥ","pres. + nom.","He, the One, presides over (them)"]],"lit":"\"They, devoted to the yoga of meditation, beheld the <strong>self-power of God (devātma-śakti)</strong>, hidden by its own qualities (<em>guṇas</em>) — He, the <strong>One</strong>, who presides over all those causes, together with time and the individual self.\"","para":"The answer to v. 1–2 dawns in <strong>meditation (dhyāna-yoga)</strong>: the seers <em>saw</em> the <strong>devātma-śakti</strong> — the divine creative <strong>Power</strong> that is God's own Self, <strong>veiled by the three guṇas</strong> (the modes of <em>prakṛti</em>). All the proposed \"causes\" (time, nature, the elements, even the soul) are themselves <strong>governed by the One God</strong> who wields this power. This is the Upaniṣad's first great affirmation: behind every cause stands <strong>the one Lord and His śakti</strong> — and it is <em>known by meditation, not mere argument.</em> (<em>Devātma-śakti</em> and <em>guṇa</em> are landmark terms — the seed of the later Śakti and Sāṃkhya doctrines, here held within theism.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>devātma-śakti</em> = the Lord's <em>māyā</em> (His power, non-different from Him), \"hidden by the guṇas\"; the One rules all causes — <em>jñāna</em> of this is by <em>dhyāna-yoga</em>, not speculation."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the power is \"God's own self\" (<em>deva-ātma</em>), not a separate principle; meditation, not logic, reveals it."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the decisive theistic turn — a conscious Lord with His inseparable creative power, the synthesis of Sāṃkhya's <em>prakṛti</em> with a personal God."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the seers <em>saw</em> (not merely inferred) — meditation is the organ of this knowledge; God and His power are one."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>devātmaśakti</em> as a pivotal term (the divine self-power), and <em>dhyānayoga</em> as the cognitive method — marking the Upaniṣad's Yoga affiliation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 1.4–1.5","title":"The Wheel of Brahman (the cosmos as a great wheel)","sa":["1.4: तमेकनेमिं त्रिवृतं षोडशान्तं शतार्धारं विंशतिप्रत्यराभिः । अष्टकैः षड्भिर्विश्वरूपैकपाशं त्रिमार्गभेदं द्विनिमित्तैकमोहम् ॥ ४ ॥","1.5: पञ्चस्रोतोम्बुं पञ्चयोन्युग्रवक्रां पञ्चप्राणोर्मिं पञ्चबुद्ध्यादिमूलाम् । पञ्चावर्तां पञ्चदुःखौघवेगां पञ्चाशद्भेदां पञ्चपर्वामधीमः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["tam ekanemiṃ trivṛtaṃ ṣoḍaśāntaṃ śatārdhāraṃ viṃśatipratyarābhiḥ | aṣṭakaiḥ ṣaḍbhir viśvarūpaikapāśaṃ trimārgabhedaṃ dvinimittaikamoham || 4 ||","pañcasrotombuṃ pañcayonyugravakrāṃ pañcaprāṇormiṃ pañcabuddhyādimūlām | pañcāvartāṃ pañcaduḥkhaughavegāṃ pañcāśadbhedāṃ pañcaparvām adhīmaḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"(We meditate on) Him (as the great wheel) with <strong>one felly, threefold tyre, sixteen ends, fifty spokes</strong> with twenty counter-spokes, <strong>six sets of eight</strong>, whose one rope is of many forms, with three distinct paths, and whose one delusion arises from two causes.\"<br>\"(We contemplate it as) a <strong>river of five currents</strong>, from five sources, fierce and crooked, whose waves are the five vital breaths, whose origin is the fivefold (sense-)perception, with five whirlpools, the rushing flood of the five kinds of pain, divided fiftyfold, with five branches.\"","para":"Two intricate <strong>riddle-verses</strong> picturing the cosmos (and the embodied condition) as a vast <strong>wheel (brahma-cakra)</strong> and a turbulent <strong>river</strong> — both turned/driven by the one God. The numbers encode Sāṃkhya-Yoga categories. A traditional decoding:","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the wheel is <em>saṃsāra</em> / <em>prakṛti</em> with its evolutes; every number is a Sāṃkhya tattva-set; the Lord (v. 6) is the un-turning hub. He decodes the fifties, eights, fives as the categories of bondage."],["Śaṅkarānanda","gives the standard numeric correspondences (16 = ṣoḍaśa-kalā; 50 = the bhāvas; the five = senses/breaths/pains)."],["Tyāgīśānanda","a deliberately enigmatic <em>adhyāropa</em> (superimposition) presenting the whole of relative existence as a single machine, to be meditated on as the Lord's display."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the \"river of five currents, fierce and crooked\" is the rush of sense-life sweeping the soul along; meditation steps out of the current."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","classic <em>brahmodya</em> riddle-verses encoding Sāṃkhya enumerations; Müller's notes attempt the count; evidence of Sāṃkhya-Yoga categories already systematised."]],"notes":["<table><tr><td>symbol (1.4)</td><td>refers to</td></tr><tr><td><strong>one felly (eka-nemi)</strong></td><td>the one <em>prakṛti</em> / <em>māyā</em> (the rim that contains all)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>threefold (trivṛt)</strong></td><td>the three guṇas (sattva, rajas, tamas)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>sixteen ends (ṣoḍaśānta)</strong></td><td>the 16 modifications (5 elements + 5 organs of perception + 5 of action + mind)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>fifty spokes</strong></td><td>the 50 categories/states of Sāṃkhya (the <em>pratyaya-sarga</em>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>twenty counter-spokes</strong></td><td>the 10 senses + their 10 objects</td></tr><tr><td><strong>six eights (ṣaḍ aṣṭaka)</strong></td><td>six ogdoads (e.g. the 8-fold prakṛti, dhātus, powers, etc.)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>one rope of many forms</strong></td><td>desire (<em>kāma</em>), the binding cord in countless forms</td></tr><tr><td><strong>three paths (tri-mārga)</strong></td><td>dharma, adharma, and knowledge (or the three destinies)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>one delusion from two causes</strong></td><td>the single <em>moha</em> born of merit and demerit (puṇya/pāpa)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>five currents (1.5)</strong></td><td>the five senses (or vital airs) feeding the river of life</td></tr><tr><td><strong>five pains, five whirlpools, fifty divisions, five branches</strong></td><td>the five <em>kleśas</em>, the cycles, the manifold modifications</td></tr></table>","The point of the elaborate symbolism: <strong>the entire psycho-cosmic machinery — guṇas, elements, senses, breaths, pains — is one revolving wheel/river, and on it the soul is whirled</strong> (v. 6) until it knows the <strong>One</strong> who turns it."]},{"ref":"Verse 1.6","title":"The swan whirled on the wheel; release by knowing the Mover","sa":["सर्वाजीवे सर्वसंस्थे बृहन्ते अस्मिन् हंसो भ्राम्यते ब्रह्मचक्रे । पृथगात्मानं प्रेरितारं च मत्वा जुष्टस्ततस्तेनामृतत्वमेति ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["sarvājīve sarvasaṃsthe bṛhante asmin haṃso bhrāmyate brahmacakre | pṛthagātmānaṃ preritāraṃ ca matvā juṣṭas tatas tenāmṛtatvam eti || 6 ||"],"words":[["sarva-ājīve sarva-saṃsthe bṛhante asmin brahma-cakre","n. loc. (in which all live, in which all rest, vast)","in this vast wheel of Brahman, in which all live and all come to rest,"],["haṃsaḥ bhrāmyate","m. nom. (the swan = the soul) + pres. pass.","the swan (the individual soul) is whirled about,"],["pṛthak ātmānaṃ preritāraṃ ca matvā","adv. + acc. + acc. (the impeller) + ger.","(but) having thought the Self and the Impeller (God) as distinct (from itself / from the wheel),"],["juṣṭaḥ tataḥ tena amṛtatvam eti","pp. (favoured/blessed) + abl. + instr. + pres.","being then blessed (and accepted) by Him, it attains immortality"]],"lit":"\"In this vast <strong>wheel of Brahman</strong>, in which all live and all rest, the <strong>swan (the soul)</strong> is whirled about, thinking itself separate (and helpless). But when it knows the <strong>Self and the Impeller (God)</strong> as distinct (and yet one as its support), then, blessed by Him, it attains immortality.\"","para":"On the <strong>brahma-cakra</strong> of vv. 4–5 the <strong>haṃsa</strong> (the soul, the \"swan\" — also a pun on <em>aham-saḥ</em>, \"I am He\") is <strong>spun around</strong> in saṃsāra, so long as it feels itself a separate, helpless turner. <strong>Liberation comes by knowing the Mover</strong> — distinguishing the <strong>Self</strong> and its <strong>divine Impeller (preritṛ)</strong> from the whirling mechanism, and being <strong>graced (juṣṭa)</strong> by Him. Knowledge <strong>plus</strong> grace (<em>juṣṭa</em> = favoured, accepted) yields <strong>immortality</strong> — a first hint of the <em>bhakti</em> that crowns the Upaniṣad (6.23).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the soul revolves while ignorant; knowing the <em>preritṛ</em> (the Lord) as other than the body-mind machine, and identifying with the Self, it is freed. \"Blessed by Him\" = the fruit of realised knowledge."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>haṃsa</em> = the <em>jīva</em>; <em>pṛthak</em> (separate) = discriminating the Self/Lord from the wheel of prakṛti."],["Tyāgīśānanda","note <em>juṣṭa</em> (\"favoured/graced\") — the earliest seed of <strong>grace (prasāda)</strong> in the Upaniṣads; release is by knowledge <em>accepted by</em> the Lord."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the soul, dizzy on the wheel of birth, steadies by fixing on the unmoving Mover; devotion + discrimination liberate."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","<em>brahma-cakra</em> (wheel of Brahman) and <em>haṃsa</em> (soul) are key images; <em>juṣṭa</em> read by some as theistic grace, marking the text's devotional turn."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 1.7","title":"The supreme Brahman in which the triad rests","sa":["उद्गीतमेतत्परमं तु ब्रह्म तस्मिंस्त्रयं सुप्रतिष्ठाऽक्षरं च । अत्रान्तरं ब्रह्मविदो विदित्वा लीना ब्रह्मणि तत्परा योनिमुक्ताः ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["udgītam etat paramaṃ tu brahma tasmiṃs trayaṃ supratiṣṭhākṣaraṃ ca | atrāntaraṃ brahmavido viditvā līnā brahmaṇi tatparā yonimuktāḥ || 7 ||"],"words":[["udgītam etat paramaṃ tu brahma","pp. (sung, celebrated) + n. nom.","this supreme Brahman has been celebrated (in the Vedas)"],["tasmin trayaṃ supratiṣṭhā akṣaraṃ ca","loc. + n. nom. (the triad) + (well-founded, imperishable)","in it the triad (soul, world, Lord — or the perishable, imperishable, and Lord) rests well-founded and imperishable"],["atra antaraṃ brahma-vidaḥ viditvā","adv. + n. acc. (the inner distinction) + ger.","knowing the inner difference (between soul and Lord) here,"],["līnāḥ brahmaṇi tat-parāḥ yoni-muktāḥ","nom. pl. (merged, devoted to That, freed from the womb)","the knowers of Brahman, merged in Brahman, intent on It, are freed from (re)birth"]],"lit":"\"This supreme Brahman has been celebrated (in the scriptures); in it the <strong>triad</strong> (the soul, the world, and the Lord) rests, well-founded and imperishable. The knowers of Brahman, having known the inner distinction (within it), become merged in Brahman, intent on It, <strong>freed from the womb (rebirth).</strong>\"","para":"Above the wheel stands the <strong>supreme Brahman</strong> in which the <strong>triad</strong> — the enjoying soul (<em>bhoktṛ</em>), the enjoyed world (<em>bhogya</em>), and the impelling Lord (<em>preritṛ</em>) — is grounded as in its imperishable base. To <strong>know the inner relation</strong> of these (soul ≠ Lord, yet both grounded in Brahman) is to <strong>merge in Brahman</strong> and be <strong>liberated from birth</strong>. The chapter now states the metaphysics behind the imagery: one Brahman, within which the triad of v. 12 operates.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>traya</em> (triad) = enjoyer, enjoyed, ruler (cf. v. 12); all rest in the <em>akṣara</em> (imperishable) Brahman; knowing the distinction yet the unity, one is absorbed and freed."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>antara</em> = the discriminative knowledge of the three within the One."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the synthesis — the personal triad is real <em>within</em> the impersonal Brahman; realisation is both discrimination and merger."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","knowing how soul, world, and God interrelate dissolves the separateness; one is \"freed from the womb.\""],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>yonimukta</em> (\"freed from the womb/birth\") and the triad-in-Brahman as the chapter's doctrinal core."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 1.8","title":"The Lord bears the perishable and imperishable; the soul is bound until it knows God","sa":["संयुक्तमेतत् क्षरमक्षरं च व्यक्ताव्यक्तं भरते विश्वमीशः । अनीशश्चात्मा बध्यते भोक्तृभावाज् ज्ञात्वा देवं मुच्यते सर्वपाशैः ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["saṃyuktam etat kṣaram akṣaraṃ ca vyaktāvyaktaṃ bharate viśvam īśaḥ | anīśaś cātmā badhyate bhoktṛbhāvāj jñātvā devaṃ mucyate sarvapāśaiḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["saṃyuktam etat kṣaram akṣaraṃ ca vyakta-avyaktaṃ viśvam","n. acc. (this conjoined: perishable & imperishable, manifest & unmanifest)","this whole — perishable and imperishable, manifest and unmanifest, conjoined —"],["bharate īśaḥ","pres. (bears, supports) + m. nom.","the Lord bears (supports, rules)"],["anīśaḥ ca ātmā badhyate bhoktṛ-bhāvāt","nom. (not-the-lord) + pres. pass. + abl. (because of being an enjoyer)","but the self, being not-the-lord, is bound through its enjoyer-hood"],["jñātvā devaṃ mucyate sarva-pāśaiḥ","ger. + acc. + pres. pass. + instr. pl.","(and) knowing God, it is released from all bonds"]],"lit":"\"The <strong>Lord (Īśa)</strong> bears this whole — the perishable and the imperishable, the manifest and the unmanifest, conjoined together. But the <strong>self, being not the lord (anīśa)</strong>, is bound because of its <strong>enjoyer-hood (bhoktṛ-bhāva)</strong>; and <strong>knowing God, it is freed from all bonds.</strong>\"","para":"The pivotal theology. The <strong>one Lord (Īśa)</strong> supports the entire reality — <strong>kṣara</strong> (the perishable, = matter/changing world) and <strong>akṣara</strong> (the imperishable, = the soul-principle), the <strong>manifest and unmanifest</strong>. The individual <strong>self is \"not the Lord\" (anīśa)</strong> and is <strong>bound</strong> precisely by taking itself to be a separate <strong>enjoyer (bhoktṛ)</strong> of the world. The release is single and decisive: <strong>\"knowing God, one is freed from all bonds.\"</strong> This sets the program — bondage is mistaken enjoyer-hood; liberation is the knowledge of the Lord who is one's true Self.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>kṣara/akṣara</em> = prakṛti and the jīva (or the changing and the unchanging); the Lord supports both. Bondage = the soul's false sense of independent enjoyership; <em>jñātvā devaṃ mucyate</em> — the refrain of release by knowledge of God (recurring at 1.10, 2.15, 4.16, 5.13)."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>anīśa</em> (the dependent self) contrasted with <em>Īśa</em> (the Lord); enjoyer-hood is the bond."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the verse distinguishes God, soul, and matter, yet roots all in the Lord; the famous liberation-formula <em>jñātvā devaṃ mucyate sarvapāśaiḥ</em> anchors the chapter."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","we are bound by thinking ourselves separate enjoyers; knowing God as our Self snaps every fetter."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","<em>kṣara/akṣara</em> and <em>vyakta/avyakta</em> are Sāṃkhya pairs theologised; the recurring <em>jñātvā devaṃ mucyate</em> marks the text's soteriology."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 1.9","title":"Two unborn: the knowing and the unknowing; and the infinite Self","sa":["ज्ञाज्ञौ द्वावजावीशनीशावजा ह्येका भोक्तृभोग्यार्थयुक्ता । अनन्तश्चात्मा विश्वरूपो ह्यकर्ता त्रयं यदा विन्दते ब्रह्ममेतत् ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["jñājñau dvāv ajāv īśanīśāv ajā hy ekā bhoktṛbhogyārthayuktā | anantaś cātmā viśvarūpo hy akartā trayaṃ yadā vindate brahmametat || 9 ||"],"words":[["jña-ajñau dvau ajau īśa-anīśau","m. nom. du. (the knowing & the unknowing) + (two unborn) + (lord & not-lord)","the knowing (God) and the unknowing (the bound soul) — two unborn, the lord and the not-lord;"],["ajā hi ekā bhoktṛ-bhogya-artha-yuktā","f. nom. (one unborn she = prakṛti) + adj.","and there is one unborn \"she\" (prakṛti), joined to the purposes of enjoyer and enjoyed"],["anantaḥ ca ātmā viśva-rūpaḥ hi akartā","m. nom. (infinite, all-formed, non-doer)","and the Self is infinite, of universal form, a non-doer"],["trayaṃ yadā vindate brahma etat","n. acc. (the triad) + pres. + n. nom.","when one finds (knows) this triad, that is Brahman"]],"lit":"\"Two are unborn — the <strong>knowing (God)</strong> and the <strong>unknowing (soul)</strong>, the lord and the not-lord; and there is one unborn <strong>'she' (prakṛti)</strong>, joined to the ends of enjoyer and enjoyed. And the <strong>Self is infinite, of universal form, a non-doer.</strong> When one knows this <strong>triad</strong>, that is Brahman.\"","para":"A precise statement of the <strong>three eternal (unborn, <em>aja</em>) principles</strong>: (1) the <strong>knowing, lordly God (jña, īśa)</strong>; (2) the <strong>unknowing, dependent soul (ajña, anīśa)</strong>; and (3) the one <strong>unborn \"she,\" prakṛti (ajā — a pun: \"unborn fem.\" and \"she-goat,\" picked up in 4.5)</strong>, serving as the field of enjoyer and enjoyed. Yet the <strong>true Self is infinite, all-formed, and actionless (akartā)</strong> — beyond the doership that binds. <strong>To know this triad rightly is itself Brahman-realisation.</strong> (This is the Upaniṣad's theistic Sāṃkhya: God, soul, and nature, all \"unborn,\" resolved in the one infinite Self.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the two <em>aja</em> (unborn conscious beings) = Īśvara and jīva; the one <em>ajā</em> = <em>avyākṛta</em> prakṛti/māyā; the Self is ultimately the <em>infinite, actionless</em> one — knowing the triad's truth is liberation. (Śaṅkara reads the plurality as provisional, resolved in non-duality.)"],["Śaṅkarānanda","glosses <em>ajā</em> as prakṛti (the unborn cause); the Self (real) is <em>akartā</em>, the others its appearances."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the theistic-Sāṃkhya scheme — Lord, soul, nature — held within Vedānta; \"the Self is infinite, all-formed, non-doer\" subordinates the scheme to the one Ātman."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","God, soul, and nature are the eternal triad of religious experience; knowing their unity in the infinite Self is freedom."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the three <em>aja</em>/<em>ajā</em> (the famous pun anticipating 4.5) — God, soul, and unborn matter — a key text for the text's Sāṃkhya theism."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 1.10","title":"Hara, the one God, ends all māyā","sa":["क्षरं प्रधानममृताक्षरं हरः क्षरात्मानावीशते देव एकः । तस्याभिध्यानाद्योजनात्तत्त्वभावात् भूयश्चान्ते विश्वमायानिवृत्तिः ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["kṣaraṃ pradhānam amṛtākṣaraṃ haraḥ kṣarātmānāv īśate deva ekaḥ | tasyābhidhyānād yojanāt tattvabhāvāt bhūyaś cānte viśvamāyānivṛttiḥ || 10 ||"],"words":[["kṣaraṃ pradhānam","n. nom.","the perishable is pradhāna (primal matter);"],["amṛtam akṣaraṃ haraḥ","n. nom. + m. nom. (Hara)","the immortal, imperishable (ruler) is Hara (God);"],["kṣara-ātmānau īśate devaḥ ekaḥ","acc. du. (the perishable and the soul) + pres. + nom.","the one God rules over both the perishable (matter) and the (embodied) self"],["tasya abhidhyānāt yojanāt tattva-bhāvāt","abl. (by meditating on Him, by union, by becoming the Reality)","by meditation on Him, by union with Him, by (realising) the Reality,"],["bhūyaḥ ca ante viśva-māyā-nivṛttiḥ","adv. + loc. + f. nom.","finally there is, once more, the cessation of all (the world-) illusion"]],"lit":"\"The perishable is <strong>pradhāna</strong> (primal matter); the immortal, imperishable (ruler) is <strong>Hara</strong> (God). The <strong>one God</strong> rules over both the perishable (matter) and the (embodied) self. By <strong>meditating on Him, by union with Him, by becoming one with the Reality</strong>, there is, at the last, the <strong>cessation of all the world-illusion (viśva-māyā).</strong>\"","para":"Naming the God explicitly as <strong>Hara</strong> (a name of <strong>Śiva/Rudra</strong>), the verse maps the dualities onto theism: <strong>kṣara = pradhāna</strong> (perishable matter, Sāṃkhya's <em>pradhāna</em>); <strong>akṣara = Hara</strong> (the immortal Lord). The <strong>one God</strong> (<em>deva ekaḥ</em>) governs both matter and the soul. The path to freedom is threefold: <strong>abhidhyāna</strong> (intense meditation on Him), <strong>yojana</strong> (yogic union), and <strong>tattva-bhāva</strong> (becoming/realising the Reality) — and its fruit is the <strong>complete cessation of māyā</strong>, the world-illusion. (Note the explicit <em>māyā</em> and the explicit Śaiva name <em>Hara</em> — hallmarks of this Upaniṣad.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>pradhāna</em> = avyakta prakṛti (the perishable); <em>Hara</em> = the Lord who \"carries off\" (<em>hṛ</em>) ignorance; meditation/union/identity end <em>māyā</em> — the standard Advaitic reading of release as the dissolution of nescience."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>Hara</em> etymologised as the remover of bondage; the triple means (dhyāna, yoga, tattva-realisation)."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the verse's three stages — devotional meditation, yogic union, and identity — culminating in the end of māyā; a synthesis of bhakti, yoga, and jñāna."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","sustained meditation on the Lord dissolves the cosmic illusion; the name Hara invites Śaiva devotion."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","a key Śaiva-theistic verse (the name <em>Hara</em>; <em>māyā</em> as the cosmic illusion ended by knowing the Lord); evidence of emergent Śaivism within Vedānta."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 1.11–1.12","title":"The fruit of knowing God; the threefold Brahman to be known","sa":["1.11: ज्ञात्वा देवं सर्वपाशापहानिः क्षीणैः क्लेशैर्जन्ममृत्युप्रहाणिः । तस्याभिध्यानात्तृतीयं देहभेदे विश्वैश्वर्यं केवल आप्तकामः ॥ ११ ॥","1.12: एतज्ज्ञेयं नित्यमेवात्मसंस्थं नातः परं वेदितव्यं हि किञ्चित् । भोक्ता भोग्यं प्रेरितारं च मत्वा सर्वं प्रोक्तं त्रिविधं ब्रह्ममेतत् ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["jñātvā devaṃ sarvapāśāpahāniḥ kṣīṇaiḥ kleśair janmamṛtyuprahāṇiḥ | tasyābhidhyānāt tṛtīyaṃ dehabhede viśvaiśvaryaṃ kevala āptakāmaḥ || 11 ||","etaj jñeyaṃ nityam evātmasaṃsthaṃ nātaḥ paraṃ veditavyaṃ hi kiñcit | bhoktā bhogyaṃ preritāraṃ ca matvā sarvaṃ proktaṃ trividhaṃ brahmametat || 12 ||"],"words":[["jñātvā devaṃ sarva-pāśa-apahāniḥ","ger. + f. nom.","by knowing God, (there is) the removal of all bonds;"],["kṣīṇaiḥ kleśaiḥ janma-mṛtyu-prahāṇiḥ","instr. pl. (afflictions worn away) + f. nom.","the afflictions worn away, (there is) the ending of birth and death;"],["tasya abhidhyānāt tṛtīyaṃ deha-bhede","abl. + n. acc. (a third state) + loc. (at the body's dissolution)","by meditation on Him, a third (state) — at the falling-away of the body —"],["viśva-aiśvaryaṃ kevalaḥ āpta-kāmaḥ","n. acc. (universal lordship) + nom. (alone, with all desires fulfilled)","universal sovereignty; (he becomes) the Absolute, his every desire fulfilled"]],"lit":"\"By <strong>knowing God</strong>, all bonds fall away; the afflictions (<em>kleśas</em>) worn out, birth and death cease. By meditation on Him, at the body's dissolution there is a <strong>third state — universal sovereignty</strong>; (the knower) becomes <strong>the Alone (kevala), with every desire fulfilled (āptakāma).</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>This is to be known — ever seated in the Self;</strong> there is nothing higher than this to be known. Knowing the <strong>enjoyer, the enjoyed, and the Impeller (God)</strong>, all has been declared — this <strong>threefold Brahman.</strong>\"","para":"v. 11 gives the <strong>fruits of God-knowledge</strong> in three grades: removal of bonds; the ending of <em>kleśas</em> and of birth-and-death; and — by deep meditation — at death a <strong>\"third state\"</strong> of <strong>viśva-aiśvarya (universal lordship)</strong>, becoming <strong>kevala</strong> (the absolute Alone) and <strong>āptakāma</strong> (one whose desires are all fulfilled). v. 12 sums up the chapter's metaphysics: the <strong>one reality to be known, seated in the Self</strong>, is the <strong>threefold Brahman</strong> — <strong>enjoyer (soul), enjoyed (world), Impeller (God)</strong> — nothing further need be known.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the \"third\" (<em>tṛtīya</em>) = the supreme result beyond the first two (loosing of bonds, end of kleśas) — <em>kaivalya</em>; <em>āptakāma</em> = the Self lacking nothing. The threefold Brahman (12) is the enjoyer-enjoyed-ruler resolved in the one."],["Śaṅkarānanda","enumerates the three fruits; \"ātma-saṃstha\" — the knowable ever rests in one's own Self, hence near, not far."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the graded soteriology (release → end of rebirth → lordship/kaivalya); v. 12 is the chapter's thesis-statement (the triad = Brahman)."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the supreme knowledge is <em>within</em> (\"seated in the Self\"); knowing the triad, the seeker has known all."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>kevala</em> and <em>āptakāma</em> (Sāṃkhya/Yoga <em>kaivalya</em>); the \"threefold Brahman\" as the doctrinal summary."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 1.13–1.14","title":"Fire latent in wood; OM the upper drill-stick","sa":["1.13: वह्नेर्यथा योनिगतस्य मूर्तिर्न दृश्यते नैव च लिङ्गनाशः । स भूय एवेन्धनयोनिगृह्यस्तद्वोभयं वै प्रणवेन देहे ॥ १३ ॥","1.14: स्वदेहमरणिं कृत्वा प्रणवं चोत्तरारणिम् । ध्याननिर्मथनाभ्यासादेवं पश्येन्निगूढवत् ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["vahner yathā yonigatasya mūrtir na dṛśyate naiva ca liṅganāśaḥ | sa bhūya evendhanayonigṛhyas tadvobhayaṃ vai praṇavena dehe || 13 ||","svadeham araṇiṃ kṛtvā praṇavaṃ cottarāraṇim | dhyānanirmathanābhyāsād evaṃ paśyen nigūḍhavat || 14 ||"],"words":[["vahneḥ yathā yoni-gatasya mūrtiḥ na dṛśyate","gen. (of fire, latent in its source/wood) + f. nom. + pres. pass.","as the form of fire latent in its womb (the firewood) is not seen,"],["na eva ca liṅga-nāśaḥ","+ m. nom.","yet its subtle existence is not destroyed (it is still there) —"],["saḥ bhūyaḥ eva indhana-yoni-gṛhyaḥ","nom. + pp. (to be seized again from the fuel-source)","it can be seized again from the firewood (by drilling);"],["tadvā ubhayaṃ vai praṇavena dehe","(so too) + n. acc. (both) + instr. (by OM) + loc. (in the body)","even so, both (the latent and the manifest Self) are (to be grasped) by OM, in the body"]],"lit":"\"As the form of <strong>fire latent in firewood</strong> is not seen, yet its subtle being is not destroyed, and it can be <strong>drawn out again from the wood</strong> (by friction) — even so, both (the unmanifest and manifest Self) are (to be grasped) <strong>in the body by means of OM (praṇava).</strong>\"<br>\"Making <strong>one's own body the lower fire-drill</strong> and <strong>OM the upper drill-stick</strong>, by the <strong>practice of churning that is meditation (dhyāna-nirmathana)</strong>, one should see the (hidden) God, <strong>as (one sees) the (fire) hidden (in the wood).</strong>\"","para":"A beautiful, practical image. The Self/God is like <strong>fire latent in wood</strong> — unseen, but not absent, and <strong>recoverable by friction</strong>. So in meditation: make <strong>your own body the lower araṇi</strong> (drill-block) and <strong>OM the upper araṇi</strong> (the spinning stick); by the <strong>friction of sustained meditation (dhyāna)</strong> you \"kindle\" and <strong>behold the hidden God within.</strong> OM (<em>praṇava</em>) is the means; the body the field; meditation the churning. (One of the Upaniṣad's most concrete meditation-instructions.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","fire-in-wood = the Self ever-present but unmanifest in the body; OM is the <em>pratīka</em>/means; <em>dhyāna-nirmathana</em> (churning by meditation) manifests it — knowledge, not production (the fire was always there)."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the two araṇis (body + OM) and the friction (meditation) literalise the kindling of Self-vision."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the classic Vedic fire-drill (<em>araṇi</em>) repurposed as a meditation manual; OM the upper stick is the technique of Adhyāya 2."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","a direct practice — repeat and meditate on OM with the body as base until the inner Light \"kindles\"; the Self is uncovered, not created."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the <em>araṇi</em> (fire-churning) simile and OM as the meditative instrument; a vivid bridge to the Yoga chapter."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 1.15–1.16","title":"As oil in sesame, ghee in milk: the Self everywhere, found by truth and austerity","sa":["1.15: तिलेषु तैलं दधिनीव सर्पिरापः स्रोतःस्वरणीषु चाग्निः । एवमात्माऽत्मनि गृह्यतेऽसौ सत्येनैनं तपसायोऽनुपश्यति ॥ १५ ॥","1.16: सर्वव्यापिनमात्मानं क्षीरे सर्पिरिवार्पितम् । आत्मविद्यातपोमूलं तद्ब्रह्मोपनिषत् परम् ॥ १६ ॥"],"iast":["tileṣu tailaṃ dadhinīva sarpir āpaḥ srotaḥsv araṇīṣu cāgniḥ | evam ātmātmani gṛhyate'sau satyenainaṃ tapasāyo'nupaśyati || 15 ||","sarvavyāpinam ātmānaṃ kṣīre sarpir ivārpitam | ātmavidyātapomūlaṃ tad brahmopaniṣat param || 16 ||"],"words":[["tileṣu tailaṃ, dadhani iva sarpiḥ","loc. + n. nom. (oil in sesame; as ghee in curd)","as oil (is) in sesame, as butter in curd,"],["āpaḥ srotaḥsu, araṇīṣu ca agniḥ","f. nom. + loc. (water in streams; fire in the drill-woods)","as water in (underground) streams, and fire in the fire-sticks,"],["evam ātmā ātmani gṛhyate asau","adv. + loc. + pres. pass.","even so this Self is grasped within the self (by introspection)"],["satyena enaṃ tapasā yaḥ anupaśyati","instr. (by truth, by austerity) + rel. + pres.","by him who beholds it through truthfulness and austerity"]],"lit":"\"As <strong>oil in sesame seeds</strong>, as <strong>butter in curd</strong>, as <strong>water in (hidden) streams</strong>, and as <strong>fire in the fire-sticks</strong> — even so this <strong>Self is grasped within one's own self</strong> by him who beholds it through <strong>truth (satya) and austerity (tapas).</strong>\"<br>\"(He realises) the <strong>all-pervading Self, set (within) like butter in milk</strong> — that (Self), rooted in <strong>Self-knowledge and austerity (ātma-vidyā-tapas)</strong> — <strong>that is the supreme Brahman, the (teaching of the) Upaniṣad.</strong>\"","para":"Four homely similes seal the chapter: the Self is <strong>immanent yet hidden</strong>, like <strong>oil in sesame, butter in curd/milk, water in springs, fire in kindling</strong> — <em>present throughout, but drawn out only by effort.</em> The \"effort\" is named: <strong>satya (truthfulness) and tapas (austerity)</strong>, grounded in <strong>ātma-vidyā (Self-knowledge)</strong>. The all-pervading Self, \"churned out\" by this discipline, <strong>is the supreme Brahman — and this is the very secret (upaniṣad).</strong> (The closing line makes explicit that the chapter's teaching <em>is</em> the Upaniṣad's essence.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the similes show <em>vyāpti</em> (pervasion) with concealment — the Self pervades the body as oil the sesame; <em>satya</em> and <em>tapas</em> (with <em>ātma-vidyā</em>) are the means of its manifestation; the realised Self <em>is</em> the supreme Brahman."],["Śaṅkarānanda","\"butter in milk\" — present everywhere yet unseen till churned; truth and austerity are the churning."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the chapter ends by naming its own fruit \"the supreme Brahman, the Upaniṣad\" — and the ethical-ascetic prerequisites (truth, austerity)."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the Self is the \"butter\" hidden in the \"milk\" of the body-mind; truth and tapas are the daily churning; meditation makes it visible."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the chain of immanence-similes (oil/sesame, butter/milk, fire/wood) — stock Upaniṣadic images for the indwelling Self — and <em>ātmavidyā-tapas</em> as the means."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Adhyāya 1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The first chapter lays the <strong>whole program</strong> of the Upaniṣad in miniature:","1. <strong>The question (1.1–2).</strong> What is the cause? The rival answers — Time, Nature, Necessity, Chance, the elements, matter, the soul — all fail: insentient principles cannot explain conscious experience, and the ordinary soul is not self-determining.","2. <strong>The answer: the one God and His power (1.3, 1.8, 1.10).</strong> Seen in <strong>meditation</strong>, the <strong>devātma-śakti</strong> (God's own self-power, veiled by the guṇas) and the <strong>one Lord (Īśa/Hara/deva ekaḥ)</strong> rule all causes, bearing the perishable (matter, <em>pradhāna/kṣara</em>) and the imperishable (soul, <em>akṣara</em>). <strong>\"Knowing God, one is freed from all bonds.\"</strong>","3. <strong>The cosmos as wheel/river (1.4–6).</strong> Saṃsāra is a vast <strong>brahma-cakra</strong> (decoded by Sāṃkhya enumerations) on which the <strong>soul (haṃsa)</strong> is whirled — until, distinguishing the <strong>Impeller</strong>, and <strong>graced (juṣṭa)</strong> by Him, it attains immortality.","4. <strong>The threefold Brahman (1.7, 1.9, 1.12).</strong> Within the supreme Brahman rests the eternal <strong>triad: enjoyer (soul) — enjoyed (world) — Impeller (God)</strong>; the Self itself is <strong>infinite, all-formed, actionless</strong>. To know this triad rightly <em>is</em> Brahman.","5. <strong>The method (1.13–16).</strong> The hidden God is <strong>fire latent in wood</strong>, <strong>oil in sesame</strong>, <strong>butter in milk</strong> — recovered by the <strong>friction of meditation</strong> with <strong>OM</strong> (the body the lower drill, OM the upper), grounded in <strong>truth and austerity</strong>. <em>This</em> is the supreme Brahman, the Upaniṣad's secret.","Two notes: (a) the chapter already names <strong>māyā, Hara, guṇa, pradhāna, devātma-śakti</strong> — the distinctive theistic-Sāṃkhya vocabulary that makes Śvetāśvatara the bridge between Vedānta, Sāṃkhya-Yoga, and emergent Śaivism; (b) it weds <strong>jñāna</strong> (knowing the triad), <strong>yoga</strong> (meditation/churning), and the first hint of <strong>grace/bhakti</strong> (<em>juṣṭa</em>), the three strands the later chapters develop."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Adhyāya 1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE QUESTION (1.1-2):  cause? birth? life? rest? who ordains joy &amp; pain?\n   rejected answers:  Time · Nature · Necessity · Chance · elements · matter · the soul\n                              |\n                              v   (seen in DHYĀNA-YOGA, 1.3)\n   ====&gt;  THE ONE GOD (deva ekaḥ / Īśa / HARA) + His DEVĀTMA-ŚAKTI (veiled by the 3 guṇas)\n          rules all causes &quot;together with time and the self&quot;\n\n   THE BRAHMA-WHEEL (1.4-6) — saṃsāra as a great wheel / fierce river:\n     1 felly (prakṛti) · 3 tyre (guṇas) · 16 ends · 50 spokes · 20 counter-spokes · 6 eights\n     5 currents/breaths/pains/whirlpools ...  ON IT the HAṂSA (soul) is WHIRLED\n       -&gt; knows the IMPELLER as distinct + is GRACED (juṣṭa) -&gt; AMṚTATVA (immortality)\n\n   THE TRIAD in Brahman (1.7, 1.9, 1.12):\n        ENJOYER (soul, anīśa) — ENJOYED (world, kṣara) — IMPELLER (God, Īśa)\n        the SELF = infinite · all-formed · actionless ;  &quot;knowing God -&gt; freed of all bonds&quot;\n\n   THE METHOD (1.13-16):  the Self is hidden like\n        FIRE in wood · OIL in sesame · BUTTER in milk · WATER in springs\n        -&gt; churn it out:  body = lower araṇi | OM (praṇava) = upper araṇi | friction = MEDITATION\n        grounded in SATYA (truth) + TAPAS (austerity) = the supreme Brahman (the Upaniṣad)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Adhyāya 1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Hold the great question.</strong> Begin not with answers but with the seekers' question (1.1): <em>what is the source, support, and ordainer of my life and my joys and sorrows?</em> Let it deepen earnestness.","2. <strong>Don't settle for a partial cause</strong> (1.2). Notice the temptation to credit \"time,\" \"nature,\" \"luck,\" or even the bare ego for your lot. The Upaniṣad points past all of these to the conscious Lord who is your own Self.","3. <strong>Seek by meditation, not only argument</strong> (1.3). The seers <em>saw</em> the divine power \"given to the yoga of meditation.\" Make <em>dhyāna</em> — not just reasoning — your means of knowing.","4. <strong>Step off the wheel</strong> (1.6). When whirled by desires and events, recall the <strong>haṃsa on the brahma-cakra</strong>: distinguish the unmoving Impeller from the turning machine, and rest in Him.","5. <strong>Churn out the hidden fire</strong> (1.13–14). Take up the concrete practice: sit with the body as the steady base, <strong>OM</strong> as the turning stick, and let <strong>sustained meditation</strong> generate the \"friction\" that kindles inner vision. The Self is already present, like fire in wood — uncover it.","6. <strong>Ground it in truth and austerity</strong> (1.15–16). The Self is \"grasped by him who beholds it through <em>satya</em> and <em>tapas</em>.\" Truthfulness and self-discipline are not optional ornaments but the very means by which the butter is churned from the milk.","7. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the whole Upaniṣad is summed here — <em>the one God is the cause, He is your own Self, and \"knowing God, one is freed from all bonds\"</em> (1.8). Everything that follows (Yoga, Rudra, māyā, devotion) unfolds this."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 2 — Yoga: the Yoking of Mind to God","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya 2","title":"Yoga: the Yoking of Mind to God","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The second chapter is the Upaniṣad's</em> <strong>manual of meditation (Yoga)</strong> — <em>one of the earliest and most concrete in the Veda. It opens with five</em> <strong>\"yoking\" (yuñjāna) verses</strong> <em>invoking</em> <strong>Savitṛ</strong> <em>(the Impeller-Sun) to harness the mind toward the light; then gives the practical discipline —</em> <strong>place, posture, breath-control, the inner visions</strong> — <em>and the</em> <strong>signs of progress in yoga</strong>; <em>and culminates in the vision of the</em> <strong>Self like a polished mirror</strong>, <em>and the</em> <strong>one God present in fire, water, herbs, and all beings</strong>, <em>to whom homage is paid.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Verses 2.1–2.5","title":"The Savitṛ verses: \"yoking\" mind and powers toward the Light","sa":["2.1: युञ्जानः प्रथमं मनस्तत्त्वाय सविता धियः । अग्नेर्ज्योतिर्निचाय्य पृथिव्या अध्याभरत् ॥ १ ॥","2.2: युक्तेन मनसा वयं देवस्य सवितुः सवे । सुवर्गेयाय शक्त्या ॥ २ ॥","2.3: युक्त्वाय मनसा देवान् सुवर्यतो धिया दिवम् । बृहज्ज्योतिः करिष्यतः सविता प्रसुवाति तान् ॥ ३ ॥","2.4: युञ्जते मन उत युञ्जते धियो विप्रा विप्रस्य बृहतो विपश्चितः । वि होत्रा दधे वयुनाविदेक इन्मही देवस्य सवितुः परिष्टुतिः ॥ ४ ॥","2.5: युजे वां ब्रह्म पूर्व्यं नमोभिर्विश्लोक एतु पथ्येव सूरेः । शृण्वन्तु विश्वे अमृतस्य पुत्रा आ ये धामानि दिव्यानि तस्थुः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["yuñjānaḥ prathamaṃ manas tattvāya savitā dhiyaḥ | agner jyotir nicāyya pṛthivyā adhyābharat || 1 ||","yuktena manasā vayaṃ devasya savituḥ save | suvargeyāya śaktyā || 2 ||","yuktvāya manasā devān suvaryato dhiyā divam | bṛhajjyotiḥ kariṣyataḥ savitā prasuvāti tān || 3 ||","yuñjate mana uta yuñjate dhiyo viprā viprasya bṛhato vipaścitaḥ | vi hotrā dadhe vayunāvid eka in mahī devasya savituḥ pariṣṭutiḥ || 4 ||","yuje vāṃ brahma pūrvyaṃ namobhir viśloka etu pathyeva sūreḥ | śṛṇvantu viśve amṛtasya putrā ā ye dhāmāni divyāni tasthuḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"<strong>(2.1)</strong> Savitṛ, first yoking the mind and the thoughts toward the Truth, and discerning the light of Agni, brought it up out of the earth. <strong>(2.2)</strong> With mind yoked, we (are), by the power, in the impulse of the divine Savitṛ, for the attaining of heaven. <strong>(2.3)</strong> Yoking the powers (devas) with the mind, (those) going to heaven by intelligence, about to make a great light — Savitṛ impels them. <strong>(2.4)</strong> The seers yoke their mind, and yoke their thoughts — (those) wise, of the great seer (Savitṛ); the One who knows the rites has ordained the offerings; great is the praise of the divine Savitṛ. <strong>(2.5)</strong> I yoke (you two / the powers) with adorations to the ancient Brahman; may my verses go forth on the path, like (the rays) of the sun. <strong>Hear, all you children of the immortal (amṛtasya putrāḥ), and you who dwell in the divine realms!</strong>\"","para":"Five <strong>Vedic mantras</strong> (drawn from the Ṛgveda/Vājasaneyi-Saṃhitā Savitṛ-hymns) form the <strong>invocation of Yoga</strong>. Their refrain is the verb <strong>yuj</strong> (\"to yoke/join\") — the root of <em>yoga</em>: the seers <strong>harness the mind and the intellect (manas, dhī)</strong> toward the <strong>Truth</strong> and the inner <strong>Light</strong>, under the impulse of <strong>Savitṛ</strong>, the divine Impeller (the Sun as the power that \"sets in motion\"). Two phrases are famous: the call to <strong>\"yoke the mind to the ancient Brahman\"</strong> (2.5a), and the ringing address — <strong>\"Hear, O children of immortality (śṛṇvantu viśve amṛtasya putrāḥ)!\"</strong> (2.5c) — proclaiming that all beings are heirs of the deathless. These set the devotional-meditative key for the practical instructions that follow.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Savitṛ verses are <em>yoga-aṅga-stuti</em> — invocations consecrating the practice; \"yoking the mind\" = <em>samādhāna</em>, gathering the mind to Brahman; Savitṛ = the Impeller, ultimately the Lord."],["Śaṅkarānanda","glosses the Vedic phrases; <em>amṛtasya putrāḥ</em> = all souls, fit for immortality."],["Tyāgīśānanda","notes these are borrowed Ṛgvedic mantras (RV 5.81 etc.) prefacing the yoga; <em>amṛtasya putrāḥ</em> (\"children of immortality\") is the Upaniṣad's gospel — humanity's divine birthright (made famous by Vivekananda)."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the invocation to \"yoke\" the mind is the very definition of yoga; address to the \"children of immortality\" is the call to spiritual awakening."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","identify the Ṛgvedic Savitṛ-mantras adapted as a Yoga-prelude; <em>amṛtasya putrāḥ</em> highlighted as a luminous phrase."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 2.6–2.7","title":"Where mind is born; resort to the ancient Brahman","sa":["2.6: अग्निर्यत्राभिमथ्यते वायुर्यत्राधिरुध्यते । सोमो यत्रातिरिच्यते तत्र सञ्जायते मनः ॥ ६ ॥","2.7: सवित्रा प्रसवेन जुषेत ब्रह्म पूर्व्यम् । यत्र योनिं कृणवसे न हि ते पूर्तमक्षिपत् ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["agnir yatrābhimathyate vāyur yatrādhirudhyate | somo yatrātiricyate tatra sañjāyate manaḥ || 6 ||","savitrā prasavena juṣeta brahma pūrvyam | yatra yoniṃ kṛṇavase na hi te pūrtam akṣipat || 7 ||"],"words":[["agniḥ yatra abhimathyate","loc. + pres. pass.","where the fire is kindled (by friction),"],["vāyuḥ yatra adhirudhyate","pres. pass.","where the breath/air is restrained,"],["somaḥ yatra atiricyate","pres. pass.","where the soma (the mind's nectar/sap) overflows,"],["tatra sañjāyate manaḥ","pres. + n. nom.","there the (illumined) mind is born"]],"lit":"\"Where the <strong>fire is kindled (by churning)</strong>, where the <strong>breath is controlled (adhirudhyate)</strong>, where the <strong>soma overflows</strong> — <strong>there the (enlightened) mind is born.</strong>\"<br>\"By the impulse of Savitṛ, let one <strong>resort to the ancient Brahman</strong>; (if) you make your seat there, <strong>no past merit can fling you down</strong> (your former good deeds will not be lost / will not bind).\"","para":"v. 6 names the inner conditions in which <strong>higher consciousness (\"the mind\") is born</strong>: the kindling of the inner <strong>fire (tapas/concentration)</strong>, the <strong>restraint of breath (prāṇāyāma)</strong>, and the <strong>overflowing of soma</strong> (the blissful sap of the steadied mind) — meditation's three converging streams. v. 7 urges, by Savitṛ's impelling grace, to <strong>make Brahman one's resort</strong>, with the assurance that grounding oneself in Brahman secures rather than scatters one's spiritual gains.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the three (fire kindled, breath controlled, soma overflowing) are the bodily-yogic conditions in which the mind becomes fit for Self-vision; \"resort to the ancient Brahman\" = take refuge in the eternal."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>adhirudhyate</em> = prāṇāyāma; <em>soma atiricyate</em> = the mind's contentment swelling; then illumined <em>manas</em> arises."],["Tyāgīśānanda","a compact statement of yoga's physiology (concentration, breath, inner joy) preceding the detailed method."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","practical — kindle inner heat, regulate breath, let the mind grow blissful; then meditation matures."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the Vedic ritual vocabulary (fire-churning, soma) interiorised as yoga."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 2.8–2.10","title":"Posture, breath, and place of meditation","sa":["2.8: त्रिरुन्नतं स्थाप्य समं शरीरं हृदीन्द्रियाणि मनसा संनिवेश्य । ब्रह्मोडुपेन प्रतरेत विद्वान् स्रोतांसि सर्वाणि भयानकानि ॥ ८ ॥","2.9: प्राणान् प्रपीड्येह संयुक्तचेष्टः क्षीणे प्राणे नासिकयोच्छ्वसीत । दुष्टाश्वयुक्तमिव वाहमेनं विद्वान् मनो धारयेताप्रमत्तः ॥ ९ ॥","2.10: समे शुचौ शर्करावह्निवालिकाविवर्जिते शब्दजलाश्रयादिभिः । मनोनुकूले न तु चक्षुपीडने गुहानिवाताश्रयणे प्रयोजयेत् ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["trir unnataṃ sthāpya samaṃ śarīraṃ hṛdīndriyāṇi manasā saṃniveśya | brahmoḍupena pratareta vidvān srotāṃsi sarvāṇi bhayānakāni || 8 ||","prāṇān prapīḍyeha saṃyuktaceṣṭaḥ kṣīṇe prāṇe nāsikayocchvasīta | duṣṭāśvayuktam iva vāham enaṃ vidvān mano dhārayetāpramattaḥ || 9 ||","same śucau śarkarāvahnivālikāvivarjite śabdajalāśrayādibhiḥ | manonukūle na tu cakṣupīḍane guhānivātāśrayaṇe prayojayet || 10 ||"],"words":[["triḥ unnataṃ samaṃ śarīraṃ sthāpya","adv. (the three [chest, neck, head] erect) + acc. + ger.","holding the body erect and even, with the three upper parts (chest, neck, head) held straight,"],["hṛdi indriyāṇi manasā saṃniveśya","loc. + acc. pl. + instr. + ger.","drawing the senses into the heart by the mind,"],["brahma-uḍupena pratareta vidvān","instr. (with the boat of Brahman) + opt. + nom.","the wise one should cross, by the boat of Brahman,"],["srotāṃsi sarvāṇi bhayānakāni","acc. pl. + adj.","all the fearful currents (of saṃsāra)"]],"lit":"\"Holding the <strong>body erect and even, the three (upper parts) straight</strong>, drawing the <strong>senses into the heart by the mind</strong>, let the wise one <strong>cross all the fearful currents (of saṃsāra) by the boat of Brahman (OM).</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>Restraining the breaths (prāṇāyāma)</strong> here, his movements controlled, let him breathe out through the nostrils when the breath is (nearly) spent. Like a chariot yoked to vicious horses, let the wise one <strong>hold the mind firmly, vigilant and undistracted.</strong>\"<br>\"Let him <strong>practise (yoga) in a place</strong> that is <strong>level and clean</strong>, free of pebbles, fire, and gravel, free of noise, water, and (such) resorts — <strong>agreeable to the mind, not painful to the eye</strong> — a sheltered, wind-free retreat (a cave or the like).\"","para":"Three classic yoga-instructions, among the earliest in Sanskrit and echoed in the Bhagavad-Gītā and later Haṭha/Rāja Yoga: <strong>(2.8) Posture</strong> — body <strong>erect and steady</strong>, chest-neck-head aligned, senses <strong>withdrawn into the heart</strong>; with <strong>OM (\"the boat of Brahman\")</strong> one crosses the \"fearful currents\" of worldly life. <strong>(2.9) Breath</strong> — practise <strong>prāṇāyāma</strong> (restraint of breath), gently regulating in- and out-breath; and <strong>rein in the restless mind</strong> as a charioteer controls <strong>vicious horses</strong> — <em>vigilantly, undistracted (apramatta).</em> <strong>(2.10) Place</strong> — a <strong>clean, level, quiet, pleasant, sheltered spot</strong>, free of distractions and harsh stimuli. Together: <em>right place, right posture, right breath, controlled mind</em> — the outer frame of meditation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>trir unnata</em> = the three (chest, neck, head) held erect; <em>brahma-uḍupa</em> = OM, the raft across saṃsāra; the vicious-horses simile = the unruly senses/mind to be reined; the place-prescription ensures undisturbed <em>dhāraṇā</em>."],["Śaṅkarānanda","details the prāṇāyāma (controlled exhalation when breath is spent) and the requisites of the seat."],["Tyāgīśānanda","one of the oldest yoga-manuals; anticipates Gītā 6.11–13 and Patañjali (āsana, prāṇāyāma, pratyāhāra, suitable place)."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","practical core — steady erect spine, regulated breath, withdrawn senses, a quiet clean place; the \"boat of OM\" carries one over."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note this as among the earliest systematic descriptions of yogic posture, breath-control, and locale — a key text for the prehistory of classical Yoga."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 2.11–2.13","title":"The preliminary visions and the first signs of yoga","sa":["2.11: नीहारधूमार्कानिलानलानां खद्योतविद्युत्स्फटिकशशीनाम् । एतानि रूपाणि पुरःसराणि ब्रह्मण्यभिव्यक्तिकराणि योगे ॥ ११ ॥","2.12: पृथिव्यप्तेजोऽनिलखे समुत्थिते पञ्चात्मके योगगुणे प्रवृत्ते । न तस्य रोगो न जरा न मृत्युः प्राप्तस्य योगाग्निमयं शरीरम् ॥ १२ ॥","2.13: लघुत्वमारोग्यमलोलुपत्वं वर्णप्रसादः स्वरसौष्ठवं च । गन्धः शुभो मूत्रपुरीषमल्पं योगप्रवृत्तिं प्रथमां वदन्ति ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["nīhāradhūmārkānilānalānāṃ khadyotavidyutsphaṭikaśaśīnām | etāni rūpāṇi puraḥsarāṇi brahmaṇyabhivyaktikarāṇi yoge || 11 ||","pṛthivyaptejo'nilakhe samutthite pañcātmake yogaguṇe pravṛtte | na tasya rogo na jarā na mṛtyuḥ prāptasya yogāgnimayaṃ śarīram || 12 ||","laghutvam ārogyam alolupatvaṃ varṇaprasādaḥ svarasauṣṭhavaṃ ca | gandhaḥ śubho mūtrapurīṣam alpaṃ yogapravṛttiṃ prathamāṃ vadanti || 13 ||"],"words":[["nīhāra-dhūma-arka-anila-analānāṃ","gen. pl.","of mist, smoke, sun, wind, fire,"],["khadyota-vidyut-sphaṭika-śaśīnām","gen. pl.","of firefly, lightning, crystal, and moon —"],["etāni rūpāṇi puraḥsarāṇi","n. nom. pl. + adj. (forerunning)","these forms are the forerunners (preliminary appearances)"],["brahmaṇi abhivyaktikarāṇi yoge","loc. + adj. + loc.","that lead to the manifestation of Brahman in yoga"]],"lit":"\"<strong>Mist, smoke, sun, wind, fire, firefly, lightning, crystal, the moon</strong> — these forms, appearing <strong>as forerunners</strong>, lead to the <strong>manifestation of Brahman in yoga.</strong>\"<br>\"When the <strong>fivefold quality of yoga</strong> has arisen — born of <strong>earth, water, fire, air, and ether</strong> — and yoga has set in, then for him who has gained a body made of <strong>the fire of yoga</strong>, there is <strong>no disease, no old age, no death.</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>Lightness, health, steadiness (absence of craving), clearness of complexion, beauty of voice, a pleasant odour, and scanty excretions</strong> — these, they say, are the <strong>first signs of progress in yoga.</strong>\"","para":"v. 11 describes the <strong>inner lights and visions</strong> that appear as <strong>forerunners</strong> (<em>puraḥsara</em>) on the meditative path — subtle luminous phenomena (mist, smoke, lightning-flashes, crystal-clarity, moon-like radiance) heralding the <strong>manifestation of Brahman</strong>. (The advice, per the commentators, is to <em>note but not cling to</em> these.) v. 12 promises that as the <strong>five-element subtle body</strong> is mastered and \"the fire of yoga\" pervades it, the yogī becomes <strong>free of disease, aging, and death</strong> (a refined, \"yoga-fire\" body). v. 13 lists the <strong>first concrete signs of genuine progress</strong>: <strong>lightness, health, freedom from craving, a clear complexion, a fine voice, sweet bodily odour, and reduced waste</strong> — the early fruits that confirm the practice is taking root.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the lights (2.11) are <em>pūrva-rūpāṇi</em>, signs of approaching <em>brahma-sākṣātkāra</em>; not the goal, but encouraging marks; (2.12) the \"yoga-fire body\" = the perfected subtle body, free of decay; (2.13) the empirical signs of right practice."],["Śaṅkarānanda","enumerates the nine visions and the seven signs; warns not to mistake the lights for the goal."],["Tyāgīśānanda","a remarkably detailed early account of meditative phenomena and somatic signs; parallels later Haṭha-Yoga texts."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the lights are <em>real</em> experiences to be observed dispassionately; the bodily signs (lightness, health, fragrance) are practical confirmations."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the catalogue of photisms (2.11) and yogic <em>siddhi</em>-signs (2.13) as among the earliest in the literature."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 2.14–2.15","title":"The Self seen like a cleaned mirror; release","sa":["2.14: यथैव बिम्बं मृदयोपलिप्तं तेजोमयं भ्राजते तत् सुधान्तम् । तद्वाऽऽत्मतत्त्वं प्रसमीक्ष्य देही एकः कृतार्थो भवते वीतशोकः ॥ १४ ॥","2.15: यदात्मतत्त्वेन तु ब्रह्मतत्त्वं दीपोपमेनेह युक्तः प्रपश्येत् । अजं ध्रुवं सर्वतत्त्वैर्विशुद्धं ज्ञात्वा देवं मुच्यते सर्वपापैः ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["yathaiva bimbaṃ mṛdayopaliptaṃ tejomayaṃ bhrājate tat sudhāntam | tadvātmatattvaṃ prasamīkṣya dehī ekaḥ kṛtārtho bhavate vītaśokaḥ || 14 ||","yadātmatattvena tu brahmatattvaṃ dīpopameneha yuktaḥ prapaśyet | ajaṃ dhruvaṃ sarvatattvair viśuddhaṃ jñātvā devaṃ mucyate sarvapāpaiḥ || 15 ||"],"words":[["yathā eva bimbaṃ mṛdā upaliptaṃ","n. acc. (a mirror/disc) + instr. (with clay/dirt) + pp.","just as a mirror, (once) smeared with dirt,"],["tejomayaṃ bhrājate tat sudhāntam","adj. + pres. + pp. (well-cleansed)","shines bright and lustrous when well cleaned,"],["tadvā ātma-tattvaṃ prasamīkṣya","(so) + n. acc. + ger.","so, having clearly seen the truth of the Self,"],["dehī ekaḥ kṛtārthaḥ bhavate vītaśokaḥ","m. nom. (the embodied one) + adj.","the embodied one, become one (with it), fulfilled, is freed from sorrow"]],"lit":"\"Just as a <strong>mirror covered with dirt shines bright once well cleaned</strong>, so, having clearly seen the <strong>truth of the Self</strong>, the embodied one becomes <strong>one (with it), fulfilled, and free from sorrow.</strong>\"<br>\"When, by the <strong>light of the Self (ātma-tattva)</strong> — like a lamp — the disciplined one here <strong>sees the truth of Brahman</strong>, then, <strong>knowing God — the unborn, the constant, pure of all categories — he is freed from all sins.</strong>\"","para":"The <strong>goal of the yoga</strong>. v. 14: the Self is like a <strong>mirror caked with dust</strong> — its radiance hidden, not lost; <strong>cleaned by meditation</strong>, it shines, and the one who <strong>clearly sees the Self (ātma-tattva)</strong> becomes <strong>kṛtārtha</strong> (his life's purpose fulfilled) and <strong>vītaśoka</strong> (beyond grief). v. 15: by the <strong>lamp of the purified Self</strong> one <strong>sees the truth of Brahman</strong>, and <strong>knowing God — unborn (aja), constant (dhruva), pure beyond all the tattvas (categories) — is freed from all sin.</strong> The refrain <em>jñātvā devaṃ mucyate</em> (cf. 1.8) returns: yoga culminates not in powers but in the <strong>liberating vision of God/the Self.</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the mirror = the <em>antaḥkaraṇa</em> (inner organ), dust = <em>mala</em> (impurity); cleaned by yoga, it reflects the Self; seeing \"the truth of Brahman by the truth of the Self\" = realising their identity; release from all sin."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>ātma-tattvena brahma-tattvaṃ</em> — by the realised Self one knows Brahman; the lamp-simile for self-luminous knowledge."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the climax of the yoga-chapter — purification yields direct vision; \"unborn, constant, pure of all categories\" describes <em>nirguṇa</em> Brahman."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","clean the mirror of the mind by meditation; then the ever-present Self shines of itself; this is the fruit, beyond all <em>siddhis</em>."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the mirror- and lamp-similes for the purified mind and self-luminous knowledge."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 2.16–2.17","title":"The one God, present everywhere; homage","sa":["2.16: एषो ह देवः प्रदिशोऽनु सर्वाः । पूर्वो ह जातः स उ गर्भे अन्तः । स एव जातः स जनिष्यमाणः प्रत्यङ् जनास्तिष्ठति सर्वतोमुखः ॥ १६ ॥","2.17: यो देवो अग्नौ योऽप्सु यो विश्वं भुवनमाविवेश । य ओषधीषु यो वनस्पतिषु तस्मै देवाय नमो नमः ॥ १७ ॥"],"iast":["eṣo ha devaḥ pradiśo'nu sarvāḥ | pūrvo ha jātaḥ sa u garbhe antaḥ | sa eva jātaḥ sa janiṣyamāṇaḥ pratyaṅ janās tiṣṭhati sarvatomukhaḥ || 16 ||","yo devo agnau yo'psu yo viśvaṃ bhuvanam āviveśa | ya oṣadhīṣu yo vanaspatiṣu tasmai devāya namo namaḥ || 17 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ devaḥ agnau yaḥ apsu","rel. + loc.","the God who is in fire, who is in water,"],["yaḥ viśvaṃ bhuvanam āviveśa","rel. + acc. + pf.","who has entered the whole world,"],["yaḥ oṣadhīṣu yaḥ vanaspatiṣu","loc. pl.","who is in the herbs, who is in the great trees —"],["tasmai devāya namaḥ namaḥ","dat. + indecl. (repeated)","to that God, homage, homage!"]],"lit":"\"This <strong>God</strong> (faces) toward <strong>all the quarters</strong>. He was born first, and He is within the womb; He alone is (now) born, and He will be born (again); He stands behind all persons (<em>pratyaṅ</em>, the inmost), <strong>facing in every direction (sarvatomukha).</strong>\"<br>\"The God who is <strong>in fire</strong>, who is <strong>in water</strong>, who has <strong>entered the whole world</strong>, who is <strong>in the herbs</strong>, who is <strong>in the great trees</strong> — to that God, <strong>homage, homage!</strong>\"","para":"The chapter ends by lifting the gaze from technique to the <strong>omnipresent God</strong> whom the yoga reveals. v. 16: the one <strong>Deva faces every direction (sarvatomukha)</strong>, is the <strong>first-born and the ever-being-born</strong>, present <strong>in the womb</strong> and <strong>behind every person as the inmost Self (pratyaṅ)</strong> — at once transcendent source and indwelling presence. v. 17 (a beloved verse, echoing RV/AV): the same God is <strong>in fire and water, in herbs and trees, having entered the whole world</strong> — and the only fitting response is <strong>adoration: \"to that God, homage, homage!\"</strong> Yoga's culmination is <strong>devotion</strong> to the God seen everywhere — the keynote that swells through the rest of the Upaniṣad.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>deva</em> is the Self/Lord, <em>pratyaṅ</em> (the inner Self of all) yet <em>sarvatomukha</em> (all-pervading); \"born first / to be born\" = Hiraṇyagarbha's manifestation; immanent in all elements and plants."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the God is both <em>garbhe antaḥ</em> (in the womb, immanent) and <em>sarvatomukha</em> (omnipresent); homage to the all-pervader."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the yoga issues in <em>bhakti</em> — seeing God in fire, water, herbs, trees, and bowing; immanence as the ground of worship."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the practical fruit of meditation is to see the Divine everywhere and to live in adoration; v. 17 a perfect prayer of immanence."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","v. 17 noted as a Ṛgvedic/Atharvan-style hymn of the all-pervading deity, here turned to Upaniṣadic devotion."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Adhyāya 2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This is the <strong>Yoga chapter</strong> — and one of the most historically important early descriptions of meditative practice:","1. <strong>Consecration (2.1–7).</strong> The <strong>Savitṛ \"yoking\" verses</strong> set the tone: <em>yoga = yoking</em> the mind and intellect, by the Impeller's grace, toward the Light and the \"ancient Brahman.\" The ringing call <strong>\"Hear, O children of immortality!\"</strong> (2.5) proclaims the universal birthright of liberation. The inner conditions: kindled fire (concentration), controlled breath, overflowing soma (inner joy) (2.6).","2. <strong>The method (2.8–10).</strong> <strong>Posture</strong> (erect, steady, senses withdrawn into the heart; OM as the boat across saṃsāra), <strong>breath-control</strong> (prāṇāyāma; rein the mind like vicious horses, vigilantly), and <strong>place</strong> (clean, level, quiet, pleasant, sheltered). This is a recognisable ancestor of Gītā 6 and Patañjali's <em>āsana / prāṇāyāma / pratyāhāra / deśa</em>.","3. <strong>The signs (2.11–13).</strong> The <strong>forerunner lights</strong> (mist, smoke, lightning, crystal, moon — to be noted, not clung to) and the <strong>first bodily signs</strong> (lightness, health, no craving, clear complexion, fine voice, sweet odour, scant waste) confirm progress; the perfected \"<strong>yoga-fire body</strong>\" is free of disease, age, and death.","4. <strong>The goal (2.14–15).</strong> The mind, like a <strong>dust-covered mirror cleaned</strong>, reflects the Self; by the <strong>lamp of the Self</strong> one sees <strong>Brahman — unborn, constant, pure</strong> — and \"<strong>knowing God, is freed from all sin.</strong>\"","5. <strong>The fruit is devotion (2.16–17).</strong> The God revealed is <strong>all-facing yet inmost (sarvatomukha / pratyaṅ)</strong>, present <strong>in fire, water, herbs, trees, the whole world</strong> — to whom the heart bows: <strong>\"namo namaḥ.\"</strong>","The chapter thus binds <strong>Yoga (method) → jñāna (the vision of the Self/Brahman) → bhakti (adoration of the omnipresent God)</strong> — the integral path that the Upaniṣad will keep weaving."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Adhyāya 2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE YOGA OF ŚVETĀŚVATARA (Adhyāya 2)\n   ====================================\n   CONSECRATION (2.1-7): &quot;YUJ&quot; (yoke) the mind &amp; intellect toward the Light, by SAVITṚ&#x27;s grace\n        &quot;Śṛṇvantu viśve AMṚTASYA PUTRĀḤ!&quot; (Hear, O children of immortality!)  [2.5]\n        inner conditions: fire kindled · breath controlled · soma overflowing -&gt; illumined mind [2.6]\n                              |\n   METHOD (2.8-10):\n        POSTURE  -&gt; body erect &amp; even (chest-neck-head straight); senses drawn into the HEART\n                    cross saṃsāra by the BOAT OF OM (brahma-uḍupa)\n        BREATH   -&gt; prāṇāyāma; rein the mind like VICIOUS HORSES, vigilant &amp; undistracted\n        PLACE    -&gt; level · clean · quiet · pleasant · sheltered (a cave, wind-free)\n                              |\n   SIGNS (2.11-13):\n        forerunner LIGHTS: mist·smoke·sun·wind·fire·firefly·lightning·crystal·moon (note, don&#x27;t cling)\n        bodily SIGNS: lightness · health · no craving · clear skin · fine voice · sweet odour · scant waste\n        -&gt; &quot;yoga-fire body&quot;: no disease, no age, no death\n                              |\n   GOAL (2.14-15): mind = a DUST-COVERED MIRROR, CLEANED -&gt; reflects the SELF\n        by the LAMP of the Self -&gt; see BRAHMAN (unborn · constant · pure) -&gt; &quot;freed from all sin&quot;\n                              |\n   FRUIT = DEVOTION (2.16-17): the one God is all-facing (sarvatomukha) yet inmost (pratyaṅ),\n        in fire · water · herbs · trees · the whole world  -&gt;  &quot;NAMO NAMAḤ&quot;</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Adhyāya 2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Yoke, don't drift.</strong> <em>Yoga</em> means <em>to yoke</em>: at the start of practice, consciously <strong>harness mind and intellect</strong> toward the inner Light (2.1–5), and remember you are a <strong>\"child of immortality\"</strong> — practice from dignity, not deficiency.","2. <strong>Set the outer frame</strong> (2.8–10): a <strong>clean, quiet, pleasant</strong> spot; <strong>spine erect and steady</strong>, the three (chest, neck, head) aligned; <strong>regulate the breath</strong> gently; and <strong>withdraw the senses into the heart.</strong> OM is your \"boat\" across the restless currents.","3. <strong>Rein the mind vigilantly</strong> (2.9). Treat the mind like a chariot of <strong>unruly horses</strong> — hold it <em>firmly but alertly (apramatta)</em>; not forced rigidity, but undistracted steadiness.","4. <strong>Note the lights, but don't chase them</strong> (2.11). Inner luminous experiences may come; treat them as <strong>road-signs</strong>, not destinations — encouraging, but not the goal.","5. <strong>Read the real signs</strong> (2.13): genuine progress shows as <strong>lightness, health, calm, freedom from craving, clarity, a settled voice</strong> — quiet transformations of body and temperament, not dramatic powers.","6. <strong>Clean the mirror</strong> (2.14). The Self is already radiant, only <strong>dust-covered</strong>; meditation is the cleaning. Expect not to <em>create</em> an experience but to <em>uncover</em> what is ever-present.","7. <strong>Let yoga ripen into adoration</strong> (2.16–17). The end of the method is to <strong>see the one God in fire, water, herbs, trees — everywhere — and to bow.</strong> Carry the practice into a life of <em>\"namo namaḥ\"</em> — reverence for the Divine present in all."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 3 — The One Rudra, the Cosmic Person","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya 3","title":"The One Rudra, the Cosmic Person","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The chapter unfolds the</em> <strong>one God</strong> <em>of Adhyāya 1 as</em> <strong>Rudra</strong> — <em>\"One Rudra only, without a second\" (3.2) — at once the cosmic Creator-Destroyer-Protector and the</em> <strong>Puruṣa</strong>, <em>the supreme Person. Drawing in the great Vedic mantras (the all-eyed God; the thousand-headed Puruṣa; the thumb-sized indweller; \"subtler than the subtle, greater than the great\"), it presents the Lord as transcendent and immanent, beyond darkness, knowable only by His grace — \"knowing Him alone one passes beyond death; there is no other path.\"</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Verse 3.1","title":"The one Net-holder who rules by His powers","sa":["य एको जालवानीशत ईशनीभिः सर्वाँल्लोकानीशत ईशनीभिः । य एवैक उद्भवे सम्भवे च य एतद् विदुरमृतास्ते भवन्ति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["ya eko jālavān īśata īśanībhiḥ sarvāṃl lokān īśata īśanībhiḥ | ya evaika udbhave sambhave ca ya etad vidur amṛtās te bhavanti || 1 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ ekaḥ jālavān īśate īśanībhiḥ","rel. + nom. (holder of the net) + pres. + instr. pl. (by His ruling powers)","the One who, holding the net (of māyā), rules by His ruling powers,"],["sarvān lokān īśate īśanībhiḥ","acc. pl. + pres.","(who) rules all the worlds by His powers;"],["yaḥ eva ekaḥ udbhave sambhave ca","rel. + loc. (at the arising and the becoming)","who, being one, (presides) over the arising and the persisting (of the worlds) —"],["ye etad viduḥ amṛtāḥ te bhavanti","rel. pl. + pf. + nom. pl.","those who know this become immortal"]],"lit":"\"The <strong>One</strong> who, wielding the net (of māyā), <strong>rules by His ruling powers (īśanībhiḥ)</strong>, who rules all the worlds by His powers, who alone (presides) over arising and becoming — <strong>those who know this become immortal.</strong>\"","para":"The Lord is the <strong>one Net-holder (jālavān)</strong> — He who holds the <strong>net of māyā</strong> and <strong>governs all worlds by His powers (śaktis)</strong>, presiding over their <strong>origin and continuance</strong>. He is utterly <strong>one</strong> behind the manifold rule. <strong>To know this is to become immortal</strong> — the refrain (<em>ye etad vidur amṛtās te bhavanti</em>) that recurs through this chapter, binding knowledge to deathlessness.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>jālavān</em> = wielder of māyā (the \"net\"); <em>īśanībhiḥ</em> = His powers/śaktis; the one Lord rules creation and maintenance — knowledge of Him is immortality."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the \"net\" is the snare of saṃsāra He casts and controls; the powers are His māyā-śaktis."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the Lord both binds (the net) and frees (knowledge of Him); His unity behind plural powers."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","one God runs the cosmos through countless powers, as one weaver one web; know Him and be free."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>jālavān</em> (\"net-holder\") as an early image of the Lord's māyā; <em>īśanī</em> (ruling powers) prefigures Śakti."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 3.2","title":"One Rudro, without a second","sa":["एको हि रुद्रो न द्वितीयाय तस्थुर्य इमाँल्लोकानीशत ईशनीभिः । प्रत्यङ् जनास्तिष्ठति सञ्चुकोचान्तकाले संसृज्य विश्वा भुवनानि गोपाः ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["eko hi rudro na dvitīyāya tasthur ya imāṃl lokān īśata īśanībhiḥ | pratyaṅ janās tiṣṭhati sañcukocāntakāle saṃsṛjya viśvā bhuvanāni gopāḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["ekaḥ hi rudraḥ na dvitīyāya tasthuḥ","nom. + dat. + pf.","for there is one Rudra only; they did not allow for a second"],["yaḥ imān lokān īśate īśanībhiḥ","rel. + acc. pl. + pres. + instr.","who rules these worlds by His powers;"],["pratyaṅ janān tiṣṭhati","adv. (inward) + acc. pl. + pres.","He stands within (behind) (all) persons (as the inmost Self);"],["saṃsṛjya viśvā bhuvanāni gopāḥ sañcukoca antakāle","ger. + acc. pl. + nom. (the protector) + pf. of saṃ-kuc + loc.","having created all the worlds, He, the protector, draws (them) in at the end of time"]],"lit":"\"For there is <strong>one Rudra only — they admit no second</strong> — who rules these worlds by His powers. He <strong>stands within all persons (pratyaṅ, the inmost Self)</strong>; having <strong>created all the worlds</strong>, He, their <strong>protector (gopāḥ)</strong>, <strong>withdraws them at the end of time.</strong>\"","para":"The Upaniṣad's <strong>decisive monotheism</strong>: <strong>\"eko hi rudraḥ\" — one Rudra only, without a second.</strong> This one God is the <strong>creator, protector, and (at the cosmic end) the withdrawer</strong> of all the worlds — the threefold cosmic function (sṛṣṭi, sthiti, saṃhāra) here ascribed to <strong>Rudra/Śiva</strong>. And He is not merely transcendent ruler but <strong>pratyaṅ — the indwelling inmost Self of every being.</strong> Cosmic Lord and inner Self are one. (A foundational verse for Śaiva theology and for Vedāntic theism alike.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"one Rudra, no second\" = the non-dual Lord; <em>pratyaṅ</em> = He is the inner Self of all; creation, protection, dissolution are His; ultimately the one Brahman/Ātman."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>na dvitīyāya tasthuḥ</em> — the knowers leave no room for a second God; Rudra = the destroyer-of-sorrow, the Self."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the great monotheistic declaration; the threefold function (create-protect-withdraw) unified in one God who is also the indweller."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","one God, immanent (pratyaṅ) and transcendent (gopāḥ); the basis of unwavering devotion."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","\"eko hi rudraḥ\" marks the emergence of monotheistic Śaivism; <em>pratyaṅ</em> fuses cosmic deity with inner Self."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 3.3–3.4","title":"The all-eyed Creator; Rudra who begot Hiraṇyagarbha","sa":["3.3: विश्वतश्चक्षुरुत विश्वतोमुखो विश्वतोबाहुरुत विश्वतस्पात् । सं बाहुभ्यां धमति सम्पतत्रैर्द्यावाभूमी जनयन् देव एकः ॥ ३ ॥","3.4: यो देवानां प्रभवश्चोद्भवश्च विश्वाधिपो रुद्रो महर्षिः । हिरण्यगर्भं जनयामास पूर्वं स नो बुद्ध्या शुभया संयुनक्तु ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["viśvataścakṣur uta viśvatomukho viśvatobāhur uta viśvataspāt | saṃ bāhubhyāṃ dhamati sampatatrair dyāvābhūmī janayan deva ekaḥ || 3 ||","yo devānāṃ prabhavaścodbhavaśca viśvādhipo rudro maharṣiḥ | hiraṇyagarbhaṃ janayāmāsa pūrvaṃ sa no buddhyā śubhayā saṃyunaktu || 4 ||"],"words":[["viśvataḥ-cakṣuḥ uta viśvataḥ-mukhaḥ","adj. (eyes on all sides; faces on all sides)","with eyes everywhere, faces everywhere,"],["viśvataḥ-bāhuḥ uta viśvataḥ-pāt","(arms on all sides; feet on all sides)","arms everywhere, feet everywhere;"],["saṃ bāhubhyāṃ dhamati sampatatraiḥ","instr. du. + pres. + instr. (with wings)","He forges (the worlds) with His arms, with wings (bellows);"],["dyāvā-bhūmī janayan devaḥ ekaḥ","acc. du. + pres.part. + nom.","the one God, generating heaven and earth"]],"lit":"\"With <strong>eyes on every side, faces on every side, arms and feet on every side</strong>, the <strong>one God</strong> forges (creation) with His arms and with wings (as with bellows), bringing forth <strong>heaven and earth.</strong>\" <em>(= Ṛgveda 10.81.3, the Viśvakarman hymn.)</em><br>\"He who is the <strong>source and origin of the gods, the ruler of all, Rudra, the great seer</strong>, who <strong>begot Hiraṇyagarbha (the cosmic Golden Germ) in the beginning</strong> — may He <strong>endow us with clear understanding.</strong>\"","para":"v. 3 (a Ṛgvedic Viśvakarman mantra) depicts the <strong>one God as the cosmic artisan</strong> — all-eyed, all-faced, all-armed — <strong>forging the worlds</strong> as a smith works with bellows, <strong>generating heaven and earth</strong>. v. 4 names Him <strong>Rudra, the great ṛṣi, ruler of all, the very source of the gods</strong>, who <strong>first produced Hiraṇyagarbha</strong> (the cosmic soul/Brahmā) — and adds a <strong>prayer</strong>: <em>\"may He unite us with auspicious wisdom (buddhyā śubhayā saṃyunaktu).\"</em> (Note: even the first of created beings, Hiraṇyagarbha, is <em>His</em> offspring — so Rudra is the Supreme, prior to all gods.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"eyes/faces everywhere\" = the Lord's all-pervading consciousness (the limbs are figurative); He is the material-and-efficient cause; begetting Hiraṇyagarbha shows His supremacy over even the first-born."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the \"bellows/wings\" = the means of cosmic \"forging\"; the prayer for <em>śubhā buddhi</em> (good understanding) is for the grace that enables knowledge."],["Tyāgīśānanda","Rudra is set above Hiraṇyagarbha (whom He creates) — i.e., the Supreme Lord, not a sectarian sub-deity; the prayer introduces grace."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the cosmic Person works through all eyes and hands; pray for the auspicious intellect that alone can know Him."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","3.3 = RV 10.81.3 (Viśvakarman); note the assertion of Rudra's priority over Hiraṇyagarbha."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 3.5–3.6","title":"The Śatarudriya prayer: show us Thy gracious form","sa":["3.5: या ते रुद्र शिवा तनूरघोराऽपापकाशिनी । तया नस्तनुवा शन्तमया गिरिशन्ताभिचाकशीहि ॥ ५ ॥","3.6: याभिषुं गिरिशन्त हस्ते बिभर्ष्यस्तवे । शिवां गिरित्र तां कुरु मा हिंसीः पुरुषं जगत् ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["yā te rudra śivā tanūr aghorā'pāpakāśinī | tayā nas tanuvā śantamayā giriśantābhicākaśīhi || 5 ||","yābhiṣuṃ giriśanta haste bibharṣyastave | śivāṃ giritra tāṃ kuru mā hiṃsīḥ puruṣaṃ jagat || 6 ||"],"words":[["yā te rudra śivā tanūḥ aghorā apāpa-kāśinī","rel. f. + voc. + nom. (auspicious body) + (non-terrible, sin-effacing)","O Rudra, that gracious form of Thine, not terrible, revealing no evil —"],["tayā naḥ tanuvā śantamayā","instr. (with that most peace-giving form)","with that most peaceful form"],["giriśanta abhicākaśīhi","voc. (dweller on the mountain, giver of peace) + impv.","O dweller-on-the-mountain, look graciously upon us"]],"lit":"\"O <strong>Rudra</strong>, that <strong>gracious form (śivā tanū)</strong> of Thine — <strong>not terrible, effacing all evil</strong> — with that <strong>most peace-bringing form</strong>, O Dweller-on-the-Mountain (Giriśanta), <strong>look graciously upon us.</strong>\"<br>\"O Giriśanta, the <strong>arrow Thou holdest in Thy hand to shoot</strong> — make it <strong>gracious (śivā)</strong>, O Protector-on-the-Mountain (Giritra); <strong>harm not man or world.</strong>\" <em>(Both from the Yajurveda Śatarudrīya / Rudra-praśna.)</em>","para":"Two verses lifted from the <strong>Śatarudrīya</strong> (the great Vedic litany to Rudra), turning the chapter from doctrine to <strong>prayer and surrender</strong>. They petition Rudra to reveal His <strong>śivā tanū</strong> — His <strong>benign, peaceful, sin-dispelling form</strong> (the \"Śiva\" aspect, from which the name <em>Śiva</em> derives) — and to <strong>withhold His destructive \"arrow,\"</strong> sparing man and world. This is <strong>bhakti</strong> in the older Vedic key: approaching the awesome God with reverence, seeking His <strong>grace and protection</strong>, not merely His knowledge. (The names <em>Giriśanta, Giritra</em> — \"He who gives peace / protects from the mountain\" — are classic Rudra-epithets.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","prays for the Lord's <em>anugraha</em> (grace) in His benign aspect; the \"gracious form\" is His <em>prasāda</em>; the arrow = His power to destroy, asked to be turned to blessing."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>śivā tanū</em> = the auspicious body; the verses cultivate the devotional attitude of the <em>upāsaka</em>."],["Tyāgīśānanda","notes the Śatarudrīya source; the chapter weds <em>jñāna</em> with <em>bhakti</em> and <em>prapatti</em> (surrender) — a hallmark of this Upaniṣad."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the soul approaches the terrible-yet-gracious God, begging for His peaceful, sin-removing aspect — the heart of devotion."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","3.5–6 = Vājasaneyi Śatarudrīya (VS 16.2, 16.3); evidence of the text's deliberate Rudra-Śaiva affiliation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 3.7–3.10","title":"The supreme Brahman beyond all, knowing whom one is immortal","sa":["3.7: ततः परं ब्रह्म परं बृहन्तं यथानिकायं सर्वभूतेषु गूढम् । विश्वस्यैकं परिवेष्टितारमीशं तं ज्ञात्वाऽमृता भवन्ति ॥ ७ ॥","3.8: वेदाहमेतं पुरुषं महान्तमादित्यवर्णं तमसः परस्तात् । तमेव विदित्वातिमृत्युमेति नान्यः पन्था विद्यतेऽयनाय ॥ ८ ॥","3.9: यस्मात् परं नापरमस्ति किञ्चिद्यस्मान्नणीयो न ज्यायोऽस्ति कश्चित् । वृक्ष इव स्तब्धो दिवि तिष्ठत्येकस्तेनेदं पूर्णं पुरुषेण सर्वम् ॥ ९ ॥","3.10: ततो यदुत्तरततं तदरूपमनामयम् । य एतद्विदुरमृतास्ते भवन्ति अथेतरे दुःखमेवापियन्ति ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["tataḥ paraṃ brahma paraṃ bṛhantaṃ yathānikāyaṃ sarvabhūteṣu gūḍham | viśvasyaikaṃ pariveṣṭitāram īśaṃ taṃ jñātvāmṛtā bhavanti || 7 ||","vedāham etaṃ puruṣaṃ mahāntam ādityavarṇaṃ tamasaḥ parastāt | tam eva viditvātimṛtyum eti nānyaḥ panthā vidyate'yanāya || 8 ||","yasmāt paraṃ nāparamasti kiñcid yasmān naṇīyo na jyāyo'sti kaścit | vṛkṣa iva stabdho divi tiṣṭhaty ekas tenedaṃ pūrṇaṃ puruṣeṇa sarvam || 9 ||","tato yad uttarataraṃ tad arūpam anāmayam | ya etad vidur amṛtās te bhavanti athetare duḥkham evāpiyanti || 10 ||"],"words":[["veda aham etaṃ puruṣaṃ mahāntam","1 sg. pf. + acc.","I know this great Person,"],["āditya-varṇaṃ tamasaḥ parastāt","acc. (sun-coloured) + abl. (beyond darkness)","sun-coloured, beyond the darkness (of ignorance);"],["tam eva viditvā atimṛtyum eti","acc. + ger. + acc. + pres.","knowing Him alone, one passes beyond death;"],["na anyaḥ panthāḥ vidyate ayanāya","nom. + pres. pass. + dat.","there is no other path for the going (to liberation)"]],"lit":"\"<strong>Higher than that (the world)</strong> is <strong>Brahman, the supreme, the vast</strong>, hidden in all beings according to each body, <strong>the One who envelops the universe</strong> — knowing Him, the Lord, <strong>they become immortal.</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>I know this great Person, sun-coloured, beyond the darkness.</strong> Knowing Him alone, one <strong>passes beyond death; there is no other path for the going (to liberation).</strong>\" <em>(≈ a famous mantra, also in the Puruṣa-context of the Yajurveda; repeated at 6.15.)</em><br>\"Than whom there is <strong>nothing higher, nothing smaller, nothing greater</strong> — <strong>the One who stands fixed like a tree, immovable, in His own glory (the 'heaven')</strong> — by that <strong>Person all this is filled (pūrṇa).</strong>\"<br>\"What is <strong>higher than that — formless and free of ill</strong> — those who know it <strong>become immortal; but the rest go only to sorrow.</strong>\"","para":"Four verses on the <strong>supreme, transcendent Brahman-Puruṣa</strong>: (3.7) higher than the whole created order is <strong>Brahman, vast and supreme</strong>, yet <strong>hidden in every being</strong> as its inner measure, the <strong>One enwrapping all</strong> — to know Him is immortality. (3.8) the great formula of <strong>luminous transcendence</strong>: the Person is <strong>sun-coloured, beyond all darkness (tamas = ignorance/death)</strong>; <strong>\"knowing Him alone one crosses death — no other path exists for liberation (nānyaḥ panthā vidyate'yanāya).\"\"</strong> (3.9) He is the <strong>absolute</strong> — none higher, smaller, or greater — standing <strong>immovable like a tree</strong> in His own majesty, <strong>filling all (tenedaṃ pūrṇaṃ … sarvam).</strong> (3.10) and the formless, faultless Reality beyond — known, it gives immortality; unknown, only sorrow. The refrain hammers: <strong>knowledge of the one God = deathlessness; ignorance = suffering.</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the supreme Brahman is <em>para</em> (transcendent) yet <em>gūḍha</em> (hidden in all); 3.8's \"sun-coloured, beyond darkness\" = the self-luminous Self beyond <em>avidyā</em>; \"no other path\" = knowledge alone liberates (not works); 3.9 = the all-pervading Puruṣa filling everything."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>yathānikāyaṃ</em> = present in each body proportionate to it; <em>vṛkṣa iva stabdhaḥ</em> = unmoving in His glory."],["Tyāgīśānanda","3.8 the central soteriological verse (\"knowing Him alone, beyond death; no other way\"); the God is luminous, transcendent, all-filling."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","He is beyond darkness (ignorance) — realise Him and cross death; there is literally no alternative road."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","3.8 (= a mantra also at VS 31.18 / 6.15 here) and 3.9–10 noted as borrowed/adapted Puruṣa-mantras; \"no other path\" a key liberation-claim."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 3.11–3.13","title":"All-faced; the Lord of sattva; the thumb-sized indweller","sa":["3.11: सर्वानन शिरोग्रीवः सर्वभूतगुहाशयः । सर्वव्यापी स भगवांस्तस्मात् सर्वगतः शिवः ॥ ११ ॥","3.12: महान् प्रभुर्वै पुरुषः सत्वस्यैष प्रवर्तकः । सुनिर्मलामिमां प्राप्तिमीशानो ज्योतिरव्ययः ॥ १२ ॥","3.13: अङ्गुष्ठमात्रः पुरुषोऽन्तरात्मा सदा जनानां हृदये संनिविष्टः । हृदा मनीषा मनसाभिक्लृप्तो य एतद् विदुरमृतास्ते भवन्ति ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["sarvānana śirogrīvaḥ sarvabhūtaguhāśayaḥ | sarvavyāpī sa bhagavāṃs tasmāt sarvagataḥ śivaḥ || 11 ||","mahān prabhur vai puruṣaḥ satvasyaiṣa pravartakaḥ | sunirmalām imāṃ prāptim īśāno jyotir avyayaḥ || 12 ||","aṅguṣṭhamātraḥ puruṣo'ntarātmā sadā janānāṃ hṛdaye sanniviṣṭaḥ | hṛdā manīṣā manasābhiklṛpto ya etad vidur amṛtās te bhavanti || 13 ||"],"words":[["aṅguṣṭha-mātraḥ puruṣaḥ antarātmā","nom. (thumb-sized) + (the inner self)","the Person, thumb-sized, the inner Self,"],["sadā janānāṃ hṛdaye sanniviṣṭaḥ","gen. pl. + loc. + pp.","ever seated in the heart of people;"],["hṛdā manīṣā manasā abhiklṛptaḥ","instr. (by the heart, by thought, by the mind) + pp.","grasped (revealed) by the heart, by the intellect, by the mind —"],["ye etad viduḥ amṛtāḥ te bhavanti","rel. + pf.","those who know this become immortal"]],"lit":"\"<strong>All-faced, all-headed, all-necked</strong>, dwelling in the <strong>heart-cave of all beings</strong>, <strong>all-pervading</strong> is that <strong>Lord (Bhagavān)</strong>; therefore He is <strong>all-present, the Auspicious (Śiva).</strong>\"<br>\"The <strong>Person is the great Lord</strong>, the <strong>mover of sattva (the impeller of pure being/the mind toward goodness)</strong>; (He bestows) this <strong>most stainless attainment</strong>; <strong>He, the Ruler (Īśāna), is light imperishable.</strong>\"<br>\"The <strong>Person, thumb-sized (aṅguṣṭha-mātra), the inner Self, ever seated in the heart of all</strong>, is <strong>revealed by the heart, the intellect, and the mind</strong> — those who know this <strong>become immortal.</strong>\" <em>(cf. Kaṭha 2.1.12–13.)</em>","para":"Three verses on <strong>immanence</strong>. (3.11) The Lord is <strong>all-faced and all-pervading</strong>, dwelling in <strong>every heart-cave</strong>, and <em>therefore</em> called <strong>Śiva (the auspicious, all-good).</strong> (3.12) He is the <strong>great Lord, the impeller of sattva</strong> (the power that turns beings toward purity and the goal), bestower of the <strong>stainless attainment (liberation)</strong>, <strong>Īśāna, imperishable Light.</strong> (3.13) Most famously, He is the <strong>aṅguṣṭha-mātra puruṣa — the \"thumb-sized\" Person</strong>, the <strong>inner Self enthroned in the heart</strong> of everyone, <strong>knowable by the purified heart-and-mind</strong> (an inheritance from the Kaṭha). The transcendent God of 3.7–10 is the <strong>intimate indweller</strong> of 3.11–13 — to be sought <em>within</em>.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the \"thumb-sized\" measure = the heart-space/the size of the <em>liṅga</em> in the heart, a meditative localisation of the limitless Self; \"revealed by heart, intellect, mind\" = grasped by the purified inner organ; Śiva = the all-auspicious all-pervader."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>sattvasya pravartakaḥ</em> = the inspirer of the pure mind toward Brahman; <em>aṅguṣṭhamātra</em> a meditation-aid, not a literal size."],["Tyāgīśānanda","immanence-verses; the cosmic God is the heart-dwelling Self, accessible to devotion and meditation."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","meditate on the thumb-sized Person in the heart; the all-pervading Lord is nearer than the nearest."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","3.13 ≈ Kaṭha 6.17 / 4.12–13; <em>aṅguṣṭhamātra</em> a widespread Upaniṣadic image of the indwelling soul."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 3.14–3.16","title":"The Puruṣa-sūkta: the thousand-headed Person","sa":["3.14: सहस्रशीर्षा पुरुषः सहस्राक्षः सहस्रपात् । स भूमिं विश्वतो वृत्वा अत्यतिष्ठद्दशाङ्गुलम् ॥ १४ ॥","3.15: पुरुष एवेदं सर्वं यद् भूतं यच्च भव्यम् । उतामृतत्वस्येशानो यदन्नेनातिरोहति ॥ १५ ॥","3.16: सर्वतः पाणिपादं तत् सर्वतोऽक्षिशिरोमुखम् । सर्वतः श्रुतिमल्लोके सर्वमावृत्य तिष्ठति ॥ १६ ॥"],"iast":["sahasraśīrṣā puruṣaḥ sahasrākṣaḥ sahasrapāt | sa bhūmiṃ viśvato vṛtvā atyatiṣṭhad daśāṅgulam || 14 ||","puruṣa evedaṃ sarvaṃ yad bhūtaṃ yacca bhavyam | utāmṛtatvasyeśāno yad annenātirohati || 15 ||","sarvataḥ pāṇipādaṃ tat sarvato'kṣiśiromukham | sarvataḥ śrutimal loke sarvam āvṛtya tiṣṭhati || 16 ||"],"words":[["sahasra-śīrṣā puruṣaḥ sahasra-akṣaḥ sahasra-pāt","nom. (thousand-headed, -eyed, -footed)","the Person of a thousand heads, a thousand eyes, a thousand feet;"],["saḥ bhūmiṃ viśvataḥ vṛtvā atyatiṣṭhat daśa-aṅgulam","acc. + ger. + impf. + acc.","covering the earth on all sides, He extended ten finger-breadths beyond"],["puruṣaḥ eva idaṃ sarvaṃ yat bhūtaṃ yat ca bhavyam","nom. + n.","the Person alone is all this — what has been and what will be;"],["uta amṛtatvasya īśānaḥ yat annena atirohati","+ gen. + nom. + rel.","and He is the Lord of immortality, (and of all) that grows by food"]],"lit":"\"The <strong>Person of a thousand heads, a thousand eyes, a thousand feet</strong> — covering the earth on every side, He extended <strong>ten finger-breadths beyond</strong> (it).\" <em>(= Ṛgveda 10.90.1, the Puruṣa-sūkta.)</em><br>\"The <strong>Person alone is all this — what has been and what will be</strong>; and He is the <strong>Lord of immortality</strong>, and (of all) that <strong>grows by food.</strong>\" <em>(= RV 10.90.2.)</em><br>\"With <strong>hands and feet on every side, eyes, heads, and faces on every side, ears on every side</strong>, <strong>He stands enveloping all</strong> in the world.\" <em>(≈ RV / Bhagavad-Gītā 13.13.)</em>","para":"The Upaniṣad incorporates the great <strong>Puruṣa-sūkta</strong> (RV 10.90), identifying its <strong>cosmic thousand-formed Person</strong> with its own one God. (3.14) the <strong>Puruṣa</strong> has innumerable heads, eyes, feet — He is the entire embodied cosmos, yet <strong>exceeds it (\"ten fingers beyond\")</strong>: immanent <em>and</em> transcendent. (3.15) <strong>He is all that was and will be</strong>, the <strong>Lord of immortality</strong> and of the food-fed world of becoming — i.e., Lord of both the eternal and the temporal. (3.16) restates His all-pervasion (hands, feet, eyes everywhere), <strong>enveloping everything.</strong> The point of the borrowing: the <strong>personal Rudra/Śiva of this Upaniṣad <em>is</em> the universal Puruṣa of the Veda</strong> — sectarian devotion grounded in the oldest revelation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the \"thousand\" = innumerable (all beings are His heads/eyes/feet); \"ten fingers beyond\" = transcending the cosmos He pervades; Lord of \"immortality and food\" = of the unchanging and the changing."],["Śaṅkarānanda","identifies the Puruṣa-sūkta Person with the Self/Lord; <em>daśāṅgulam</em> a figure for transcendence."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the deliberate equation of Rudra with the Vedic Puruṣa, securing scriptural authority for the theism."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the cosmic Person is your own Self writ large; all hands that work, all eyes that see, are His."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","3.14–16 = Puruṣa-sūkta (RV 10.90.1–2) + the \"hands everywhere\" mantra; a clear case of Vedic incorporation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 3.17–3.18","title":"Shining through the senses yet beyond them; the swan in the nine-gated city","sa":["3.17: सर्वेन्द्रियगुणाभासं सर्वेन्द्रियविवर्जितम् । सर्वस्य प्रभुमीशानं सर्वस्य शरणं सुहृत् ॥ १७ ॥","3.18: नवद्वारे पुरे देही हंसो लेलायते बहिः । वशी सर्वस्य लोकस्य स्थावरस्य चरस्य च ॥ १८ ॥"],"iast":["sarvendriyaguṇābhāsaṃ sarvendriyavivarjitam | sarvasya prabhum īśānaṃ sarvasya śaraṇaṃ suhṛt || 17 ||","navadvāre pure dehī haṃso lelāyate bahiḥ | vaśī sarvasya lokasya sthāvarasya carasya ca || 18 ||"],"words":[["sarva-indriya-guṇa-ābhāsaṃ","acc. (illumining the qualities/functions of all the senses)","shining forth through the functions of all the senses,"],["sarva-indriya-vivarjitam","acc. (devoid of all the senses)","yet itself devoid of all the senses;"],["sarvasya prabhum īśānaṃ","acc. (the Lord, Ruler of all)","the Lord and Ruler of all,"],["sarvasya śaraṇaṃ suhṛt","acc. (refuge of all, the friend)","the refuge of all, the (great) Friend"]],"lit":"\"<strong>Shining through the functions of all the senses, yet itself without any sense-organ</strong>, the <strong>Lord and Ruler of all, the refuge of all, the (loving) Friend (suhṛt).</strong>\" <em>(cf. Gītā 13.14.)</em><br>\"The <strong>embodied one (the Self), the swan</strong>, in the <strong>city of nine gates</strong> (the body), <strong>sports/flutters even beyond it</strong>; the <strong>controller of the whole world, both the moving and the unmoving.</strong>\" <em>(cf. Kaṭha; Gītā 5.13.)</em>","para":"v. 17: a precise paradox — the Self/God <strong>lends light to every sense-function</strong> (it is by His consciousness that eyes see, ears hear) <strong>yet is itself sense-less</strong> (not an organ, not dependent on organs); He is <strong>Lord, refuge, and loving Friend (suhṛt)</strong> of all — a tender devotional note. v. 18: the <strong>dehī (embodied Self), the haṃsa (swan)</strong>, dwells in the <strong>nine-gated city</strong> (the body, with its nine apertures) yet <strong>ranges beyond it (bahiḥ)</strong> — free, the <strong>ruler of all that moves and is still.</strong> Immanent operator of the senses, yet transcendent and free.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Self illumines the senses (their \"appearance\") while being <em>nirindriya</em> (organ-less) — the witness that empowers without being involved; <em>suhṛt</em> = the unconditioned benefactor (cf. Gītā \"suhṛdaṃ sarvabhūtānām\")."],["Śaṅkarānanda","\"shines through the senses, devoid of senses\" — the witness-consciousness; the swan free even while in the body-city."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the loving epithets (refuge, friend) deepen the devotional theism; v. 18 the soul's essential freedom."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","God is the light behind your every perception, yet untouched; take refuge in Him, the Friend; the swan-Self is ever free."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","3.17 ≈ Gītā 13.14; 3.18 the \"nine-gated city\" (cf. Kaṭha 5.1, Gītā 5.13) — the body as the soul's city."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 3.19–3.21","title":"Without hands He grasps; subtler than the subtle; the unaging Person","sa":["3.19: अपाणिपादो जवनो ग्रहीता पश्यत्यचक्षुः स शृणोत्यकर्णः । स वेत्ति वेद्यं न च तस्यास्ति वेत्ता तमाहुरग्र्यं पुरुषं महान्तम् ॥ १९ ॥","3.20: अणोरणीयान् महतो महीयानात्मा गुहायां निहितोऽस्य जन्तोः । तमक्रतुः पश्यति वीतशोको धातुः प्रसादान्महिमानमीशम् ॥ २० ॥","3.21: वेदाहमेतमजरं पुराणं सर्वात्मानं सर्वगतं विभुत्वात् । जन्मनिरोधं प्रवदन्ति यस्य ब्रह्मवादिनो हि प्रवदन्ति नित्यम् ॥ २१ ॥"],"iast":["apāṇipādo javano grahītā paśyaty acakṣuḥ sa śṛṇoty akarṇaḥ | sa vetti vedyaṃ na ca tasyāsti vettā tam āhur agryaṃ puruṣaṃ mahāntam || 19 ||","aṇor aṇīyān mahato mahīyān ātmā guhāyāṃ nihito'sya jantoḥ | tam akratuḥ paśyati vītaśoko dhātuḥ prasādān mahimānam īśam || 20 ||","vedāham etam ajaraṃ purāṇaṃ sarvātmānaṃ sarvagataṃ vibhutvāt | janmanirodhaṃ pravadanti yasya brahmavādino hi pravadanti nityam || 21 ||"],"words":[["aṇoḥ aṇīyān mahataḥ mahīyān","abl. + compar. (subtler than the subtle; greater than the great)","subtler than the subtle, greater than the great,"],["ātmā guhāyāṃ nihitaḥ asya jantoḥ","nom. + loc. + pp. + gen.","the Self is set in the heart-cave of this creature;"],["tam akratuḥ paśyati vītaśokaḥ","acc. + nom. (the desireless one) + (free of sorrow)","the desireless one, freed of sorrow, beholds Him —"],["dhātuḥ prasādāt mahimānam īśam","gen. (of the Creator/the Self) + abl. (by grace) + acc.","by the grace of the Creator, (beholds) the majesty, the Lord"]],"lit":"\"<strong>Without hands or feet, He grasps and moves swiftly; without eyes, He sees; without ears, He hears.</strong> He knows whatever is to be known, but <strong>none knows Him</strong>; they call Him the <strong>foremost, the great Person.</strong>\" <em>(cf. Kaṭha; Muṇḍaka.)</em><br>\"<strong>Subtler than the subtle, greater than the great</strong>, the <strong>Self is set in the heart-cave</strong> of (every) creature. The <strong>desireless one (akratu), freed of sorrow, beholds Him — the majesty, the Lord — by the grace of the Creator (dhātuḥ prasādāt).</strong>\" <em>(= Kaṭha 1.2.20, Mahānārāyaṇa.)</em><br>\"<strong>I know this Person — unaging, ancient, the Self of all, all-pervading by His omnipresence</strong> — whom the discoursers on Brahman declare to be <strong>free of birth (janma-nirodha)</strong>, ever-existing.\"","para":"The chapter's summit. (3.19) the <strong>paradox of the Self's powers</strong>: it <strong>acts, moves, sees, hears, knows — without organs</strong>, for it is <strong>pure consciousness</strong>, the <strong>knower whom none can know as an object</strong> (cf. BU 2.4.14, 3.4.2). (3.20) the magnificent <strong>aṇor-aṇīyān mahato mahīyān</strong> — <strong>subtler than the subtlest, vaster than the vastest</strong> — the <strong>Self in the heart-cave</strong> of all; and crucially, He is beheld by the <strong>desireless (akratu)</strong>, freed of grief, <strong>by the grace of the Creator (dhātuḥ prasādāt)</strong> — explicit <strong>divine grace</strong> as the means of vision. (3.21) the seer's confident testimony: <strong>\"I know this Person — unaging, ancient, the all-pervading Self, free of birth.\"</strong> The transcendent Absolute, the heart-dwelling Self, and the gracious Lord are <strong>one</strong> — and known by <strong>desirelessness and grace.</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","3.19 — the Self's organless agency = consciousness empowering all functions; \"none knows Him\" = the unobjectifiable witness. 3.20 = the supreme description; <em>dhātuḥ prasādāt</em> read by Advaitins as \"by the serenity/purity of the (own) self/intellect,\" but the verse's plain sense (and the theistic context) is <strong>divine grace</strong> — a landmark."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>akratu</em> = free of desire/will; the vision dawns on the desireless by grace."],["Tyāgīśānanda","stresses <em>dhātuḥ prasādāt</em> as <strong>grace</strong> — the Upaniṣad's clearest grace-statement before 6.21–23; the union of effort (desirelessness) and grace."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the subtle-yet-vast Self in the heart is seen when desire is stilled <em>and</em> grace descends — the meeting of self-effort and the Lord's mercy."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","3.19–20 ≈ Kaṭha 1.2.20–21 / Mahānārāyaṇa; <em>dhātuḥ prasādāt</em> noted as a key (and debated) early statement of grace."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Adhyāya 3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This chapter is the <strong>theological heart</strong> of the Upaniṣad — the <strong>one God as Rudra/Puruṣa</strong>, transcendent and immanent, known by knowledge, devotion, and grace:","1. <strong>Strict monotheism (3.1–4).</strong> The <strong>one Net-holder</strong> ruling by His <strong>powers (śaktis)</strong>; <strong>\"one Rudra only, without a second\" (eko hi rudraḥ)</strong> — creator, protector, withdrawer, <em>and</em> the <strong>inmost Self (pratyaṅ)</strong>; the all-eyed cosmic artisan (RV Viśvakarman) who <strong>begot even Hiraṇyagarbha</strong> — hence the Supreme, prior to all gods.","2. <strong>Devotion and surrender (3.5–6).</strong> The <strong>Śatarudrīya prayers</strong> for Rudra's <strong>gracious, peaceful form (śivā tanū)</strong> and His sparing arrow — <em>bhakti</em> and <em>prapatti</em> woven into the doctrine.","3. <strong>Transcendence (3.7–10).</strong> The supreme <strong>Brahman-Puruṣa</strong> beyond all, <strong>sun-coloured, beyond darkness</strong>, the <strong>All-filling</strong> — <strong>\"knowing Him alone, one crosses death; no other path exists.\"</strong>","4. <strong>Immanence (3.11–18).</strong> The <strong>all-faced Śiva</strong> in every heart-cave; the <strong>thumb-sized Person</strong>; the <strong>Puruṣa-sūkta's</strong> thousand-formed cosmic Person who yet <strong>exceeds the cosmos</strong>; the light <strong>behind all senses</strong> though <strong>sense-less</strong>; the <strong>swan in the nine-gated city</strong>, free.","5. <strong>The Absolute, and grace (3.19–21).</strong> <strong>Without organs He does all</strong>; the <strong>unknowable Knower</strong>; <strong>subtler than the subtle, vaster than the vast</strong>, in the heart — beheld by the <strong>desireless, by the grace of the Creator (dhātuḥ prasādāt)</strong> — the <strong>unaging, ancient, birthless Self of all.</strong>","The chapter's genius is its <strong>synthesis</strong>: the impersonal Brahman of the older Upaniṣads, the cosmic Puruṣa of the Veda, and the personal Rudra-Śiva of emerging theism are affirmed as <strong>one Reality</strong> — approached by <strong>jñāna (knowledge), upāsanā/dhyāna (meditation), bhakti (devotion), and prasāda (grace).</strong>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Adhyāya 3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE ONE RUDRA / PURUṢA (Adhyāya 3)\n   ==================================\n   MONOTHEISM:  &quot;EKO HI RUDRO na dvitīyāya&quot; (one Rudra, no second)  [3.2]\n        net-holder ruling by His ŚAKTIS [3.1] · all-eyed cosmic artisan (RV) [3.3]\n        begot even HIRAṆYAGARBHA -&gt; prior to all gods [3.4]\n        creator + protector + withdrawer  AND  pratyaṅ = the inmost Self [3.2]\n                              |\n   DEVOTION (Śatarudrīya): &quot;show us Thy ŚIVĀ TANŪ (gracious form); spare Thy arrow&quot; [3.5-6]\n                              |\n   TRANSCENDENT:  supreme Brahman, sun-coloured, BEYOND DARKNESS [3.8]\n        &quot;knowing Him ALONE -&gt; beyond death; NO OTHER PATH&quot; · none higher/smaller/greater · ALL-FILLING [3.7-10]\n                              |\n   IMMANENT:  all-faced ŚIVA in every heart-cave [3.11] · AṄGUṢṬHA-MĀTRA in the heart [3.13]\n        Puruṣa-sūkta: thousand-headed, yet &quot;ten fingers beyond&quot; the cosmos [3.14-16]\n        shines THROUGH the senses yet sense-less; refuge &amp; FRIEND (suhṛt) [3.17]\n        the HAṂSA in the NINE-GATED city, free [3.18]\n                              |\n   ABSOLUTE + GRACE:  organless, He sees/hears/knows; the unknowable KNOWER [3.19]\n        &quot;AṆOR AṆĪYĀN MAHATO MAHĪYĀN&quot; in the heart-cave [3.20]\n        beheld by the DESIRELESS, &quot;by the GRACE of the Creator (dhātuḥ prasādāt)&quot;\n        =&gt; unaging · ancient · birthless · Self of all [3.21]</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Adhyāya 3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Affirm the One.</strong> Let \"<em>eko hi rudraḥ</em> — one God only, without a second\" (3.2) steady devotion: the same Lord creates, sustains, and withdraws, <em>and</em> dwells as your inmost Self. Worship of any name reaches the One.","2. <strong>Approach with reverence and prayer</strong> (3.5–6). The God is awesome as well as gracious; cultivate the devotional posture — ask to be shown His <strong>gracious, peace-giving form</strong>, and to be spared. <em>Bhakti</em> is part of the path, not a lower substitute.","3. <strong>Seek Him within</strong> (3.11–13). Meditate on the <strong>thumb-sized Person in the heart</strong> — the all-pervading Lord localised for contemplation; \"nearer than the nearest,\" revealed by the purified heart-and-mind.","4. <strong>See His light behind your senses</strong> (3.17). Notice that it is His consciousness by which you see and hear, though He uses no organ; behind every perception is the witnessing Lord, your <strong>refuge and Friend.</strong>","5. <strong>Still desire, and invite grace</strong> (3.20). The vision comes to the <strong>desireless (akratu), freed of sorrow</strong> — <em>and</em> \"by the grace of the Creator.\" Combine <strong>self-effort</strong> (quieting craving) with <strong>surrender</strong> (openness to grace); neither alone suffices.","6. <strong>Hold the great formula</strong> (3.8): <em>\"knowing Him alone one passes beyond death; there is no other path.\"</em> Make God-knowledge the one non-negotiable aim.","7. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the transcendent Absolute, the heart's indweller, and the gracious personal Lord are <strong>not three but one</strong> — and this Upaniṣad lets you love Him, meditate on Him, and know Him as your own Self, all at once."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 4 — The God of All Forms; Māyā; the Two Birds","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya 4","title":"The God of All Forms; Māyā; the Two Birds","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The fourth chapter celebrates the</em> <strong>one colourless God who, by His power, ordains countless forms</strong> — <em>\"Thou art woman, Thou art man\" — and gives the Upaniṣad's most decisive statements on</em> <strong>māyā</strong>: <em>the</em> <strong>one unborn \"she\" (ajā)</strong> <em>of three colours that breeds the worlds (4.5), and the great equation</em> <strong>\"know māyā as prakṛti, and the māyin (wielder) as Maheśvara\" (4.10)</strong>. <em>It sets the famous</em> <strong>two birds</strong> <em>(4.6–7) — the eating soul and the watching Lord — and rises to the</em> <strong>imageless, transcendent God</strong> <em>(\"there is no likeness of Him,\" \"the sun shines not there\"), closing with</em> <strong>Rudra-prayers</strong> <em>for protection.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Verse 4.1","title":"The colourless One who dispenses many forms","sa":["य एकोऽवर्णो बहुधा शक्तियोगाद् वरणाननेकान् निहितार्थो दधाति । विचैति चान्ते विश्वमादौ च देवः स नो बुद्ध्या शुभया संयुनक्तु ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["ya eko'varṇo bahudhā śaktiyogād varaṇān anekān nihitārtho dadhāti | vicaiti cānte viśvam ādau ca devaḥ sa no buddhyā śubhayā saṃyunaktu || 1 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ ekaḥ avarṇaḥ bahudhā śakti-yogāt","rel. + nom. (one, colourless) + adv. + abl. (by the application of His power)","the One, colourless, who by the exercise of His power, in many ways,"],["varaṇān anekān nihita-arthaḥ dadhāti","acc. pl. (many colours/forms) + nom. (with hidden purpose) + pres.","dispenses many colours/forms, with a hidden purpose;"],["vicaiti ca ante viśvam ādau ca devaḥ","pres. (dissolves) + loc. + acc. + loc.","and the God dissolves the universe at the end and (creates it) at the beginning —"],["saḥ naḥ buddhyā śubhayā saṃyunaktu","impv.","may He unite us with auspicious understanding"]],"lit":"\"The <strong>One, colourless</strong>, who by the application of His <strong>power</strong> dispenses, in manifold ways and <strong>for a hidden purpose</strong>, <strong>many colours (forms)</strong>; in whom the universe <strong>dissolves at the end and (arises) at the beginning</strong> — may that <strong>God endow us with clear understanding.</strong>\"","para":"The God is in Himself <strong>avarṇa — without colour, attribute, or distinction</strong> — yet <strong>through His power (śakti-yoga)</strong> He brings forth the <strong>manifold \"colours\" (varṇa = the diversity of forms, beings, and the social orders)</strong> \"with a hidden purpose (<em>nihitārtha</em>),\" and at the cosmic end <strong>withdraws all into Himself.</strong> The verse repeats the chapter-4 refrain-prayer: <strong>\"may He unite us with auspicious wisdom.\"</strong> (Colourless Absolute → colourful world, by His māyā-śakti — the theme the chapter unfolds.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>avarṇa</em> = without the distinctions of caste/quality (nirguṇa); by <em>śakti</em> (māyā) He projects the variegated world \"with hidden intent\" (the unsearchable purpose of creation); the recurring prayer for <em>śubhā buddhi</em> (grace)."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>varaṇān</em> = the many colours/forms/orders; the colourless One is their source and dissolver."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the one formless God as the wielder of the many-formed creation; <em>nihitārtha</em> hints at the moral order (karma) behind diversity."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the attributeless Absolute appears as the rainbow of the world through His power; pray for the auspicious intellect to see Him."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","<em>avarṇa/varaṇa</em> wordplay (colourless/colours); <em>śakti-yoga</em> — the Lord's creative power, an early Śakti reference."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 4.2–4.4","title":"He is all gods and all beings; \"Thou art woman, Thou art man\"","sa":["4.2: तदेवाग्निस्तदादित्यस्तद्वायुस्तदु चन्द्रमाः । तदेव शुक्रं तद् ब्रह्म तदापस्तत् प्रजापतिः ॥ २ ॥","4.3: त्वं स्त्री त्वं पुमानसि त्वं कुमार उत वा कुमारी । त्वं जीर्णो दण्डेन वञ्चसि त्वं जातो भवसि विश्वतोमुखः ॥ ३ ॥","4.4: नीलः पतङ्गो हरितो लोहिताक्षस्तडिद्गर्भ ऋतवः समुद्राः । अनादिमत् त्वं विभुत्वेन वर्तसे यतो जातानि भुवनानि विश्वा ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["tadevāgnis tadādityas tadvāyus tadu candramāḥ | tadeva śukraṃ tad brahma tadāpas tat prajāpatiḥ || 2 ||","tvaṃ strī tvaṃ pumān asi tvaṃ kumāra uta vā kumārī | tvaṃ jīrṇo daṇḍena vañcasi tvaṃ jāto bhavasi viśvatomukhaḥ || 3 ||","nīlaḥ pataṅgo harito lohitākṣas taḍidgarbha ṛtavaḥ samudrāḥ | anādimat tvaṃ vibhutvena vartase yato jātāni bhuvanāni viśvā || 4 ||"],"words":[["tvaṃ strī tvaṃ pumān asi","nom.","Thou art the woman, Thou art the man,"],["tvaṃ kumāraḥ uta vā kumārī","nom.","Thou art the youth, or the maiden too;"],["tvaṃ jīrṇaḥ daṇḍena vañcasi","nom. (the old man) + instr. + pres.","Thou, grown old, totterest with a staff;"],["tvaṃ jātaḥ bhavasi viśvataḥ-mukhaḥ","nom. + pres. + adj.","Thou, (ever) being born, art all-faced (turned everywhere)"]],"lit":"\"<strong>That alone is Agni, that the Sun, that the Wind, and that the Moon; that alone is the bright (Śukra/the seed/stars), that is Brahman, that is the Waters, that is Prajāpati.</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>Thou art the woman, Thou art the man; Thou art the youth and the maiden too. Thou, grown old, totterest on a staff; Thou, ever being born, facest in every direction.</strong>\"<br>\"Thou art the <strong>dark-blue bird and the green (parrot) with red eyes; Thou art (the cloud) pregnant with lightning, the seasons, the seas.</strong> Beginningless, Thou abidest by Thy all-pervadingness — Thou, <strong>from whom all the worlds are born.</strong>\"","para":"A surging hymn of the <strong>God who is all</strong>. (4.2) every cosmic power — <strong>fire, sun, wind, moon, the bright stars, Brahman, the waters, Prajāpati</strong> — is <strong>none but He.</strong> (4.3) the beloved verse <strong>\"tvaṃ strī tvaṃ pumān asi…\"</strong>: He is <strong>woman and man, boy and girl, the tottering old man, and the newborn</strong> — every gender, age, and creature is His form, and <strong>He faces all ways (viśvatomukha).</strong> (4.4) He is the <strong>blue bird, the green parrot, the lightning-bearing cloud, the seasons, the oceans</strong> — yet <strong>beginningless</strong>, present everywhere by His pervasion, <strong>the womb of all worlds.</strong> Pure immanence: there is nothing that is not He. (Contrast with the transcendence of 4.18–20 — the Upaniṣad holds both.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","all forms are the Lord's <em>vibhūtis</em> (manifestations); \"Thou art woman/man\" etc. = the one Self appearing as every limited form, while remaining the all-faced, beginningless source."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the litany of identifications expresses <em>sarvātmatva</em> (His being the Self of all); even decay and birth are His play."],["Tyāgīśānanda","4.3 a peak of immanent devotion — God in every person and stage of life; the basis of seeing the Divine in all."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","\"Thou art all\" — the meditation that dissolves the sense of the secular; everyone you meet is a form of the one God."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the pantheistic-immanent hymn (cf. AV, RV); 4.3 a celebrated verse of the all-forms deity."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 4.5","title":"The one unborn \"she\" (ajā); two unborn males (the famous goat-pun)","sa":["अजामेकां लोहितशुक्लकृष्णां बह्वीः प्रजाः सृजमानां सरूपाः । अजो ह्येको जुषमाणोऽनुशेते जहात्येनां भुक्तभोगामजोऽन्यः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["ajām ekāṃ lohitaśuklakṛṣṇāṃ bahvīḥ prajāḥ sṛjamānāṃ sarūpāḥ | ajo hyeko juṣamāṇo'nuśete jahātyenāṃ bhuktabhogām ajo'nyaḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["ajām ekāṃ lohita-śukla-kṛṣṇāṃ","f. acc. (one unborn she / one she-goat) + (red, white, black)","the one unborn (female) — red, white, and black (the three guṇas) —"],["bahvīḥ prajāḥ sṛjamānāṃ sa-rūpāḥ","f. acc. pl. + pres.part. + adj. (of like form)","bringing forth many offspring of like nature;"],["ajaḥ hi ekaḥ juṣamāṇaḥ anuśete","m. nom. (one unborn male) + pres.part. + pres.","one unborn (male), attached, lies enjoying her,"],["jahāti enāṃ bhukta-bhogām ajaḥ anyaḥ","pres. + acc. (her, her enjoyment spent) + nom.","(while) another unborn (male) leaves her, his enjoyment done"]],"lit":"\"There is <strong>one unborn 'she' (ajā)</strong> — <strong>red, white, and black</strong> — who gives birth to <strong>many creatures of her own kind</strong>. One <strong>unborn (male, aja)</strong>, attached, <strong>lies enjoying her</strong>; another <strong>unborn (male) leaves her</strong>, his enjoyment spent.\"","para":"A dense allegory using the <strong>pun on <em>ajā/aja</strong></em> (= \"unborn, fem./masc.\" <em>and</em> \"she-goat / he-goat\"). The <strong>one unborn \"she\" (ajā) = prakṛti / māyā</strong>, of <strong>three colours — red, white, black = the three guṇas</strong> (rajas, sattva, tamas; or fire, water, earth), who <strong>breeds the whole world</strong> of like-natured beings. Two <strong>unborn males (aja)</strong> are the <strong>two kinds of soul</strong>: the <strong>bound jīva</strong>, who <strong>clings to her in enjoyment (bhoga)</strong> and so transmigrates; and the <strong>liberated soul (or the Lord)</strong>, who, having <strong>finished with her enjoyments, leaves her</strong> — freed. The verse compresses the entire Sāṃkhya drama of <strong>prakṛti and puruṣa</strong> into one image, set within the Upaniṣad's theism (cf. the two birds, 4.6). (The Gītā 14.5–18 and later Sāṃkhya develop the three-coloured prakṛti.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>ajā</em> = the unborn <em>avyākta</em> prakṛti/māyā (red/white/black = rajas/sattva/tamas, or the three elements); the enjoying <em>aja</em> = the bound soul; the renouncing <em>aja</em> = the liberated soul who, knowing her, abandons her. (Śaṅkara stresses she is <em>His</em> power, not independent.)"],["Śaṅkarānanda","the three colours = the guṇas; the two males = baddha and mukta jīvas; the verse teaches discrimination (viveka) of self from prakṛti."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the celebrated <em>ajā</em> allegory — prakṛti and the two souls (bound/free); the pun \"unborn = goat\" makes it vivid and memorable."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","nature breeds endlessly; one soul is enchanted and stays, another tastes and renounces — the parting of the bound and the free."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","the famous <em>ajā</em> riddle (goat/unborn); red-white-black = the guṇas (Müller's note); a key Sāṃkhya-in-Vedānta verse."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 4.6–4.7","title":"The two birds: the eating soul and the watching Lord","sa":["4.6: द्वा सुपर्णा सयुजा सखाया समानं वृक्षं परिषस्वजाते । तयोरन्यः पिप्पलं स्वाद्वत्त्यनश्नन्नन्यो अभिचाकशीति ॥ ६ ॥","4.7: समाने वृक्षे पुरुषो निमग्नोऽनीशया शोचति मुह्यमानः । जुष्टं यदा पश्यत्यन्यमीशमस्य महिमानमिति वीतशोकः ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["dvā suparṇā sayujā sakhāyā samānaṃ vṛkṣaṃ pariṣasvajāte | tayor anyaḥ pippalaṃ svādvatty anaśnann anyo abhicākaśīti || 6 ||","samāne vṛkṣe puruṣo nimagno'nīśayā śocati muhyamānaḥ | juṣṭaṃ yadā paśyaty anyam īśam asya mahimānam iti vītaśokaḥ || 7 ||"],"words":[["dvā suparṇā sayujā sakhāyā","nom. du. (two birds, closely joined, friends)","two birds, ever-companions, fast friends,"],["samānaṃ vṛkṣaṃ pariṣasvajāte","acc. + 3 du. pf.","cling to the same tree;"],["tayoḥ anyaḥ pippalaṃ svādu atti","gen. du. + nom. + acc. (the sweet berry) + pres.","of the two, one eats the sweet berry,"],["anaśnan anyaḥ abhicākaśīti","pres.part. (not eating) + nom. + pres.","(while) the other, not eating, looks on"]],"lit":"\"<strong>Two birds, ever-joined companions, cling to the self-same tree. One of them eats the sweet berry (fruit); the other, not eating, looks on.</strong>\" <em>(= Ṛgveda 1.164.20; = Muṇḍaka 3.1.1.)</em><br>\"On the <strong>same tree</strong>, the (individual) <strong>Person, sunk (in matter), helpless (anīśā), grieves, deluded</strong>; but when he <strong>sees the other — the worshipped Lord — and His glory, then he is freed from sorrow.</strong>\" <em>(= Muṇḍaka 3.1.2.)</em>","para":"The great <strong>two-birds simile</strong> (shared with the Ṛgveda and the Muṇḍaka). On the <strong>one tree (the body / the field of nature)</strong> perch <strong>two birds, inseparable friends</strong>: the <strong>jīva (individual soul)</strong>, who <strong>eats the fruits (the sweet and bitter of karma/experience)</strong>, and the <strong>Īśvara/Self (or the witnessing Self)</strong>, who <strong>eats nothing but only watches.</strong> v. 7 draws the moral: as long as the soul, <strong>\"sunk and helpless (anīśā),\"</strong> identifies with eating, it <strong>grieves in delusion</strong>; but <strong>when it beholds the other bird — the adored Lord — and recognises His glory (and its own true nature), it is freed from sorrow.</strong> Liberation = turning from the <strong>eater</strong> to the <strong>watcher</strong>, from the helpless self to the Lord who is its real Self. (One of the supreme images of Indian spirituality — bondage as identification with experience, freedom as recognition of the witnessing Lord.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the eating bird = the <em>jīva</em> (the reflected/conditioned self, doer-enjoyer); the watching bird = the supreme Self/Īśvara; on realising the witness as one's own glory, grief ends (the two are ultimately one)."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>anīśā</em> = the soul's sense of helplessness in bondage; <em>juṣṭam</em> = the worshipped/served Lord; seeing Him, <em>vītaśoka</em>."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the classic allegory of soul and God on the tree of life; freedom by devoted vision of the Lord (\"juṣṭa\") — knowledge and bhakti together."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","stop being the bird that eats (the worried experiencer); become the bird that watches (the serene witness/Lord) — the whole of yoga in one image."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","4.6–7 = RV 1.164.20 / Muṇḍaka 3.1.1–2; among the most quoted Vedic similes; note the shared transmission."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 4.8–4.10","title":"The Imperishable in which the gods rest; the Māyin who is Maheśvara","sa":["4.8: ऋचो अक्षरे परमे व्योमन् यस्मिन्देवा अधि विश्वे निषेदुः । यस्तं न वेद किमृचा करिष्यति य इत्तद्विदुस्त इमे समासते ॥ ८ ॥","4.9: छन्दांसि यज्ञाः क्रतवो व्रतानि भूतं भव्यं यच्च वेदा वदन्ति । अस्मान् मायी सृजते विश्वमेतत्तस्मिंश्चान्यो मायया संनिरुद्धः ॥ ९ ॥","4.10: मायां तु प्रकृतिं विद्यान्मायिनं च महेश्वरम् । तस्यवयवभूतैस्तु व्याप्तं सर्वमिदं जगत् ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["ṛco akṣare parame vyoman yasmin devā adhi viśve niṣeduḥ | yastaṃ na veda kimṛcā kariṣyati ya it tad vidus ta ime samāsate || 8 ||","chandāṃsi yajñāḥ kratavo vratāni bhūtaṃ bhavyaṃ yacca vedā vadanti | asmān māyī sṛjate viśvam etat tasmiṃś cānyo māyayā sanniruddhaḥ || 9 ||","māyāṃ tu prakṛtiṃ vidyān māyinaṃ ca maheśvaram | tasyāvayavabhūtais tu vyāptaṃ sarvam idaṃ jagat || 10 ||"],"words":[["māyāṃ tu prakṛtiṃ vidyāt","f. acc. + acc. + opt.","one should know māyā as prakṛti (primal nature),"],["māyinaṃ ca maheśvaram","acc. (the wielder of māyā) + acc.","and the wielder of māyā (the māyin) as Maheśvara (the Great Lord);"],["tasya avayava-bhūtaiḥ tu vyāptaṃ","gen. + instr. pl. (by His parts/portions) + pp.","by His parts (the beings/forms that are His portions), pervaded,"],["sarvam idaṃ jagat","n. nom.","is this whole world"]],"lit":"\"The <strong>verses (ṛcas) abide in the supreme, imperishable (akṣara) heaven (vyoman), in which all the gods are seated.</strong> <strong>He who knows not That, what will he do with the verse? But those who know it — they sit (here, fulfilled).</strong>\" <em>(= Ṛgveda 1.164.39.)</em><br>\"The <strong>meters, the sacrifices, the rites, the vows, the past, the future, and what the Vedas declare</strong> — out of this (Brahman) the <strong>Māyin (the wielder of māyā) projects this whole world; and in it the other (the soul) is bound by māyā.</strong>\"<br>\"Know <strong>māyā to be prakṛti (primal nature), and the wielder of māyā (the māyin) to be Maheśvara (the Great Lord). By the beings that are His parts, this whole world is pervaded.</strong>\"","para":"The Upaniṣad's <strong>classic statement of māyā-theism</strong>. (4.8) the <strong>ṛk-mantras and the gods themselves rest in the supreme imperishable (akṣara)</strong>; mere recitation without knowing That is fruitless — <em>knowledge</em> is the point. (4.9) from Brahman the <strong>Māyin (the Lord as magician/wielder of power) projects the entire world</strong> — all scriptures, rites, time, and beings — <strong>while \"the other\" (the individual soul) is bound by that same māyā.</strong> (4.10) the decisive equation: <strong>\"know māyā as prakṛti, and the māyin as Maheśvara\"</strong> — i.e., the <strong>creative Power (māyā) = Sāṃkhya's prakṛti</strong>, and <strong>its Wielder = the Great Lord (Śiva)</strong>; and <strong>the whole world is pervaded by the beings that are His parts.</strong> This single verse fuses <strong>Vedānta (māyā), Sāṃkhya (prakṛti), and Śaiva theism (Maheśvara)</strong> — the Upaniṣad's signature synthesis, and the seed of later Advaita's <em>māyā</em> and of Śaiva metaphysics alike.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>māyā = prakṛti =</em> the Lord's beginningless power of projection (not an independent reality); <em>māyin = Maheśvara/Īśvara</em> wielding it; the world is His appearance. The bound \"other\" = the jīva under māyā; release is knowing the Māyin."],["Śaṅkarānanda","māyā is the material cause (prakṛti), the Lord its controller; \"His parts\" = the souls/forms pervading the world."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the locus classicus equating māyā and prakṛti, and identifying the personal Lord as their master — the bridge from Sāṃkhya dualism to theistic non-dualism."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the world is the Lord's magic-show; know the Magician (Maheśvara) behind the magic (māyā/prakṛti) and be free; the soul is \"caught in the show\" until then."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","4.10 the earliest explicit <em>māyā = prakṛti</em> / <em>māyin = Maheśvara</em>; a pivotal verse for the history of the māyā-doctrine and of Śaivism; 4.8 = RV 1.164.39."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 4.11–4.14","title":"The one Lord over all wombs; the supreme subtle Creator","sa":["4.11: यो योनिं योनिमधितिष्ठत्येको यस्मिन्निदं सं च विचैति सर्वम् । तमीशानं वरदं देवमीड्यं निचाय्येमां शान्तिमत्यन्तमेति ॥ ११ ॥","4.12: यो देवानां प्रभवश्चोद्भवश्च विश्वाधिपो रुद्रो महर्षिः । हिरण्यगर्भं पश्यत जायमानं स नो बुद्ध्या शुभया संयुनक्तु ॥ १२ ॥","4.13: यो देवानामधिपो यस्मिन्ल्लोका अधिश्रिताः । य ईशे अस्य द्विपदश्चतुष्पदः कस्मै देवाय हविषा विधेम ॥ १३ ॥","4.14: सूक्ष्मातिसूक्ष्मं कलिलस्य मध्ये विश्वस्य स्रष्ठारमनेकरूपम् । विश्वस्यैकं परिवेष्टितारं ज्ञात्वा शिवं शान्तिमत्यन्तमेति ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["yo yoniṃ yonim adhitiṣṭhaty eko yasminn idaṃ saṃ ca vicaiti sarvam | tam īśānaṃ varadaṃ devam īḍyaṃ nicāyyemāṃ śāntim atyantam eti || 11 ||","yo devānāṃ prabhavaścodbhavaśca viśvādhipo rudro maharṣiḥ | hiraṇyagarbhaṃ paśyata jāyamānaṃ sa no buddhyā śubhayā saṃyunaktu || 12 ||","yo devānām adhipo yasminllokā adhiśritāḥ | ya īśe asya dvipadaścatuṣpadaḥ kasmai devāya haviṣā vidhema || 13 ||","sūkṣmātisūkṣmaṃ kalilasya madhye viśvasya sraṣṭhāram anekarūpam | viśvasyaikaṃ pariveṣṭitāraṃ jñātvā śivaṃ śāntim atyantam eti || 14 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"<strong>(4.11)</strong> The <strong>One who governs every single womb (source)</strong>, in whom this all comes together and dissolves — <strong>beholding that Lord, the boon-giver, the adorable God, one attains peace, the absolute peace.</strong> <strong>(4.12)</strong> He who is the <strong>source and origin of the gods, the ruler of all, Rudra the great seer, who beheld Hiraṇyagarbha being born</strong> — may He <strong>endow us with clear understanding.</strong> <strong>(4.13)</strong> He who is the <strong>Lord of the gods, in whom the worlds rest, who rules over the two-footed and the four-footed</strong> — to <strong>what God shall we offer our oblation?</strong> (= Him alone, the <em>Kasmai devāya</em> of RV 10.121, Hiraṇyagarbha-sūkta). <strong>(4.14)</strong> Knowing the <strong>subtler-than-the-subtle, in the midst of the world's confusion (kalila), the Creator of all, of many forms, the One who envelops the universe — the Auspicious (Śiva) — one attains absolute peace.</strong>\"","para":"Four verses extolling the <strong>one Lord as the sole worthy object of worship and the giver of supreme peace</strong>: (4.11) He governs <strong>every womb/source</strong> of beings, in whom all arises and dissolves — <strong>beholding Him, the boon-giving adorable God, one wins absolute peace (atyanta śānti).</strong> (4.12) repeats 3.4 with a variation — Rudra, source of the gods, who <strong>watched Hiraṇyagarbha being born</strong> — with the grace-prayer. (4.13) borrows the <strong>Hiraṇyagarbha-sūkta's refrain \"kasmai devāya haviṣā vidhema?\" (\"to what God shall we offer?\")</strong> — answered by this Upaniṣad: <strong>to the one Rudra/Īśa.</strong> (4.14) <strong>knowing Śiva</strong> — subtler than the subtle, the many-formed Creator amid the world's \"confusion,\" the One enveloping all — yields <strong>absolute peace.</strong> The keynote: <strong>vision of the one Lord = atyanta śānti (perfect, final peace).</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Lord governs all <em>yonis</em> (sources/species); knowing Him = supreme peace; 4.13's <em>kasmai devāya</em> answered — the supreme Self/Lord; <em>kalila</em> = the dense confusion of saṃsāra, in whose midst He is the subtle creator."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>atyanta śānti</em> = liberation; <em>Śiva</em> = the auspicious Absolute; the boon-giver (varada) invites devotion."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the Upaniṣad claims the Ṛgvedic \"unknown God\" (RV 10.121) as its own known Lord; peace is the fruit of His vision."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","behold the one boon-giving Lord behind all births and you reach the peace that passes all — the goal of every prayer."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","4.13 incorporates RV 10.121 (Hiraṇyagarbha/Ka); the chapter answers the Veda's open question with its theism."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 4.15–4.17","title":"The hidden Protector; subtler than the cream of ghee; the Friend in the heart","sa":["4.15: स एव काले भुवनस्य गोप्ता विश्वाधिपः सर्वभूतेषु गूढः । यस्मिन् युक्ता ब्रह्मर्षयो देवताश्च तमेवं ज्ञात्वा मृत्युपाशांश्छिनत्ति ॥ १५ ॥","4.16: घृतात् परं मण्डमिवातिसूक्ष्मं ज्ञात्वा शिवं सर्वभूतेषु गूढम् । विश्वस्यैकं परिवेष्टितारं ज्ञात्वा देवं मुच्यते सर्वपाशैः ॥ १६ ॥","4.17: एष देवो विश्वकर्मा महात्मा सदा जनानां हृदये संनिविष्टः । हृदा मनीषा मनसाभिक्लृप्तो य एतद् विदुरमृतास्ते भवन्ति ॥ १७ ॥"],"iast":["sa eva kāle bhuvanasya goptā viśvādhipaḥ sarvabhūteṣu gūḍhaḥ | yasmin yuktā brahmarṣayo devatāśca tam evaṃ jñātvā mṛtyupāśāṃśchinatti || 15 ||","ghṛtāt paraṃ maṇḍam ivātisūkṣmaṃ jñātvā śivaṃ sarvabhūteṣu gūḍham | viśvasyaikaṃ pariveṣṭitāraṃ jñātvā devaṃ mucyate sarvapāśaiḥ || 16 ||","eṣa devo viśvakarmā mahātmā sadā janānāṃ hṛdaye sanniviṣṭaḥ | hṛdā manīṣā manasābhiklṛpto ya etad vidur amṛtās te bhavanti || 17 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"<strong>(4.15)</strong> He alone, in time, is the <strong>Protector of the world, the ruler of all, hidden in all beings</strong>, in whom the <strong>Brahmā-seers and the gods are united</strong>; <strong>knowing Him thus, one cuts the bonds of death.</strong> <strong>(4.16)</strong> Knowing the <strong>Auspicious (Śiva), hidden in all beings, subtler than even the finest essence (the 'cream' above ghee), the One who envelops the universe — knowing this God, one is freed from all bonds.</strong> <strong>(4.17)</strong> This <strong>God, the All-maker (Viśvakarmā), the great Self, ever seated in the heart of all</strong>, is <strong>revealed by the heart, the intellect, and the mind</strong> — those who know this <strong>become immortal.</strong>\"","para":"Three verses on the <strong>God hidden within all, whose knowledge cuts the bonds of death.</strong> (4.15) He is the <strong>world's Protector, ruler of all, concealed in every being</strong> — knowing Him <strong>severs death's noose (mṛtyu-pāśa).</strong> (4.16) <strong>subtler than the subtlest</strong> — \"<strong>finer than the cream that rises above ghee</strong>\" — the <strong>auspicious Śiva hidden in all</strong>; knowing Him, <strong>freed from all bonds</strong> (the refrain <em>mucyate sarvapāśaiḥ</em>, cf. 1.8, 2.15). (4.17) the <strong>All-maker (Viśvakarmā), the great Self, ever enthroned in the heart</strong>, revealed by the <strong>purified heart-mind</strong> (echoing 3.13) — knowing whom, one is <strong>immortal.</strong> Transcendent-yet-indwelling, supremely subtle, the heart's own God — known, He liberates.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"hidden in all beings\" yet the ruler; <em>mṛtyu-pāśa-chid</em> = knowledge cutting the death-bond; \"subtler than ghee's cream\" = the extreme subtlety of the Self; revealed in the heart by purified cognition."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>viśvakarmā</em> = the maker of all; the heart is the place of His revelation; the refrains of release."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the immanent Lord, knowable in the heart, freer from death — uniting theism with the heart-meditation of the older Upaniṣads."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","God is subtler than the subtlest and yet in your own heart; meditation reveals Him, and the chains fall."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>viśvakarmā</em> (the Vedic All-maker) identified with the heart-Self; the \"cream above ghee\" simile for subtlety."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 4.18–4.20","title":"Beyond day and night; no likeness of Him; unseen by the eye","sa":["4.18: यदाऽतमस्तान्न दिवा न रात्रिः न सन्नचासच्छिव एव केवलः । तदक्षरं तत् सवितुर्वरेण्यं प्रज्ञा च तस्मात् प्रसृता पुराणी ॥ १८ ॥","4.19: नैनमूर्ध्वं न तिर्यञ्चं न मध्ये न परिजग्रभत् । न तस्य प्रतिमा अस्ति यस्य नाम महद् यशः ॥ १९ ॥","4.20: न संदृशे तिष्ठति रूपमस्य न चक्षुषा पश्यति कश्चनैनम् । हृदा हृदिस्थं मनसा य एनमेवं विदुरमृतास्ते भवन्ति ॥ २० ॥"],"iast":["yadā'tamas tān na divā na rātriḥ na sannacāsacchiva eva kevalaḥ | tadakṣaraṃ tat saviturvareṇyaṃ prajñā ca tasmāt prasṛtā purāṇī || 18 ||","nainam ūrdhvaṃ na tiryañcaṃ na madhye na parijagrabhat | na tasya pratimā asti yasya nāma mahad yaśaḥ || 19 ||","na sandṛśe tiṣṭhati rūpam asya na cakṣuṣā paśyati kaścanainam | hṛdā hṛdisthaṃ manasā ya enam evaṃ vidur amṛtās te bhavanti || 20 ||"],"words":[["na enaṃ ūrdhvaṃ na tiryañcaṃ na madhye","acc. + adv. (above, across, in the middle)","Him none has grasped — not above, not across, not in the middle;"],["na parijagrabhat","3 sg. pf. of pari-grah","none has comprehended (Him);"],["na tasya pratimā asti","gen. + f. nom. (likeness, image) + pres.","there is no likeness of Him,"],["yasya nāma mahat yaśaḥ","gen. + n. nom.","whose name is \"the Great Glory\""]],"lit":"\"When there is <strong>no darkness, then there is neither day nor night, neither being nor non-being — the Auspicious (Śiva) alone (is).</strong> That is the <strong>Imperishable, that the adorable (light) of Savitṛ; and from it the ancient Wisdom (prajñā) streamed forth.</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>Him none has grasped — not from above, across, or in the middle. There is no likeness (pratimā) of Him whose name is 'the Great Glory.'</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>His form does not stand within sight; no one sees Him with the eye. Those who know Him — set in the heart, (grasped) by the heart and the mind — become immortal.</strong>\" <em>(= Kaṭha 2.3.9.)</em>","para":"The chapter's <strong>apophatic summit</strong>, balancing the immanence of 4.2–4. (4.18) In the state beyond ignorance (\"no darkness\"), the ordinary distinctions — <strong>day/night, being/non-being</strong> — fall away; <strong>Śiva alone remains, the Imperishable, the adorable Light</strong> (identified with the <em>vareṇya</em> light of the Gāyatrī's Savitṛ), the source of <strong>primal Wisdom.</strong> (4.19) <strong>He has no image, no likeness (na tasya pratimā asti)</strong> — uncomprehended from any direction — <strong>\"whose name is the Great Glory\"</strong>: a celebrated verse against any adequate representation of the Supreme. (4.20) <strong>no eye can see His form</strong>; He is known only <strong>within the heart, by the purified heart-and-mind</strong> — and that knowledge is <strong>immortality.</strong> The God who is <em>all forms</em> (4.3) is in Himself <strong>beyond all form and likeness</strong> — seen not with the eye but in the heart.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","4.18 = the <em>nirguṇa</em> state where all dualities vanish, only the Auspicious; the <em>akṣara</em> and the Gāyatrī's <em>vareṇya</em> light; 4.19 — no <em>pratimā</em> (image/equal) — the Absolute beyond comparison; 4.20 — known by the heart, not the eye."],["Śaṅkarānanda","\"no likeness\" = neither material image nor any equal; the heart-vision (not sense-perception) reveals Him."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the imageless God balances the all-forms God; <em>na tasya pratimā asti</em> a key text on the formless Absolute (and, ironically, often cited in image-worship debates)."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","God has every form and yet no form to which He can be reduced; seek Him not outside with the eye but within, with the heart."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","4.20 = Kaṭha 6.9; <em>na tasya pratimā asti</em> noted as a strong statement of the Absolute's incomparability/imagelessness."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 4.21–4.22","title":"Refuge in Rudra; the Vedic prayer for protection","sa":["4.21: अजात इत्येवं कश्चिद्भीरुः प्रपद्यते । रुद्र यत्ते दक्षिणं मुखं तेन मां पाहि नित्यम् ॥ २१ ॥","4.22: मा नस्तोके तनये मा न आयुषि मा नो गोषु मा न अश्वेषु रीरिषः । वीरान् मा नो रुद्र भामितो वधीर्हविष्मन्तः सदामित् त्वा हवामहे ॥ २२ ॥"],"iast":["ajāta ityevaṃ kaścidbhīruḥ prapadyate | rudra yatte dakṣiṇaṃ mukhaṃ tena māṃ pāhi nityam || 21 ||","mā nastoke tanaye mā na āyuṣi mā no goṣu mā na aśveṣu rīriṣaḥ | vīrān mā no rudra bhāmito vadhīr haviṣmantaḥ sadāmit tvā havāmahe || 22 ||"],"words":[["ajātaḥ iti evaṃ kaścit bhīruḥ prapadyate","nom. (the unborn) + nom. (some fearful one) + pres. of pra-pad","\"(He is) the Unborn\" — thus some fearful (devotee) takes refuge (in Him);"],["rudra yat te dakṣiṇaṃ mukhaṃ","voc. + rel. + n. (Thy right/gracious face)","O Rudra, that gracious (right) face of Thine,"],["tena māṃ pāhi nityam","instr. + acc. + impv. + adv.","with that protect me always"]],"lit":"\"<strong>'He is the Unborn (ajāta)!' — thus some fearful one takes refuge (in Him). O Rudra, that gracious face of Thine — with that, protect me ever.</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>Injure not our children and descendants, nor our life, nor our cattle, nor our horses; slay not our heroes, O Rudra, in Thy wrath. With oblations we ever call upon Thee.</strong>\" <em>(= Ṛgveda 1.114.8 / Śatarudrīya.)</em>","para":"The chapter closes, as it touched at 3.5–6, on <strong>refuge and prayer (prapatti / śaraṇāgati)</strong>. (4.21) the <strong>fearful soul takes refuge</strong> in the <strong>Unborn Lord</strong>, begging Rudra's <strong>gracious face (dakṣiṇa mukha — His benign aspect)</strong> for <strong>eternal protection.</strong> (4.22) a famous <strong>Vedic prayer (from the Rudra-hymn)</strong> for the <strong>safety of children, life, cattle, horses, and heroes</strong> — that Rudra, in His terrible aspect, spare them. The lofty metaphysics of māyā and the imageless Absolute thus comes to rest in <strong>humble, trusting petition</strong> — the Upaniṣad's characteristic union of <strong>the highest jñāna with heartfelt bhakti and surrender.</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the seeker, awed, takes refuge in the Lord's benign aspect; the Vedic protection-prayer expresses the devotee's dependence even amid knowledge."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>dakṣiṇaṃ mukham</em> = the auspicious, grace-bestowing face (vs. the terrible); <em>prapadyate</em> = surrenders."],["Tyāgīśānanda","notes the deliberate close on <em>prapatti</em> (self-surrender) and the Vedic Rudra-prayer — bhakti framing the chapter."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","even the knower bows and prays for protection; surrender (<em>prapatti</em>) crowns knowledge."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","4.22 = RV 1.114.8 (the great Rudra protection-mantra, still recited); the chapter's Śaiva-devotional seal."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Adhyāya 4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The fourth chapter holds together, with unusual boldness, the <strong>two faces of the Divine — utter immanence and utter transcendence</strong> — and gives the Upaniṣad's defining <strong>doctrine of māyā</strong>:","1. <strong>Immanence: the God of all forms (4.2–4).</strong> He is <strong>fire, sun, wind, moon, Brahman, the waters, Prajāpati</strong>; <strong>\"Thou art woman, Thou art man, the youth, the maiden, the old man, the newborn\"</strong>; the blue bird, the green parrot, the lightning-cloud, the seasons, the seas — <strong>everything is He.</strong>","2. <strong>The Sāṃkhya allegories (4.5–7).</strong> The <strong>one unborn ajā (prakṛti)</strong> of <strong>three colours (the guṇas)</strong> breeding all creatures, with the <strong>two unborn males</strong> (bound and free souls); and the immortal <strong>two birds</strong> — the <strong>eating soul</strong> and the <strong>watching Lord</strong> — freedom coming when the soul beholds the Lord as its glory.","3. <strong>The māyā-doctrine (4.8–10).</strong> Out of the <strong>imperishable</strong> Brahman the <strong>Māyin projects the world</strong>; <strong>\"know māyā as prakṛti, and the māyin as Maheśvara\"</strong> — the world pervaded by the beings that are His parts. This fuses <strong>Vedānta, Sāṃkhya, and Śaiva theism.</strong>","4. <strong>The one worshipful Lord and supreme peace (4.11–17).</strong> Ruler of every womb; the answer to the Veda's <em>\"to what God?\"</em>; subtler than the cream of ghee; the <strong>All-maker in the heart</strong> — knowing whom, one <strong>cuts death's bonds</strong> and attains <strong>absolute peace.</strong>","5. <strong>Transcendence: the imageless God (4.18–20).</strong> Beyond day and night, being and non-being; <strong>\"there is no likeness of Him whose name is the Great Glory\"</strong>; unseen by the eye, known only <strong>in the heart.</strong>","6. <strong>Refuge (4.21–22).</strong> The seeker <strong>takes refuge</strong> in Rudra's gracious face and prays the old Vedic prayer for protection — <strong>bhakti and prapatti</strong> sealing the chapter.","Its distinctive achievement: the <strong>same God is all forms and beyond all form</strong>, <strong>wields māyā/prakṛti as Maheśvara</strong>, and is approached by <strong>knowledge, meditation, devotion, and surrender</strong> at once."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Adhyāya 4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE GOD OF ALL FORMS &amp; MĀYĀ (Adhyāya 4)\n   =======================================\n   IMMANENT (4.2-4):  He is Agni·Sun·Wind·Moon·Brahman·Waters·Prajāpati\n        &quot;TVAṂ STRĪ TVAṂ PUMĀN&quot; — woman·man·youth·maiden·old man·newborn · all birds·seasons·seas\n                              |\n   SĀṂKHYA ALLEGORIES:\n        AJĀ (one unborn &quot;she&quot; = PRAKṚTI), 3 colours = the GUṆAS (red·white·black), breeds all [4.5]\n        + two unborn males: the BOUND soul (enjoys her) &amp; the FREE soul (leaves her)\n        TWO BIRDS [4.6-7]: one EATS the berry (jīva, grieving) | one WATCHES (Lord)\n            -&gt; see the Lord&#x27;s glory -&gt; VĪTAŚOKA (freed of sorrow)\n                              |\n   MĀYĀ-DOCTRINE [4.8-10]:  the MĀYIN projects the world; &quot;the other&quot; bound by māyā\n        ====&gt;  &quot;MĀYĀṂ TU PRAKṚTIṂ VIDYĀT, MĀYINAṂ CA MAHEŚVARAM&quot;\n               (māyā = prakṛti ; its wielder = the Great Lord) — Vedānta + Sāṃkhya + Śaiva\n                              |\n   THE ONE LORD -&gt; ATYANTA ŚĀNTI (absolute peace) [4.11-17]:\n        ruler of every womb · &quot;to what God?&quot; = HIM · subtler than ghee&#x27;s cream · ALL-MAKER in the heart\n        -&gt; cuts death&#x27;s bonds · &quot;freed from all bonds&quot;\n                              |\n   TRANSCENDENT (4.18-20): beyond day/night, being/non-being -&gt; ŚIVA ALONE\n        &quot;NA TASYA PRATIMĀ ASTI&quot; (no likeness of Him, the Great Glory) · unseen by eye, known in the HEART\n                              |\n   REFUGE (4.21-22): take refuge in Rudra&#x27;s GRACIOUS FACE; &quot;harm not our children...&quot; (prapatti)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Adhyāya 4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>See God in every form</strong> (4.3). Practise the <em>\"tvaṃ strī tvaṃ pumān\"</em> vision: in every person — man, woman, child, the aged — and every creature, recognise the one God wearing that form. It transfigures ordinary encounters into worship.","2. <strong>Be the watching bird</strong> (4.6–7). When swept up in the sweet-and-bitter \"berries\" of experience, remember the <strong>two birds</strong>: shift identity from the <strong>eating, grieving soul</strong> to the <strong>serene, watching Lord</strong> — and \"behold His glory.\" Sorrow ends in that turn of attention.","3. <strong>Know the Magician behind the magic</strong> (4.10). Meet the dazzling, binding world as <strong>māyā = prakṛti</strong>, and look past it to the <strong>Māyin = Maheśvara</strong> who wields it. Don't fight the show; know its Master.","4. <strong>Discriminate self from nature</strong> (4.5). Watch how <em>prakṛti</em> (the three-coloured ajā) ceaselessly breeds forms and moods; be the soul that, having tasted, <strong>lets go</strong> — not the one who clings and is whirled.","5. <strong>Worship the imageless in the heart</strong> (4.19–20). Hold both truths: God has every form (for love and worship) <em>and</em> <strong>no likeness</strong> (for the highest knowledge). Seek Him finally <strong>not with the eye but in the heart</strong>, \"whose name is the Great Glory.\"","6. <strong>Take refuge</strong> (4.21–22). Let knowledge flower into <strong>surrender (prapatti)</strong>: ask for the Lord's <strong>gracious face</strong> and His protection over all you love. The highest wisdom is not too proud to pray.","7. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the one God is <strong>all forms and beyond all form, the wielder of māyā, the Friend in the heart</strong> — to be <strong>known, meditated on, loved, and surrendered to.</strong> Vision of Him is <em>atyanta śānti</em> — the peace beyond all peace."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 5 — Knowledge and Ignorance; the Lord and the Individual Soul","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya 5","title":"Knowledge and Ignorance; the Lord and the Individual Soul","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The fifth chapter sets the</em> <strong>two imperishables hidden in Brahman — vidyā (knowledge) and avidyā (ignorance)</strong> — <em>and the</em> <strong>third, the Lord who rules both</strong>. <em>It depicts the one God presiding over all wombs and natures (and \"upholding the seer Kapila,\" a much-discussed reference), then turns to the</em> <strong>individual soul (jīva)</strong>: <em>thumb-sized yet sun-like, the \"hundredth part of a hair-tip\" yet \"fit for infinitude,\" neither male nor female, assuming body after body by its own karma and guṇas — until, knowing the</em> <strong>one Auspicious God who makes the parts (kalā)</strong>, <em>it casts off the body for good.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Verse 5.1","title":"Two imperishables: knowledge and ignorance; and the Third who rules both","sa":["द्वे अक्षरे ब्रह्मपरे त्वनन्ते विद्याविद्ये निहिते यत्र गूढे । क्षरं त्वविद्या ह्यमृतं तु विद्या विद्याविद्ये ईशते यस्तु सोऽन्यः ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["dve akṣare brahmapare tvanante vidyāvidye nihite yatra gūḍhe | kṣaraṃ tvavidyā hyamṛtaṃ tu vidyā vidyāvidye īśate yastu so'nyaḥ || 1 ||"],"words":[["dve akṣare brahma-pare tu anante","n. nom. du. (two imperishables) + (in the supreme Brahman) + (endless)","in the supreme, endless Brahman two imperishables (are hidden) —"],["vidyā-avidye nihite yatra gūḍhe","f. nom. du. (knowledge & ignorance) + pp. du. (placed, hidden)","knowledge and ignorance, set there concealed;"],["kṣaraṃ tu avidyā hi amṛtaṃ tu vidyā","n. nom.","the perishable is ignorance; the immortal is knowledge;"],["vidyā-avidye īśate yaḥ tu saḥ anyaḥ","acc. du. + pres. + rel. + nom.","but He who rules both knowledge and ignorance is another (a third, distinct from both)"]],"lit":"\"In the supreme, infinite <strong>Brahman</strong> lie hidden <strong>two imperishables — knowledge and ignorance (vidyā and avidyā).</strong> The <strong>perishable (kṣara) is ignorance</strong>; the <strong>immortal (amṛta) is knowledge.</strong> But <strong>He who rules over both knowledge and ignorance is yet another (the Third).</strong>\"","para":"Within Brahman are <strong>two beginningless \"imperishables\"</strong>: <strong>avidyā</strong> (ignorance — the cause of the <em>perishable</em> world and bondage) and <strong>vidyā</strong> (knowledge — that which yields <em>immortality</em>). But above and <strong>ruling both</strong> is a <strong>Third — the Lord (Īśa)</strong> — distinct from both ignorance and (relative) knowledge. This refines the māyā-doctrine of ch. 4: the Lord is master of both the binding power (avidyā) and the liberating power (vidyā); He is not caught in either. (The verse is a touchstone for the later Advaitic analysis of <em>māyā/avidyā</em> and Īśvara.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>avidyā</em> = the cause of saṃsāra (perishable); <em>vidyā</em> = its remover (immortality); the <strong>Third (anya)</strong> = Īśvara, the Lord untouched by both, their controller — ultimately the one Brahman."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the two are His powers (concealing and revealing); He who wields them is supreme."],["Tyāgīśānanda","a precise statement of the Lord's transcendence over both ignorance and knowledge — the master of māyā in both its modes."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","bondage (avidyā) and liberation (vidyā) are both within Brahman's economy; God presides over both — surrender to the Third."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","key verse for the vidyā/avidyā pair and Īśvara as their ruler (cf. Īśā 9–11, in counterpoint)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 5.2–5.3","title":"The one God over all wombs; upholding Kapila; creating and withdrawing","sa":["5.2: यो योनिं योनिमधितिष्ठत्येको विश्वानि रूपाणि योनीश्च सर्वाः । ऋषिं प्रसूतं कपिलं यस्तमग्रे ज्ञानैर्बिभर्ति जायमानं च पश्येत् ॥ २ ॥","5.3: एकैक जालं बहुधा विकुर्वन्नस्मिन् क्षेत्रे संहरत्येष देवः । भूयः सृष्ट्वा पतयस्तथेशः सर्वाधिपत्यं कुरुते महात्मा ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["yo yoniṃ yonim adhitiṣṭhaty eko viśvāni rūpāṇi yonīśca sarvāḥ | ṛṣiṃ prasūtaṃ kapilaṃ yastam agre jñānair bibharti jāyamānaṃ ca paśyet || 2 ||","ekaika jālaṃ bahudhā vikurvann asmin kṣetre saṃharatyeṣa devaḥ | bhūyaḥ sṛṣṭvā patayas tatheśaḥ sarvādhipatyaṃ kurute mahātmā || 3 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ yoniṃ yonim adhitiṣṭhati ekaḥ","rel. + acc. (each and every womb) + pres. + nom.","the One who presides over each and every womb (source),"],["viśvāni rūpāṇi yonīḥ ca sarvāḥ","acc. pl.","(and over) all forms and all wombs;"],["ṛṣiṃ prasūtaṃ kapilaṃ yaḥ tam agre jñānaiḥ bibharti","acc. (the seer Kapila, brought forth) + rel. + acc. + adv. + instr. + pres.","who bore (nourished) with knowledge the seer Kapila, born in the beginning,"],["jāyamānaṃ ca paśyet","pres.part. + opt.","and beheld him as he was being born"]],"lit":"\"The <strong>One who presides over every womb, over all forms and all sources</strong>, who in the beginning <strong>bore (sustained) with (all) knowledge the born seer Kapila, and beheld him as he came to birth</strong> — (He is the Lord).\"<br>\"Spreading out <strong>each single net in manifold ways</strong> in this field (the world), and <strong>withdrawing it</strong>, this <strong>God</strong>, having again <strong>created the lords (patis)</strong>, the <strong>Ruler, the great Self, exercises His universal sovereignty.</strong>\"","para":"v. 2: the one Lord governs <strong>every yoni</strong> (source/species) and <strong>all forms</strong>; and — in a famous and much-debated half-verse — He <strong>\"bore/nourished with knowledge the seer Kapila, born of old, and watched over his birth.\"</strong> (<strong>Kapila</strong> = traditionally the founder of Sāṃkhya, here a <em>creature</em> the Lord sustains — the Upaniṣad subordinating Sāṃkhya's sage to its God; some read <em>kapila</em> as \"tawny/golden,\" i.e. Hiraṇyagarbha.) v. 3: the God <strong>spreads out the \"net\" (of creation) in countless ways and withdraws it</strong>, repeatedly <strong>creating the cosmic rulers</strong>, exercising <strong>total sovereignty</strong> as the <strong>Great Self.</strong> Creator, sustainer, withdrawer — and the source even of the sages and lords.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Lord over all wombs; \"Kapila\" he glosses as <strong>Hiraṇyagarbha</strong> (the golden/tawny first-born) whom the Lord sustains \"with knowledge\" — i.e., the Lord is prior even to the first seer; the net = creation spread and withdrawn."],["Śaṅkarānanda","notes the two readings of <em>kapila</em> (the sage / the golden Hiraṇyagarbha); either way subordinate to the Lord."],["Tyāgīśānanda","discusses the Kapila reference — the Upaniṣad acknowledges Sāṃkhya yet places its founder under the supreme God; the Lord's repeated creation/withdrawal."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","God presides over every birth and even over the great sages; He spins and withdraws the cosmic net at will."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","5.2 the celebrated <em>kapila</em> verse — pivotal for dating Sāṃkhya and for the text's theistic absorption of it; Müller discusses sage-vs-colour readings."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 5.4–5.6","title":"The radiant Lord over all natures; hidden in the Vedānta; known by the gods of old","sa":["5.4: सर्वा दिश ऊर्ध्वमधश्च तिर्यक् प्रकाशयन् भ्राजते यद्वनड्वान् । एवं स देवो भगवान् वरेण्यो योनिस्वभावानधितिष्ठत्येकः ॥ ४ ॥","5.5: यच्च स्वभावं पचति विश्वयोनिः पाच्यांश्च सर्वान् परिणामयेद् यः । सर्वमेतद् विश्वमधितिष्ठत्येको गुणांश्च सर्वान् विनियोजयेद् यः ॥ ५ ॥","5.6: तद् वेदगुह्योपनिषत्सु गूढं तद् ब्रह्मा वेदते ब्रह्मयोनिम् । ये पूर्वं देवा ऋषयश्च तद् विदुस्ते तन्मया अमृता वै बभूवुः ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["sarvā diśa ūrdhvam adhaśca tiryak prakāśayan bhrājate yadvanaḍvān | evaṃ sa devo bhagavān vareṇyo yonisvabhāvān adhitiṣṭhaty ekaḥ || 4 ||","yacca svabhāvaṃ pacati viśvayoniḥ pācyāṃśca sarvān pariṇāmayed yaḥ | sarvam etad viśvam adhitiṣṭhaty eko guṇāṃśca sarvān viniyojayed yaḥ || 5 ||","tad vedaguhyopaniṣatsu gūḍhaṃ tad brahmā vedate brahmayonim | ye pūrvaṃ devā ṛṣayaśca tad viduste tanmayā amṛtā vai babhūvuḥ || 6 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"<strong>(5.4)</strong> As the <strong>sun</strong>, illumining all the quarters — above, below, and across — <strong>shines</strong>, even so that <strong>one God, the Lord, the adorable (vareṇya), presides over all (the wombs and) natures.</strong> <strong>(5.5)</strong> The <strong>womb of all (viśva-yoni)</strong>, who <strong>ripens each thing's own nature</strong> and <strong>brings to maturity all that is to be ripened</strong>, who <strong>presides over all this universe</strong> and <strong>assigns all the guṇas to their tasks</strong> — (He is the One). <strong>(5.6)</strong> That (Brahman), <strong>hidden in the secret of the Vedas, in the Upaniṣads</strong> — that <strong>Brahmā knows as the source of Brahman (brahma-yoni)</strong>; the <strong>gods and seers of old who knew it became identified with it and immortal.</strong>\"","para":"Three verses on the <strong>sovereign, all-ripening Lord and the secret of His knowledge.</strong> (5.4) like the <strong>sun lighting all directions</strong>, the one <strong>adorable God presides over all the sources and inherent natures (yoni-svabhāva).</strong> (5.5) as <strong>viśva-yoni</strong> (the womb of all), He <strong>ripens each being according to its own nature</strong> and <strong>deploys all the guṇas</strong> to their functions — the cosmic gardener who brings everything to its fruit. (5.6) this Brahman is <strong>\"hidden in the Upaniṣads, the secret of the Veda\"</strong>; even <strong>Brahmā (Hiraṇyagarbha) knows it as the brahma-yoni (source of Brahman/the Veda)</strong>; and the <strong>ancient gods and seers who knew it became one with it (tanmaya) and immortal.</strong> Knowledge of this God is the <strong>hidden heart of all scripture</strong>, and the path the immortals trod.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Lord ripens each nature and employs the guṇas (the cosmic governor of evolution); He is the <em>brahma-yoni</em> hidden in the Upaniṣads; the knowers become <em>tanmaya</em> (of His nature), immortal."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>svabhāvaṃ pacati</em> = matures each being's latent nature/karma; the secret is the Vedānta's core."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the Lord as the ripener of all natures (a theistic reading of karmic maturation); the Upaniṣads as the repository of His secret."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","God ripens each soul in its own time and way; the secret is hidden in the scriptures, found by the worthy, who become immortal."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>vedaguhya-upaniṣatsu</em> (\"hidden in the Upaniṣads,\" an explicit self-reference) and <em>brahma-yoni</em>; the divinisation (<em>tanmaya</em>) of the knowers."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 5.7–5.8","title":"The transmigrating soul; and the soul thumb-sized yet sun-like","sa":["5.7: गुणान्वयो यः फलकर्मकर्ता कृतस्य तस्यैव स चोपभोक्ता । स विश्वरूपस्त्रिगुणस्त्रिवर्त्मा प्राणाधिपः सञ्चरति स्वकर्मभिः ॥ ७ ॥","5.8: अङ्गुष्ठमात्रो रवितुल्यरूपः सङ्कल्पाहङ्कारसमन्वितो यः । बुद्धेर्गुणेनात्मगुणेन चैव आराग्रमात्रोऽप्यपरोऽपि दृष्टः ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["guṇānvayo yaḥ phalakarmakartā kṛtasya tasyaiva sa copabhoktā | sa viśvarūpas triguṇas trivartmā prāṇādhipaḥ sañcarati svakarmabhiḥ || 7 ||","aṅguṣṭhamātro ravitulyarūpaḥ saṅkalpāhaṅkārasamanvito yaḥ | buddher guṇenātmaguṇena caiva ārāgramātro'pyaparo'pi dṛṣṭaḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["guṇa-anvayaḥ yaḥ phala-karma-kartā","nom. (endowed with the guṇas) + (doer of fruit-bearing action)","(the soul,) endowed with the guṇas, the doer of fruit-yielding action,"],["kṛtasya tasya eva saḥ ca upabhoktā","gen. + nom. (the enjoyer of what is done)","is also the enjoyer of what he has done;"],["saḥ viśva-rūpaḥ tri-guṇaḥ tri-vartmā","nom. (of many forms, three-guṇaed, treading three paths)","of manifold forms, of the three guṇas, treading three paths,"],["prāṇa-adhipaḥ sañcarati sva-karmabhiḥ","nom. (lord of the vital airs) + pres. + instr. pl.","the lord of the vital breaths, he wanders (transmigrates) by his own deeds"]],"lit":"\"(The soul,) endowed with the <strong>guṇas</strong>, the <strong>doer of fruit-bearing action</strong>, is also the <strong>enjoyer of what it has done.</strong> Of <strong>manifold forms, three-guṇaed, treading the three paths</strong>, the <strong>lord of the vital breaths, it transmigrates by its own deeds.</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>Thumb-sized, of the appearance of the sun, endowed with volition and ego</strong> — (the soul) is seen, by the quality of the intellect and the quality of the body, <strong>also as (small as) the point of an awl</strong> (and yet, in truth, as the infinite — 5.9).\"","para":"The chapter turns to the <strong>jīva (individual soul) in bondage.</strong> (5.7) the embodied soul, <strong>invested with the guṇas</strong>, is <strong>both the doer of karma and the enjoyer of its fruits</strong>; <strong>many-formed, three-guṇaed, treading the three paths (of dharma/adharma/mokṣa, or the three destinies)</strong>, <strong>master of the breaths</strong>, it <strong>transmigrates driven by its own deeds.</strong> (5.8) it appears <strong>\"thumb-sized, sun-like, fitted with will (saṅkalpa) and ego (ahaṅkāra)\"</strong> — and, <em>as conditioned by intellect and body</em>, even <strong>\"as tiny as the tip of an awl.\"</strong> The soul's apparent size and nature are functions of its conditioning (intellect, body, karma) — not its truth, which the next verse gives.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>jīva</em> — guṇa-conditioned doer-and-enjoyer, transmigrating by karma; \"thumb-sized/awl-tipped\" = its apparent magnitude through the <em>upādhi</em> of buddhi; in reality the infinite Self (5.9)."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>trivartmā</em> = the three roads (of action); <em>prāṇādhipaḥ</em> = ruling the body's life; size is adventitious."],["Tyāgīśānanda","a clear statement of the soul as agent-enjoyer in saṃsāra, its dimensions relative to its conditioning, not real."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the soul is doer and reaper, wandering by its deeds; its \"size\" is a function of the limiting mind — drop the limit, and it is infinite."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the Sāṃkhya psychology (guṇa, buddhi, ahaṅkāra, saṅkalpa) describing the bound soul; the size-imagery from Kaṭha."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 5.9","title":"The soul as the hundredth part of a hair-tip, yet fit for the infinite","sa":["बालाग्रशतभागस्य शतधा कल्पितस्य च । भागो जीवः स विज्ञेयः स चानन्त्याय कल्पते ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["bālāgraśatabhāgasya śatadhā kalpitasya ca | bhāgo jīvaḥ sa vijñeyaḥ sa cānantyāya kalpate || 9 ||"],"words":[["bāla-agra-śata-bhāgasya","gen. (of a hundredth part of a hair's tip)","of the hundredth part of the tip of a hair,"],["śatadhā kalpitasya ca","gen. + (divided a hundredfold again)","and that (again) divided a hundredfold —"],["bhāgaḥ jīvaḥ saḥ vijñeyaḥ","nom. (a part) + nom. + gerundive","(such) a part is to be known as the soul (jīva);"],["saḥ ca ānantyāya kalpate","nom. + dat. (for infinitude) + pres.","and yet it is fit for infinitude"]],"lit":"\"Take the <strong>tip of a hair, (split into) a hundred parts, and each part again into a hundred</strong> — <strong>the soul (jīva) is to be known as (subtle as) such a part; and yet it is fit for (becomes) the infinite.</strong>\"","para":"A striking image of the soul's <strong>subtlety and its destiny</strong>: the individual soul is <strong>infinitesimally fine</strong> — \"the hundredth of the hundredth part of a hair-tip\" — when measured as a <em>conditioned</em> point of awareness. <strong>Yet \"it is fit for infinitude\" (ānantyāya kalpate)</strong> — that same soul, freed of its conditioning, is <strong>the boundless Brahman.</strong> The smallest and the greatest are one: the apparently atomic jīva is potentially (and really) the infinite Self. (A favourite verse in debates between \"atomic-soul\" theists and Advaita; the Upaniṣad holds both the subtlety <em>and</em> the infinitude.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the \"hair-tip\" minuteness is the soul's <em>apparent</em> size due to the limiting adjunct (buddhi); \"fit for infinitude\" reveals its <strong>real nature as the infinite Brahman</strong> — the conditioning, not the soul, is small."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the measure describes the subtlety of the <em>liṅga</em>/conditioned soul; its capacity for infinitude is its truth."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the verse held in tension by schools — Rāmānuja/dualists read a really atomic soul; Advaita reads apparent minuteness, real infinitude."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","however small you feel, your real measure is the infinite; the soul is a spark fit to be the whole fire."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the \"hundredth of a hair-tip\" (cf. later Vedānta's <em>aṇu-jīva</em> debate) and <em>ānantyāya kalpate</em> — the soul's infinite capacity."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 5.10–5.12","title":"Beyond sex; assuming bodies; rebirth by karma and guṇa","sa":["5.10: नैव स्त्री न पुमानेष न चैवायं नपुंसकः । यद्यच्छरीरमादत्ते तेने तेने स युज्यते ॥ १० ॥","5.11: सङ्कल्पनस्पर्शनदृष्टिमोहैर्ग्रासाम्बुवृष्ट्यात्मविवृद्धिजन्म । कर्मानुगान्यनुक्रमेण देही स्थानेषु रूपाण्यभिसम्प्रपद्यते ॥ ११ ॥","5.12: स्थूलानि सूक्ष्माणि बहूनि चैव रूपाणि देही स्वगुणैर्वृणोति । क्रियागुणैरात्मगुणैश्च तेषां संयोगहेतुरपरोऽपि दृष्टः ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["naiva strī na pumāneṣa na caivāyaṃ napuṃsakaḥ | yadyaccharīram ādatte tene tene sa yujyate || 10 ||","saṅkalpanasparśanadṛṣṭimohair grāsāmbuvṛṣṭyātmavivṛddhijanma | karmānugānyanukrameṇa dehī sthāneṣu rūpāṇyabhisamprapadyate || 11 ||","sthūlāni sūkṣmāṇi bahūni caiva rūpāṇi dehī svaguṇair vṛṇoti | kriyāguṇair ātmaguṇaiśca teṣāṃ saṃyogahetur aparo'pi dṛṣṭaḥ || 12 ||"],"words":[["na eva strī na pumān eṣaḥ na ca eva ayaṃ napuṃsakaḥ","nom.","this (soul) is neither woman, nor man, nor yet neuter;"],["yat yat śarīram ādatte","rel. + acc. + pres. (takes up)","whatever body it takes up,"],["tena tena saḥ yujyate","instr. + pres. pass.","with that very (body's gender) it is (apparently) joined"]],"lit":"\"This (soul) is <strong>neither woman, nor man, nor neuter</strong>; <strong>whatever body it takes on, with that (sex) it becomes identified.</strong>\"<br>\"By <strong>will, contact, sight, and delusion</strong>, and by the <strong>growth and birth (of the body) through food, drink, and rain</strong> (nourishment), the <strong>embodied soul successively assumes, in (various) stations, the forms that follow its karma.</strong>\"<br>\"The <strong>embodied one chooses many forms, gross and subtle, according to its own qualities (guṇas)</strong>; by the <strong>qualities of its actions and of its (inner) self</strong> their conjunction (with bodies) is caused — <strong>but there is seen yet Another (the Lord) as the cause</strong> (of the union).\"","para":"The soul's <strong>transmigration</strong>. (5.10) in itself the soul is <strong>sexless — neither male, female, nor neuter</strong> — taking on a gender only by the <strong>body</strong> it assumes (cf. BU 4; Gītā 2). (5.11) driven by <strong>mental and sensory functions (will, contact, sight, delusion)</strong> and nourished into bodily growth (food, drink, rain), the soul <strong>successively enters, in various stations, the forms determined by its karma.</strong> (5.12) it <strong>\"chooses\" gross and subtle bodies according to its own guṇas and the qualities of its actions</strong> — <em>yet</em> the verse adds the theistic corrective: <strong>\"there is also seen Another (aparo'pi dṛṣṭaḥ)\"</strong> — the <strong>Lord</strong> as the ultimate <strong>cause of the soul's union with bodies.</strong> Karma and guṇa shape rebirth, but <strong>God is the deeper ordainer</strong> (cf. the \"Third\" of 5.1).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the soul is intrinsically sexless/formless; sex and form belong to the assumed body; rebirth follows karma; <em>aparo'pi dṛṣṭaḥ</em> = the Lord, the real conjoiner — karma operates under His governance."],["Śaṅkarānanda","lists the psychic causes (saṅkalpa etc.) and the bodily (food/drink/rain) of embodiment; \"Another\" = Īśvara."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the soul's gender-neutrality and karma-driven embodiment, with the Lord as the overseeing cause — theistic karma."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","you are not your body's gender or form; you wear them by karma; but God is the silent author behind the wearing."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the detailed embryology/rebirth psychology and the theistic insertion (<em>aparo'pi dṛṣṭaḥ</em>, \"Another is seen\") subordinating karma to the Lord."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 5.13–5.14","title":"Knowing the beginningless God, one is freed; He who makes the parts (kalā)","sa":["5.13: अनाद्यनन्तं कलिलस्य मध्ये विश्वस्य स्रष्ठारमनेकरूपम् । विश्वस्यैकं परिवेष्टितारं ज्ञात्वा देवं मुच्यते सर्वपाशैः ॥ १३ ॥","5.14: भावग्राह्यमनीडाख्यं भावाभावकरं शिवम् । कलासर्गकरं देवं ये विदुस्ते जहुस्तनुम् ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["anādyanantaṃ kalilasya madhye viśvasya sraṣṭhāram anekarūpam | viśvasyaikaṃ pariveṣṭitāraṃ jñātvā devaṃ mucyate sarvapāśaiḥ || 13 ||","bhāvagrāhyamanīḍākhyaṃ bhāvābhāvakaraṃ śivam | kalāsargakaraṃ devaṃ ye viduste jahustanum || 14 ||"],"words":[["bhāva-grāhyam an-īḍa-ākhyaṃ","acc. (graspable by the pure heart/feeling) + (called \"abodeless,\" bodiless)","to be grasped by (pure) intuition, named \"the Bodiless (anīḍa),\""],["bhāva-abhāva-karaṃ śivam","acc. (maker of being and non-being) + (the Auspicious)","the maker of being and non-being, the Auspicious (Śiva),"],["kalā-sarga-karaṃ devaṃ","acc. (maker of the parts/the creation of the sixteen kalās)","the God who makes the parts (the constituents of creation) —"],["ye viduḥ te jahuḥ tanum","rel. + pf. + acc. (the body)","those who know (Him) cast off the body (for good)"]],"lit":"\"Knowing the <strong>beginningless and endless God — the Creator of the universe, of manifold form, the One who envelops all, (standing) in the midst of the world's confusion (kalila)</strong> — one is <strong>freed from all bonds.</strong>\"<br>\"The God <strong>graspable by pure intuition (bhāva-grāhya), called 'the Bodiless (anīḍa)', the maker of being and non-being, the Auspicious (Śiva), the maker of the parts (kalā-sarga-kara)</strong> — those who <strong>know Him cast off the body (and are not reborn).</strong>\"","para":"The chapter ends with the <strong>liberating knowledge.</strong> (5.13) the <strong>beginningless, endless God</strong> — <strong>Creator, many-formed, all-enveloping</strong>, present <strong>in the very midst of the world's tangled confusion (kalila)</strong> — <strong>\"knowing Him, one is freed from all bonds\"</strong> (the great refrain, cf. 1.8, 2.15, 4.16). (5.14) He is <strong>bhāva-grāhya</strong> (apprehended by the <strong>heart's pure intuition/feeling</strong>, not the senses), <strong>anīḍa</strong> (the <strong>\"abodeless / bodiless\"</strong> — having no nest/body of His own), <strong>the maker of being and non-being</strong>, the <strong>auspicious Śiva</strong>, and <strong>kalā-sarga-kara</strong> (the <strong>author of the \"parts\"</strong> — the constituents/digits of creation). <strong>Those who know Him cast off the body for good — never to be reborn.</strong> Knowledge of this God = final release.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>kalila</em> = the dense darkness/confusion of saṃsāra, in whose midst He is the creator; <em>bhāva-grāhya</em> = grasped by the purified mind/devotion, not the senses; <em>anīḍa</em> = without body; knowing Him, the bonds and rebirth end."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>kalā-sarga-kara</em> = maker of the sixteen kalās (the parts of the manifest); <em>jahuḥ tanum</em> = abandon embodiment finally."],["Tyāgīśānanda","<em>bhāva-grāhya</em> — known through love/intuition; <em>anīḍa</em> — the bodiless Lord; the chapter's release-formula."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","God is seized by the loving heart, not the eye; know the bodiless Maker and shed the body of bondage forever."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>bhāva-grāhya</em> (apprehended by feeling/intuition) and <em>anīḍa</em> (nestless/bodiless); <em>kalila</em> (chaos) and <em>kalā</em> (parts) wordplay."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Adhyāya 5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The fifth chapter pairs a <strong>metaphysics of knowledge/ignorance and the Lord</strong> with a <strong>detailed account of the bound soul</strong> and its release:","1. <strong>Two imperishables and the Third (5.1).</strong> Within Brahman lie <strong>avidyā</strong> (ignorance → the perishable, bondage) and <strong>vidyā</strong> (knowledge → immortality); ruling both is <strong>the Lord (the Third)</strong>, untouched by either — the master of māyā in both its concealing and revealing modes.","2. <strong>The sovereign over all sources (5.2–6).</strong> The God presides over <strong>every womb and nature</strong>, <strong>sustains even the first seer (Kapila / Hiraṇyagarbha)</strong>, <strong>spreads and withdraws</strong> the cosmic net, <strong>ripens each being's nature</strong> and <strong>deploys the guṇas</strong> — the secret <strong>hidden in the Upaniṣads</strong>, knowing which the gods and seers of old <strong>became immortal.</strong>","3. <strong>The bound soul (5.7–12).</strong> The <strong>jīva</strong> — <strong>doer and enjoyer</strong>, guṇa-conditioned, transmigrating by karma; appearing <strong>thumb-sized / awl-fine</strong>, even <strong>a hundredth of a hair-tip</strong>, yet <strong>\"fit for infinitude\" (5.9)</strong>; <strong>sexless in itself</strong>, assuming gender and form by the <strong>bodies</strong> it takes; reborn by <strong>karma and guṇa</strong> — yet always under <strong>\"Another\" (the Lord)</strong> as the deeper cause.","4. <strong>Release (5.13–14).</strong> <strong>Knowing the beginningless God</strong> in the midst of the world's confusion, one is <strong>freed from all bonds</strong>; the God <strong>grasped by the heart's intuition (bhāva-grāhya), the bodiless (anīḍa), the auspicious Śiva, maker of the parts</strong> — those who know Him <strong>cast off the body for ever.</strong>","The chapter's balance is characteristic: a <strong>realist psychology of the transmigrating soul</strong> (Sāṃkhya in tone) is everywhere <strong>subordinated to the one Lord</strong> (theistic Vedānta), and crowned by the <strong>soul's true infinitude</strong> and <strong>release through knowledge-and-love of God.</strong>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Adhyāya 5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   KNOWLEDGE, IGNORANCE &amp; THE SOUL (Adhyāya 5)\n   ===========================================\n   in BRAHMAN, two hidden imperishables:  AVIDYĀ (-&gt;perishable, bondage) | VIDYĀ (-&gt;immortality)\n        + THE THIRD: the LORD who rules BOTH (untouched)  [5.1]\n                              |\n   THE ONE LORD: over every womb &amp; nature · sustains KAPILA/Hiraṇyagarbha · spreads &amp; withdraws\n        the net · ripens each nature · deploys the GUṆAS · hidden in the UPANIṢADS  [5.2-6]\n                              |\n   THE BOUND SOUL (jīva) [5.7-12]:\n        doer + enjoyer · guṇa-conditioned · transmigrates by KARMA\n        &quot;thumb-sized / sun-like / awl-fine&quot; -&gt; yet a HUNDREDTH of a HAIR-TIP, &quot;FIT FOR INFINITUDE&quot; [5.9]\n        neither male/female/neuter -&gt; wears the body&#x27;s sex [5.10]\n        assumes forms by karma &amp; guṇa  ...  but &quot;ANOTHER (the Lord) is seen&quot; as the cause [5.12]\n                              |\n   RELEASE [5.13-14]:  know the BEGINNINGLESS God in the midst of the world&#x27;s confusion (kalila)\n        -&gt; &quot;FREED FROM ALL BONDS&quot;\n        He = BHĀVA-GRĀHYA (grasped by the heart) · ANĪḌA (bodiless) · ŚIVA · KALĀ-SARGA-KARA\n        -&gt; those who know Him &quot;CAST OFF THE BODY&quot; (no rebirth)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Adhyāya 5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Place ignorance and knowledge under God</strong> (5.1). Don't merely fight ignorance with knowledge as if you were their arena; recognise the <strong>Lord who rules both</strong>, and let the struggle become surrender to Him.","2. <strong>Trust the Ripener</strong> (5.5). God \"<strong>ripens each being's own nature</strong>\" in its season. Practise patience with your own and others' growth — the cosmic gardener is at work; your task is to cooperate, not to force.","3. <strong>Remember your true measure</strong> (5.9). When you feel small — a \"hundredth of a hair-tip\" amid a vast world — recall that the very same soul is <strong>\"fit for infinitude.\"</strong> Smallness is the conditioning; infinitude is the truth.","4. <strong>You are not your body's sex or form</strong> (5.10). Identify with the <strong>sexless, formless soul</strong> that <em>wears</em> the body, not with the costume it has taken on this life.","5. <strong>See \"Another\" behind your karma</strong> (5.12). Even as you own your deeds, sense the <strong>Lord as the deeper cause</strong> of every embodiment and circumstance — neither fatalism nor mere self-will, but trust in the divine ordainer.","6. <strong>Grasp God with the heart, not the eye</strong> (5.14). He is <strong>bhāva-grāhya</strong> — apprehended by <strong>pure feeling/intuition and love</strong>, not by the senses or mere intellect. Cultivate the heart's devotion as the organ of this knowledge.","7. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the soul is a transmigrating spark, shaped by karma and guṇa, yet <strong>fit for the infinite</strong> — and <strong>knowing the one bodiless God (Śiva), \"one casts off the body\" for good.</strong> The whole drama of birth and death resolves in the knowledge-and-love of the Lord."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 6 — Maheśvara, Devotion, and Release","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya 6","title":"Maheśvara, Devotion, and Release","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The final chapter crowns the Upaniṣad. Against those who credit</em> <strong>inherent nature</strong> <em>or</em> <strong>time</strong> <em>with turning the wheel of the world, it declares it to be</em> <strong>the glory of God</strong> <em>(6.1). It rises to the</em> <strong>Supreme Lord (Maheśvara)</strong> — <em>\"the God of gods, the Lord of lords\" — who \"has no task and no organ,\" whose</em> <strong>threefold power (knowledge, strength, action) is natural and innate</strong>, <em>who has</em> <strong>no master and no begetter</strong>, <em>who is the</em> <strong>inmost witness in all beings</strong> <em>(6.11), the</em> <strong>self-luminous Light by which all shines</strong> <em>(6.14). It ends with the seeker's</em> <strong>self-surrender</strong> <em>(6.18), the naming of the sage</em> <strong>Śvetāśvatara</strong> <em>(6.21), the rule for transmitting this secret, and the supreme verse on</em> <strong>devotion to God and Guru</strong> <em>(6.23).</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Verses 6.1–6.2","title":"Not nature, not time, but the glory of God turns the wheel","sa":["6.1: स्वभावमेके कवयो वदन्ति कालं तथान्ये परिमुह्यमानाः । देवस्यैष महिमा तु लोके येनेदं भ्राम्यते ब्रह्मचक्रम् ॥ १ ॥","6.2: येनावृतं नित्यमिदं हि सर्वं ज्ञः कालकारो गुणी सर्वविद् यः । तेनेशितं कर्म विवर्तते ह पृथिव्यप्तेजोनिलखानि चिन्त्यम् ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["svabhāvam eke kavayo vadanti kālaṃ tathānye parimuhyamānāḥ | devasyaiṣa mahimā tu loke yenedaṃ bhrāmyate brahmacakram || 1 ||","yenāvṛtaṃ nityam idaṃ hi sarvaṃ jñaḥ kālakāro guṇī sarvavid yaḥ | teneśitaṃ karma vivartate ha pṛthivyaptejonilakhāni cintyam || 2 ||"],"words":[["svabhāvam eke kavayaḥ vadanti","acc. (inherent nature) + nom. pl. (some thinkers) + pres.","some thinkers speak of inherent nature (as the cause),"],["kālaṃ tathā anye parimuhyamānāḥ","acc. (time) + nom. pl. (others, deluded)","and others, deluded, of time;"],["devasya eṣaḥ mahimā tu loke","gen. + m. nom. (this glory) + loc.","but this is the glory of God in the world,"],["yena idaṃ bhrāmyate brahma-cakram","rel. instr. + pres. pass. + n. nom.","by which this wheel of Brahman is made to revolve"]],"lit":"\"Some thinkers speak of <strong>inherent nature (svabhāva)</strong>, others, deluded, of <strong>time (kāla)</strong> (as the cause). But it is the <strong>glory of God</strong> in the world <strong>by which this wheel of Brahman is made to revolve.</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>By whom this whole (world) is ever enveloped — the Knower, the maker of time, the possessor of qualities, the All-knower — by Him this (cosmic) work is set in motion</strong>; (so) should one consider (the principles of) <strong>earth, water, fire, air, and ether.</strong>\"","para":"Returning to the causal question of 1.1–2, the chapter gives its <strong>final answer</strong>: it is <strong>neither svabhāva (nature) nor kāla (time)</strong> — both proposed by lesser thinkers — but <strong>\"the glory (mahimā) of God\"</strong> that <strong>turns the brahma-cakra</strong> (the wheel of the world). (6.2) That God — <strong>the Conscious One (jña), the maker of time (kāla-kāra), possessor of all powers (guṇī), the All-knowing (sarva-vit)</strong> — <strong>envelops all</strong> and <strong>sets the whole cosmic process in motion</strong>; the elements (earth, water, fire, air, ether) operate under His rule. <strong>God, not blind nature or time, is the true cause</strong> — the thesis of the whole Upaniṣad restated at its summit.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","refutes the <em>svabhāva-vādins</em> (naturalists) and <em>kāla-vādins</em> (time-fatalists); the world-wheel turns by the <strong>Lord's power (mahimā/māyā)</strong>; He is the conscious maker even of time."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>kāla-kāra</em> = creator of time (so time is His effect, not the cause); <em>guṇī</em> = wielder of the guṇas."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the climactic causal verdict — a conscious God, not impersonal nature/time, is the cause; even time is His creature."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","neither chance, nature, nor time, but God's majesty drives the cosmos; rest the inquiry in Him."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the explicit naming and rejection of <em>svabhāva-vāda</em> and <em>kāla-vāda</em> (rival causal doctrines); <em>kālakāra</em> (\"time-maker\")."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 6.3–6.6","title":"The one Creator, beyond the three times, to be worshipped within","sa":["6.5: आदिः स संयोगनिमित्तहेतुः परस्त्रिकालादकलोऽपि दृष्टः । तं विश्वरूपं भवभूतमीड्यं देवं स्वचित्तस्थमुपास्य पूर्वम् ॥ ५ ॥","6.6: स वृक्षकालाकृतिभिः परोऽन्यो यस्मात् प्रपञ्चः परिवर्ततेऽयम् । धर्मावहं पापनुदं भगेशं ज्ञात्वात्मस्थममृतं विश्वधाम ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["ādiḥ sa saṃyoganimittahetuḥ parastrikālādakalo'pi dṛṣṭaḥ | taṃ viśvarūpaṃ bhavabhūtamīḍyaṃ devaṃ svacittastham upāsya pūrvam || 5 ||","sa vṛkṣakālākṛtibhiḥ paro'nyo yasmāt prapañcaḥ parivartate'yam | dharmāvahaṃ pāpanudaṃ bhageśaṃ jñātvātmastham amṛtaṃ viśvadhāma || 6 ||"],"words":[["ādiḥ saḥ saṃyoga-nimitta-hetuḥ","nom. (the beginning) + (the cause of the conjunction)","He is the beginning, the (efficient) cause of the conjunction (of soul and matter);"],["paraḥ tri-kālāt akalaḥ api dṛṣṭaḥ","nom. (beyond the three times) + (partless) + pp.","He is seen as beyond the three times (past/present/future), and partless;"],["taṃ viśva-rūpaṃ bhava-bhūtam īḍyaṃ","acc. (of universal form, the source of becoming, adorable)","Him — of universal form, the origin of all becoming, the adorable —"],["devaṃ sva-citta-stham upāsya pūrvam","acc. + (seated in one's own mind) + ger.","the God seated in one's own heart, having first worshipped"]],"lit":"\"He is the <strong>Beginning, the efficient cause of the union (of soul and nature)</strong>; He is <strong>seen as beyond the three times (past, present, future), and as partless.</strong> Having <strong>first worshipped that adorable God of universal form, the source of all becoming, seated in one's own mind</strong> —\"<br>\"— (Him,) who is <strong>higher and other than the tree, time, and forms (the world)</strong>, from whom this <strong>manifold world revolves</strong>; <strong>knowing (Him), the bringer of dharma, remover of sin, the Lord of (all) blessings (bhageśa), seated in the self, immortal, the abode of all</strong> — (one is freed).\"","para":"Four verses on the <strong>transcendent Creator who is yet within the heart.</strong> (6.3–4) He <strong>initiates the guṇa-endowed works of creation</strong> and <strong>assigns all states to beings</strong> — creating through <strong>one, two, three, or eight principles</strong> (the Sāṃkhya counts), through time and the subtle qualities — and when His \"work\" is done, <strong>He remains \"Another\" (tattvato'nyaḥ): untouched, distinct from all He makes.</strong> (6.5) He is the <strong>primal efficient cause</strong> of the soul-nature conjunction, <strong>beyond the three times, partless</strong> — to be <strong>worshipped first as the adorable God of all forms, seated in one's own heart (sva-citta-stham).</strong> (6.6) <strong>higher and other than the world-tree, time, and forms</strong>, the <strong>source of the revolving cosmos</strong>, the <strong>giver of dharma, remover of sin, Lord of blessings (Bhageśa), the immortal Self-within, the abode of all</strong> — <strong>knowing Him, one is freed.</strong> Transcendence and indwelling, again, as one.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Lord is the efficient cause (nimitta) of the soul-prakṛti union, beyond time, partless (<em>akala</em>, <em>niṣkala</em>); <em>tattvato'nyaḥ</em> — ever distinct from the creation; to be worshipped as the <strong>heart-seated God</strong> (<em>sva-citta-stha</em>)."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the \"one, two, three, eight\" = the modes/principles of creation (Sāṃkhya tattvas); He stands beyond them."],["Tyāgīśānanda","note <em>sva-citta-stham upāsya</em> — the explicit call to <strong>inner worship</strong> of the heart-dwelling God; <em>bhageśa</em> (Lord of glories/blessings)."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","God creates yet remains \"the Other,\" untouched; worship Him within your own mind as the source and remover of sin."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","the \"one/two/three/eight\" enumerations and <em>tri-kālāt paraḥ</em> (beyond the three times) noted; the inward worship motif."]],"notes":["<strong>6.3–6.4 (gist):</strong> आरभ्य कर्माणि ... — \"Having begun the works (of creation) endowed with the guṇas, and assigning all the states (to beings) — (and) on their cessation, the dissolution of the work done — at the exhaustion of (His) work He remains, in truth, <strong>Another</strong> (untouched, distinct from it all).\" (He creates by one, two, three, or eight principles, by time and the subtle qualities of the self — 6.3.)","<em>(6.3) tatkarma kṛtvā vinivartya bhūyas tattvasya tāvena sametya yogam | ekena dvābhyāṃ tribhir aṣṭabhir vā kālena caivātmaguṇaiśca sūkṣmaiḥ || (6.4) ārabhya karmāṇi guṇānvitāni bhāvāṃśca sarvān viniyojayed yaḥ | teṣām abhāve kṛtakarmanāśaḥ karmakṣaye yāti sa tattvato'nyaḥ ||</em>"]},{"ref":"Verse 6.7","title":"The supreme God of gods, Lord of lords","sa":["तमीश्वराणां परमं महेश्वरं तं देवतानां परमं च दैवतम् । पतिं पतीनां परमं परस्ताद् विदाम देवं भुवनेशमीड्यम् ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["tam īśvarāṇāṃ paramaṃ maheśvaraṃ taṃ devatānāṃ paramaṃ ca daivatam | patiṃ patīnāṃ paramaṃ parastād vidāma devaṃ bhuvaneśam īḍyam || 7 ||"],"words":[["tam īśvarāṇāṃ paramaṃ maheśvaram","acc. + gen. pl. + acc.","Him, the supreme Lord of (all) lords, the Great Lord (Maheśvara),"],["taṃ devatānāṃ paramaṃ ca daivatam","acc. + gen. pl. + acc.","Him, the supreme Deity of (all) deities,"],["patiṃ patīnāṃ paramaṃ parastāt","acc. + gen. pl. + acc. + adv. (highest, beyond all)","the supreme Ruler of (all) rulers, beyond (all) —"],["vidāma devaṃ bhuvaneśam īḍyam","1 pl. opt./pres. + acc. (the adorable Lord of the world)","may we know (Him), the adorable God, the Lord of the universe"]],"lit":"\"Him, the <strong>supreme Lord of lords, the Great Lord (Maheśvara)</strong>; Him, the <strong>supreme Deity of deities</strong>; the <strong>supreme Ruler of rulers, beyond all</strong> — <strong>may we know that adorable God, the Lord of the universe (Bhuvaneśa).</strong>\"","para":"A ringing <strong>doxology</strong> naming the God <strong>Maheśvara</strong> (\"the Great Lord,\" the classic name of Śiva): He is <strong>the Lord above all lords, the God above all gods, the Ruler above all rulers</strong> — the absolute Supreme, <strong>beyond everything.</strong> <strong>\"May we know Him\"</strong> — the adorable <strong>Lord of all the worlds.</strong> (This verse, with its triple superlative — <em>īśvarāṇāṃ maheśvaram, devatānāṃ daivatam, patīnāṃ patim</em> — became a foundational invocation of Śaiva theology and is echoed across later hymns.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Supreme over all subordinate lords, gods, and rulers — the one Brahman/Īśvara; \"beyond all (parastāt)\" = transcending the entire hierarchy."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the three superlatives establish His absolute supremacy; <em>bhuvaneśa</em> = Lord of the cosmos."],["Tyāgīśānanda","a key Śaiva-theistic verse (Maheśvara as the Supreme); the prayerful \"may we know.\""],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","all authorities, divine and earthly, derive from and bow to the one Maheśvara; know Him as the highest."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>maheśvara</em> (Great Lord, = Śiva) and the triple superlative as a mark of mature monotheism."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 6.8–6.9","title":"He has no task, no organ; no master, no begetter; His power is innate","sa":["6.8: न तस्य कार्यं करणं च विद्यते न तत्समश्चाभ्यधिकश्च दृश्यते । परास्य शक्तिर्विविधैव श्रूयते स्वाभाविकी ज्ञानबलक्रिया च ॥ ८ ॥","6.9: न तस्य कश्चित् पतिरस्ति लोके न चेशिता नैव च तस्य लिङ्गम् । स कारणं करणाधिपाधिपो न चास्य कश्चिज्जनिता न चाधिपः ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["na tasya kāryaṃ karaṇaṃ ca vidyate na tatsamaścābhyadhikaśca dṛśyate | parāsya śaktir vividhaiva śrūyate svābhāvikī jñānabalakriyā ca || 8 ||","na tasya kaścit patir asti loke na ceśitā naiva ca tasya liṅgam | sa kāraṇaṃ karaṇādhipādhipo na cāsya kaścij janitā na cādhipaḥ || 9 ||"],"words":[["na tasya kāryaṃ karaṇaṃ ca vidyate","gen. + n. nom. (effect/task; instrument/organ) + pres.","He has neither task (effect to accomplish) nor organ (instrument);"],["na tat-samaḥ ca abhyadhikaḥ ca dṛśyate","nom. (equal to Him) + (greater) + pres. pass.","none is seen equal to Him or greater;"],["parā asya śaktiḥ vividhā eva śrūyate","f. nom. (His supreme power, manifold) + pres. pass.","His supreme power is heard (in scripture) to be of many kinds,"],["svābhāvikī jñāna-bala-kriyā ca","adj. (innate, natural) + (knowledge, strength, action)","innate (to His nature): knowledge, strength, and action"]],"lit":"\"He has <strong>no task to do and no organ (to do it with)</strong>; <strong>none is seen equal to Him or greater.</strong> His <strong>supreme Power (parā śakti)</strong> is revealed (in scripture) as <strong>manifold and innate (svābhāvikī) — (as) knowledge, strength, and action (jñāna, bala, kriyā).</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>He has no master in the world, no ruler over Him; He has no mark (liṅga, no cause/sign by which He could be inferred or generated).</strong> He is the <strong>Cause, the Lord of the lords of the sense-organs; He has no progenitor and no overlord.</strong>\"","para":"Two of the Upaniṣad's most celebrated theological verses. (6.8) The Supreme <strong>needs no instrument and has no work to accomplish</strong> (He creates effortlessly, lacking nothing) — and <strong>no one is His equal, much less His superior.</strong> Yet He is not inert: His <strong>supreme Power (parā śakti) is innate (svābhāvikī)</strong> and <strong>threefold — jñāna (knowledge/omniscience), bala (strength/omnipotence), and kriyā (action/creative activity).</strong> (This \"<strong>svābhāvikī jñāna-bala-kriyā</strong>\" is a cornerstone of later <strong>Śaiva Siddhānta</strong>, which builds its doctrine of God's natural powers on it.) (6.9) He is <strong>utterly independent: no master, no ruler, no cause or \"mark\" by which He could be produced or inferred</strong>; He is <strong>the Cause, the Lord even of the lords of the senses (the controller of all controllers)</strong>, with <strong>no begetter and no superior.</strong> The absolute, self-existent, all-powerful God.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"no task, no organ\" — being ever-fulfilled (<em>āptakāma</em>), He acts not from need; creation is His effortless play through His <em>śakti</em> (māyā); the threefold innate power = omniscience, omnipotence, creativity; \"no master, no liṅga\" — He is uncaused, unsurpassed, the cause of all."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>svābhāvikī śakti</em> = His natural, uncreated power (not adventitious); <em>liṅga</em> = a sign/cause — He has none, being self-existent."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the locus classicus for God's <strong>innate threefold power (jñāna-bala-kriyā)</strong> — central to Śaiva Siddhānta; and His absolute independence."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","God works without tools, lacking nothing; His power is His very nature — to know, to be mighty, to create; none is above Him."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>svābhāvikī jñāna-bala-kriyā</em> (the natural triad of divine powers) and the radical independence (no master, no cause) — hallmarks of developed theism."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 6.10–6.13","title":"The one God hidden in all; the eternal of the eternals; release","sa":["6.10: यस्तन्तुनाभ इव तन्तुभिः प्रधानजैः स्वभावतः । देव एकः स्वमावृणोति स नो दधातु ब्रह्माप्ययम् ॥ १० ॥","6.11: एको देवः सर्वभूतेषु गूढः सर्वव्यापी सर्वभूतान्तरात्मा । कर्माध्यक्षः सर्वभूताधिवासः साक्षी चेता केवलो निर्गुणश्च ॥ ११ ॥","6.12: एको वशी निष्क्रियाणां बहूनामेकं बीजं बहुधा यः करोति । तमात्मस्थं येऽनुपश्यन्ति धीरास्तेषां सुखं शाश्वतं नेतरेषाम् ॥ १२ ॥","6.13: नित्यो नित्यानां चेतनश्चेतनानामेको बहूनां यो विदधाति कामान् । तत्कारणं साङ्ख्ययोगाधिगम्यं ज्ञात्वा देवं मुच्यते सर्वपाशैः ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["yas tantunābha iva tantubhiḥ pradhānajaiḥ svabhāvataḥ | deva ekaḥ svam āvṛṇoti sa no dadhātu brahmāpyayam || 10 ||","eko devaḥ sarvabhūteṣu gūḍhaḥ sarvavyāpī sarvabhūtāntarātmā | karmādhyakṣaḥ sarvabhūtādhivāsaḥ sākṣī cetā kevalo nirguṇaśca || 11 ||","eko vaśī niṣkriyāṇāṃ bahūnām ekaṃ bījaṃ bahudhā yaḥ karoti | tam ātmasthaṃ ye'nupaśyanti dhīrās teṣāṃ sukhaṃ śāśvataṃ netareṣām || 12 ||","nityo nityānāṃ cetanaścetanānām eko bahūnāṃ yo vidadhāti kāmān | tatkāraṇaṃ sāṅkhyayogādhigamyaṃ jñātvā devaṃ mucyate sarvapāśaiḥ || 13 ||"],"words":[["ekaḥ devaḥ sarva-bhūteṣu gūḍhaḥ","nom. + loc. pl. + pp.","the one God, hidden in all beings,"],["sarva-vyāpī sarva-bhūta-antarātmā","nom. (all-pervading; the inner Self of all)","all-pervading, the inner Self of all beings,"],["karma-adhyakṣaḥ sarva-bhūta-adhivāsaḥ","nom. (overseer of works; dwelling in all beings)","the overseer of (all) works, dwelling in all beings,"],["sākṣī cetā kevalaḥ nirguṇaḥ ca","nom. (witness, conscious, alone, attributeless)","the Witness, the Conscious, the Absolute (alone), and beyond the guṇas"]],"lit":"\"The <strong>one God, hidden in all beings, all-pervading, the inner Self of all beings, the overseer of all works, indwelling all beings, the Witness, the Conscious one, the Absolute (alone), and beyond the guṇas (nirguṇa).</strong>\"<br>\"The <strong>one Controller of the many inactive (souls), who makes the one seed manifold</strong> — the <strong>wise who behold Him seated in their own self, theirs is eternal happiness, and no others'.</strong>\"<br>\"The <strong>Eternal among the eternals, the Conscious among the conscious, the One who, (though one,) grants the desires of the many</strong> — that <strong>Cause, attainable through Sāṃkhya and Yoga: knowing (that) God, one is freed from all bonds.</strong>\" <em>(6.13ab = Kaṭha 2.2.13.)</em>","para":"Four verses gathering the <strong>immanent, sovereign, liberating God.</strong> (6.10) like a <strong>spider covering itself with the threads it spins from itself</strong> out of <em>pradhāna</em> (primal matter), the <strong>one God veils Himself</strong> in the world He projects — <em>\"may He grant us entry into Brahman (brahmāpyaya).\"</em> (6.11) the magnificent definition: the <strong>one God, hidden in all beings, all-pervading, the inner Self of all, overseer of works, indweller, the Witness (sākṣī), the Conscious (cetā), the Absolute (kevala), beyond the guṇas (nirguṇa)</strong> — both the personal Lord and the attributeless Self in one breath. (6.12) the <strong>one Controller of the many (passive) souls</strong>, who makes <strong>one seed into many</strong> — those who <strong>see Him within have eternal joy; none else do.</strong> (6.13) the famous <strong>\"nityo nityānāṃ cetanaś cetanānām\"</strong> — the <strong>Eternal of eternals, the Conscious of the conscious</strong> — the <strong>One who fulfils the desires of the many</strong>, the <strong>Cause attainable by Sāṃkhya (discrimination) and Yoga (meditation)</strong> — <strong>\"knowing God, one is freed from all bonds.\"</strong> The chapter's refrain of liberation, now naming both paths.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the spider-simile (6.10) = the Lord projecting the world from Himself by His power, then veiling Himself in it; 6.11 unites <em>saguṇa</em> (Lord, overseer, indweller) and <em>nirguṇa</em> (witness, kevala, beyond guṇas) — the one Self; 6.13 — the supreme Self attainable by Sāṃkhya (knowledge) and Yoga (practice)."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>karmādhyakṣa</em> = the impartial overseer of all karma; <em>eternal of eternals</em> = the Self that gives reality to all relative eternals/conscious beings."],["Tyāgīśānanda","6.11 a complete portrait of God — immanent, witnessing, attributeless; 6.13 (= Kaṭha) names <strong>Sāṃkhya-Yoga</strong> as the means — the text's signature synthesis."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","God spins the world from Himself like a spider; He is the Witness in your heart; see Him there for eternal joy; both knowledge and yoga lead to Him."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","6.13ab = Kaṭha 5.13; <em>sāṅkhya-yoga-adhigamyam</em> an explicit naming of the two systems; the spider-simile (cf. Muṇḍaka 1.1.7)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 6.14–6.15","title":"The Light by which all shines; the one swan in whom is the way","sa":["6.14: न तत्र सूर्यो भाति न चन्द्रतारकं नेमा विद्युतो भान्ति कुतोऽयमग्निः । तमेव भान्तमनुभाति सर्वं तस्य भासा सर्वमिदं विभाति ॥ १४ ॥","6.15: एको हंसः भुवनस्यास्य मध्ये स एवाग्निः सलिले संनिविष्टः । तमेव विदित्वा अतिमृत्युमेति नान्यः पन्था विद्यतेऽयनाय ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["na tatra sūryo bhāti na candratārakaṃ nemā vidyuto bhānti kuto'yam agniḥ | tam eva bhāntam anubhāti sarvaṃ tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vibhāti || 14 ||","eko haṃsaḥ bhuvanasyāsya madhye sa evāgniḥ salile sanniviṣṭaḥ | tam eva viditvā atimṛtyum eti nānyaḥ panthā vidyate'yanāya || 15 ||"],"words":[["na tatra sūryaḥ bhāti na candra-tārakaṃ","loc. + pres.","there the sun shines not, nor moon and stars,"],["na imāḥ vidyutaḥ bhānti kutaḥ ayam agniḥ","f. nom. pl. + pres.","nor do these lightnings shine — how then this fire?"],["tam eva bhāntam anubhāti sarvaṃ","acc. (Him alone, shining) + pres. + nom.","Him alone, as He shines, all things shine after;"],["tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vibhāti","gen. + instr. (by His light) + pres.","by His light all this shines"]],"lit":"\"<strong>There the sun shines not, nor the moon and stars, nor do these lightnings shine — how then this (earthly) fire? Him alone, as He shines, does everything shine after; by His light all this shines.</strong>\" <em>(= Kaṭha 2.2.15; Muṇḍaka 2.2.10; cf. Gītā 15.6.)</em><br>\"<strong>The one swan (haṃsa), in the midst of this world — He indeed is the fire seated in the water.</strong> Knowing Him alone, one <strong>passes beyond death; there is no other path for the going (to liberation).</strong>\" <em>(cf. 3.8.)</em>","para":"Two luminous verses. (6.14) the <strong>supreme statement of the Self as the Light of lights</strong>: in the presence of that self-luminous Reality, <strong>sun, moon, stars, lightning, and fire have no light of their own</strong> — <strong>they shine only by <em>His</em> light</strong>; \"<strong>by His light all this shines (tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vibhāti).</strong>\" (This mantra, shared with the Kaṭha, Muṇḍaka, and Gītā, is among the most beloved in the Upaniṣads.) (6.15) the <strong>one haṃsa (swan/Self)</strong> dwells <strong>in the midst of the world</strong>, the <strong>fire latent in the waters</strong> (the divine spark in the cosmos and the heart); <strong>knowing Him alone, one crosses death — there is no other path</strong> (repeating 3.8). Self-luminous source of all light, the indwelling swan — to know Him is the <em>only</em> way to immortality.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Self is <em>svayaṃ-jyotiḥ</em> — the light that needs no other and lends light to all; sun, fire, etc., are but His borrowed radiance; the haṃsa = the supreme Self, the \"fire in the water\" (the divine in the heart/cosmos)."],["Śaṅkarānanda","\"by His light all shines\" — He is the illuminator of all illuminators; \"no other path\" = knowledge alone liberates."],["Tyāgīśānanda","6.14 the great \"Light of lights\" verse; 6.15 reaffirms the single path of God-knowledge to immortality."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","every light you see is His; behind the eye that sees the sun is the Light that lights the sun; know that one Swan and cross death."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","6.14 = Kaṭha 5.15 / Muṇḍaka 2.2.10 (and Gītā 15.6); one of the most widely shared and quoted mantras."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 6.16–6.17","title":"The Maker and Knower of all; the cause of bondage and release","sa":["6.16: स विश्वकृद् विश्वविदात्मयोनिर्ज्ञः कालकालो गुणी सर्वविद् यः । प्रधानक्षेत्रज्ञपतिर्गुणेशः संसारमोक्षस्थितिबन्धहेतुः ॥ १६ ॥","6.17: स तन्मयो ह्यमृत ईशसंस्थो ज्ञः सर्वगो भुवनस्यास्य गोप्ता । य ईशेऽस्य जगतो नित्यमेव नान्यो हेतुर्विद्यत ईशनाय ॥ १७ ॥"],"iast":["sa viśvakṛd viśvavid ātmayonir jñaḥ kālakālo guṇī sarvavid yaḥ | pradhānakṣetrajñapatir guṇeśaḥ saṃsāramokṣasthitibandhahetuḥ || 16 ||","sa tanmayo hyamṛta īśasaṃstho jñaḥ sarvago bhuvanasyāsya goptā | ya īśe'sya jagato nityam eva nānyo hetur vidyata īśanāya || 17 ||"],"words":[["saḥ viśva-kṛt viśva-vit ātma-yoniḥ","nom. (maker of all, knower of all, self-originated)","He is the maker of all, the knower of all, His own source,"],["jñaḥ kāla-kālaḥ guṇī sarva-vit yaḥ","nom. (conscious; the Time of time; possessor of qualities; all-knowing)","the Conscious, the Time of time, wielder of qualities, all-knowing;"],["pradhāna-kṣetrajña-patiḥ guṇa-īśaḥ","nom. (Lord of matter and the soul; ruler of the guṇas)","the Lord of primal matter (pradhāna) and the soul (kṣetrajña), ruler of the guṇas;"],["saṃsāra-mokṣa-sthiti-bandha-hetuḥ","nom. (the cause of bondage, continuance, release, and saṃsāra)","the cause of saṃsāra, of release, of continuance, and of bondage"]],"lit":"\"He is the <strong>Maker of all, the Knower of all, His own source (self-born); the Conscious, the Time of time (the Death of death), the wielder of the guṇas, the All-knowing; the Lord of primal matter (pradhāna) and the soul (kṣetrajña), the ruler of the guṇas; the cause of bondage, of continuance, of release, and of transmigration.</strong>\"<br>\"He, <strong>of that nature (tanmaya), immortal, established as the Lord (Īśa), the Conscious, all-pervading, the Protector of this world</strong> — who <strong>rules this world for ever — (there is) no other cause for (its) ruling (than He).</strong>\"","para":"Two summary verses on the <strong>all-comprehending Lord.</strong> (6.16) He is <strong>Creator and Knower of all, self-originated (ātma-yoni)</strong>, the <strong>conscious \"Time of time\" (kāla-kāla — He who outlasts and devours even time/death)</strong>, the <strong>Lord of both matter (pradhāna) and the soul (kṣetrajña)</strong> and <strong>ruler of the guṇas</strong> — and, strikingly, the <strong>single cause of bondage <em>and</em> release, of the world's persistence <em>and</em> its dissolution</strong> (all four states proceed from the one Lord, under His governance and grace). (6.17) He is <strong>immortal, abiding as the Lord, conscious, all-pervading, the world's Protector</strong>, the <strong>eternal Ruler — and there is no other cause of (the world's) governance than He.</strong> The one God is the <strong>complete explanation of reality</strong> — matter, soul, time, the guṇas, bondage, and liberation alike.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Lord is the cause of <em>both</em> bondage (through māyā/avidyā) and release (through knowledge/grace) — the sole governor; <em>kāla-kāla</em> = beyond time/death; <em>pradhāna-kṣetrajña-pati</em> = Lord of nature and soul (the Sāṃkhya pair, here subordinate to Him)."],["Śaṅkarānanda","He is the one cause of all four — saṃsāra, sthiti, mokṣa, bandha; <em>ātma-yoni</em> = self-caused."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the comprehensive theology — God as Lord of matter and soul, of time and the guṇas, the cause of both bondage and freedom; no second cause."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","one God is behind your binding and your freeing alike; surrender to the sole Ruler."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>pradhāna-kṣetrajña-pati</em> (Lord of Sāṃkhya's prakṛti and puruṣa) and <em>kāla-kāla</em> — the theistic capping of Sāṃkhya dualism."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 6.18","title":"I take refuge in Him, desiring liberation","sa":["यो ब्रह्माणं विदधाति पूर्वं यो वै वेदांश्च प्रहिणोति तस्मै । तं ह देवं आत्मबुद्धिप्रकाशं मुमुक्षुर्वै शरणमहं प्रपद्ये ॥ १८ ॥"],"iast":["yo brahmāṇaṃ vidadhāti pūrvaṃ yo vai vedāṃśca prahiṇoti tasmai | taṃ ha devaṃ ātmabuddhiprakāśaṃ mumukṣur vai śaraṇam ahaṃ prapadye || 18 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ brahmāṇaṃ vidadhāti pūrvaṃ","rel. + acc. (Brahmā, the creator) + pres. + adv.","He who creates Brahmā (Hiraṇyagarbha) in the beginning,"],["yaḥ vai vedān ca prahiṇoti tasmai","rel. + acc. pl. + pres. + dat. (to him)","and who sends forth the Vedas to him;"],["taṃ ha devaṃ ātma-buddhi-prakāśaṃ","acc. + (the illuminer of the intellect/the Self-revealing light)","that God, the light that illumines the intellect (toward the Self) —"],["mumukṣuḥ vai śaraṇam ahaṃ prapadye","nom. (one desiring liberation) + acc. (refuge) + 1 sg. pres. of pra-pad","I, desiring liberation, take refuge (in Him)"]],"lit":"\"He who <strong>creates Brahmā (the cosmic creator) in the beginning</strong> and <strong>sends forth the Vedas to him</strong> — <strong>that God, the illuminer of the intellect (the light of self-knowledge) — I, seeking liberation, take refuge in (Him).</strong>\"","para":"The <strong>personal climax of the Upaniṣad's devotion.</strong> The God who <strong>produced even Brahmā (Hiraṇyagarbha, the first creator) and revealed the Vedas to him</strong> — the very <strong>Light that kindles the intellect toward Self-knowledge (ātma-buddhi-prakāśa)</strong> — to <strong>Him</strong>, says the seer, <strong>\"I, longing for liberation, surrender / take refuge (śaraṇam ahaṃ prapadye).\"\"</strong> This is <strong>śaraṇāgati / prapatti</strong> — explicit, first-person <strong>self-surrender to God</strong> as the means of liberation — one of the earliest such declarations in the Upaniṣads, and a touchstone for all later bhakti (echoed in the Gītā's <em>\"mām ekaṃ śaraṇaṃ vraja\"</em>).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Lord, source of Brahmā and the Vedas, is also the <strong>inner light of the buddhi</strong> by which Self-knowledge dawns; the seeker, <em>mumukṣu</em>, takes refuge — devotion as the aid to knowledge."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>ātma-buddhi-prakāśa</em> = He who illumines the intellect/reveals the Self; <em>prapadye</em> = I surrender."],["Tyāgīśānanda","the explicit <em>prapatti</em>-verse — first-person refuge in God for liberation; the seed of the bhakti-mārga."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the highest knowledge culminates in surrender: \"I take refuge in the God who lights my very intellect.\" Grace and effort meet."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>śaraṇam ahaṃ prapadye</em> (\"I take refuge\") — a landmark of Upaniṣadic devotion/surrender, prefiguring the Gītā."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 6.19–6.20","title":"The partless bridge to immortality; the impossible measure","sa":["6.19: निष्कलं निष्क्रियं शान्तं निरवद्यं निरञ्जनम् । अमृतस्य परं सेतुं दग्धेन्धनमिवानलम् ॥ १९ ॥","6.20: यदा चर्मवदाकाशं वेष्टयिष्यन्ति मानवाः । तदा देवमविज्ञाय दुःखस्यान्तो भविष्यति ॥ २० ॥"],"iast":["niṣkalaṃ niṣkriyaṃ śāntaṃ niravadyaṃ nirañjanam | amṛtasya paraṃ setuṃ dagdhendhanam ivānalam || 19 ||","yadā carmavadākāśaṃ veṣṭayiṣyanti mānavāḥ | tadā devam avijñāya duḥkhasyānto bhaviṣyati || 20 ||"],"words":[["niṣkalaṃ niṣkriyaṃ śāntaṃ","acc. (partless, actionless, peaceful)","(Him,) partless, actionless, tranquil,"],["niravadyaṃ nirañjanam","acc. (faultless, stainless)","faultless, stainless,"],["amṛtasya paraṃ setuṃ","acc. (the supreme bridge to immortality)","the supreme bridge to immortality,"],["dagdha-indhanam iva analam","acc. (like a fire whose fuel is burnt out)","like a fire whose fuel is consumed"]],"lit":"\"(One should know Him —) <strong>partless, actionless, tranquil, faultless, stainless, the supreme bridge to immortality, like a fire whose fuel is wholly burnt out.</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>When men shall (be able to) roll up the sky like a piece of leather — (only) then, without knowing God, will there be an end of sorrow.</strong>\"","para":"(6.19) a serene description of the <strong>Absolute</strong>: <strong>niṣkala (partless), niṣkriya (actionless), śānta (peaceful), niravadya (flawless), nirañjana (untainted)</strong> — the <strong>\"supreme bridge (setu) to immortality,\"</strong> like <strong>a fire that has burnt up all its fuel</strong> (the Self with all karma/desire consumed — pure, still, blazing without smoke). (6.20) a famous <strong>adynaton (impossibility-figure)</strong>: <strong>\"only when men can roll up the sky like leather will sorrow end without knowing God.\"</strong> Since rolling up space is impossible, the meaning is absolute: <strong>there is no end of suffering except by knowing God.</strong> The two verses together: the goal (the partless, peaceful Absolute, the bridge to immortality) and the <strong>necessity</strong> of knowing Him.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>niṣkala/niṣkriya</em> etc. = the <em>nirguṇa</em> Brahman; \"fuel burnt out\" = the Self free of all upādhis/karma; \"rolling up the sky\" = an impossibility, so God-knowledge is the <em>sine qua non</em> of liberation."],["Śaṅkarānanda","the bridge (<em>setu</em>) that carries across to immortality; the impossible-measure verse stresses the indispensability of knowledge."],["Tyāgīśānanda","6.20 a striking rhetorical figure — there is <em>no</em> alternative to God-realisation for the end of sorrow."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the Absolute is the still, smokeless flame; and only knowing God ends grief — nothing else ever will."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note the <em>adynaton</em> (rolling up space like leather; cf. later texts) and the <em>niṣkala</em> description of the Absolute."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verses 6.21–6.23","title":"The sage Śvetāśvatara; the rule of transmission; and supreme devotion","sa":["6.21: तपःप्रभावाद् देवप्रसादाच्च ब्रह्म ह श्वेताश्वतरोऽथ विद्वान् । अत्याश्रमिभ्यः परमं पवित्रं प्रोवाच सम्यगृषिसङ्घजुष्टम् ॥ २१ ॥","6.22: वेदान्ते परमं गुह्यं पुराकल्पे प्रचोदितम् । नाप्रशान्ताय दातव्यं नापुत्रायाशिष्याय वा पुनः ॥ २२ ॥","6.23: यस्य देवे परा भक्तिः यथा देवे तथा गुरौ । तस्यैते कथिता ह्यर्थाः प्रकाशन्ते महात्मनः ॥ २३ ॥"],"iast":["tapaḥprabhāvād devaprasādācca brahma ha śvetāśvataro'tha vidvān | atyāśramibhyaḥ paramaṃ pavitraṃ provāca samyagṛṣisaṅghajuṣṭam || 21 ||","vedānte paramaṃ guhyaṃ purākalpe pracoditam | nāpraśāntāya dātavyaṃ nāputrāyāśiṣyāya vā punaḥ || 22 ||","yasya deve parā bhaktiḥ yathā deve tathā gurau | tasyaite kathitā hyarthāḥ prakāśante mahātmanaḥ || 23 ||"],"words":[["yasya deve parā bhaktiḥ","gen. + loc. (in God) + f. nom. (supreme devotion)","he who has supreme devotion to God,"],["yathā deve tathā gurau","adv. + loc.","and, as to God, so to the Guru (teacher);"],["tasya ete kathitāḥ hi arthāḥ","gen. + nom. pl. (these declared truths)","to him (alone) these truths that have been declared"],["prakāśante mahātmanaḥ","pres. (shine forth) + gen. (of that great soul)","shine forth — (to him,) the great soul"]],"lit":"\"By the <strong>power of his austerity (tapas) and the grace of God (deva-prasāda)</strong>, the <strong>sage Śvetāśvatara, having known Brahman, declared (it) — the supremely holy (teaching), well-approved by the company of seers — to the renunciants of the highest order (atyāśramins).</strong>\"<br>\"This <strong>supreme secret, set forth in the Vedānta in a former age (purā-kalpa), should not be given to one who is not tranquil, nor to one who is not a son or not a pupil.</strong>\"<br>\"<strong>To the great soul who has supreme devotion (parā bhakti) to God, and as to God so to the Guru — to him (alone) these truths that have been declared shine forth (reveal themselves).</strong>\"","para":"The Upaniṣad's <strong>colophon and crowning verses.</strong> (6.21) it names its source: the <strong>sage Śvetāśvatara</strong> (\"he of the white mule/horse\"), who, <strong>by tapas (austerity) and divine grace (deva-prasāda)</strong> — <em>both</em> effort and grace — <strong>knew Brahman</strong> and <strong>taught this most holy doctrine to the highest renunciants (atyāśramins)</strong>. (6.22) the <strong>rule of transmission</strong>: this <strong>supreme secret (paramaṃ guhyam)</strong> is to be imparted only to the <strong>tranquil, and to a son or qualified pupil</strong> — guarding it from the unprepared. (6.23) the <strong>final, immortal verse</strong> — the seed of the entire bhakti tradition: <strong>\"yasya deve parā bhaktir yathā deve tathā gurau\" — he who has supreme devotion to God, and the same devotion to the Guru as to God — to that great soul (mahātman) alone do these declared truths reveal themselves (prakāśante).\"\"</strong> The whole Upaniṣad — its metaphysics of the one God, its yoga, its māyā, its self-surrender — culminates in this: <strong>the highest knowledge dawns only on the heart of supreme devotion to God and Guru.</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","knowledge is won by <strong>tapas + grace</strong> (6.21); the secret is guarded (6.22); and (6.23) it <strong>\"shines forth\" only to one of supreme <em>bhakti</em> to God and Guru</strong> — devotion as the indispensable condition for the revelation of the truths (not a substitute for knowledge, but its necessary ground). A key text for <em>guru-bhakti</em>."],["Śaṅkarānanda","<em>atyāśramin</em> = the highest renunciate; the qualifications (tranquillity, sonship/discipleship); <em>parā bhakti</em> the condition of revelation."],["Tyāgīśānanda","6.23 the most famous verse — the <strong>earliest clear Upaniṣadic statement that the deepest truths reveal themselves only through devotion to God and Guru</strong>; the union of <em>jñāna</em> and <em>bhakti</em>; the named sage and the transmission-rule frame it."],["Krishnananda/Sivananda","the truths are not grasped by intellect alone but <strong>revealed by grace to the devoted heart</strong>; revere the Guru as God; this is the gateway. The capstone of the whole teaching."],["Müller/Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>deva-prasāda</em> (grace) paired with <em>tapas</em> (6.21); <em>atyāśramin</em> (a supra-āśrama renunciate); and 6.23 — a landmark for <em>bhakti</em> and <em>guru-bhakti</em>, much-quoted in later tradition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Adhyāya 6","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The final chapter is the Upaniṣad's <strong>theological and devotional summit</strong>:","1. <strong>The true cause (6.1–2).</strong> Not <strong>svabhāva (nature)</strong> nor <strong>kāla (time)</strong> — both rejected — but <strong>the glory of God</strong> turns the world-wheel; God is even the <strong>maker of time (kāla-kāra)</strong>.","2. <strong>The transcendent-immanent Creator (6.3–7).</strong> He initiates creation (through one/two/three/eight principles) yet remains <strong>\"Another,\" untouched</strong>; <strong>beyond the three times, partless</strong>, to be <strong>worshipped within the heart</strong>; <strong>Maheśvara — God of gods, Lord of lords, Ruler of rulers, beyond all.</strong>","3. <strong>The absolute, self-powered God (6.8–9).</strong> He has <strong>no task and no organ</strong>, <strong>none equal or greater</strong>; His <strong>power is innate and threefold — knowledge, strength, action (svābhāvikī jñāna-bala-kriyā)</strong>; He has <strong>no master, no cause, no begetter</strong> — utterly independent.","4. <strong>Immanent Witness and sole Cause (6.10–17).</strong> Like a <strong>spider</strong> He veils Himself in the world He spins; He is the <strong>one God hidden in all, the inner Self, Witness, Conscious, Absolute, beyond the guṇas</strong>; the <strong>Eternal of eternals</strong>, attainable by <strong>Sāṃkhya and Yoga</strong>; the <strong>self-luminous Light by which sun, moon, and fire shine</strong>; the <strong>one swan</strong>; the <strong>Lord of matter and soul, the single cause of bondage and release.</strong>","5. <strong>Surrender (6.18).</strong> <strong>\"I, seeking liberation, take refuge in Him\"</strong> — explicit <em>prapatti</em>.","6. <strong>The Absolute, and the necessity of knowing Him (6.19–20).</strong> <strong>Partless, actionless, peaceful, stainless — the bridge to immortality</strong>; and <strong>without knowing God, sorrow ends only when the sky can be rolled up like leather</strong> (i.e., never).","7. <strong>The seal: devotion (6.21–23).</strong> Won by <strong>tapas and grace</strong>, taught by the sage <strong>Śvetāśvatara</strong>, guarded for the worthy — and revealed at last <strong>only to one of supreme devotion to God and Guru (yasya deve parā bhaktiḥ…).</strong>","The Upaniṣad thus completes its <strong>grand synthesis</strong>: the one God is <strong>Brahman and Īśvara, transcendent and immanent, the wielder of māyā/prakṛti, the Witness and the Light</strong> — approached by <strong>knowledge (jñāna), discrimination (sāṅkhya), meditation (yoga), self-surrender (prapatti), and devotion (bhakti)</strong> — and its final word is that <strong>the truth reveals itself by grace, to love.</strong>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Adhyāya 6","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   MAHEŚVARA, DEVOTION, RELEASE (Adhyāya 6)\n   ========================================\n   THE CAUSE: not svabhāva (nature), not kāla (time) -&gt; the GLORY OF GOD turns the wheel [6.1-2]\n                              |\n   THE CREATOR: beyond the three times · partless · &quot;Another&quot; (untouched) · worship Him IN THE HEART [6.3-6]\n        ===&gt; MAHEŚVARA: God of gods · Lord of lords · Ruler of rulers · beyond all [6.7]\n                              |\n   THE ABSOLUTE GOD [6.8-9]:\n        &quot;na tasya kāryaṃ karaṇaṃ ca&quot; (no task, no organ) · none equal/greater\n        His innate power = SVĀBHĀVIKĪ JÑĀNA-BALA-KRIYĀ (knowledge·strength·action)\n        no master · no cause · no begetter — utterly INDEPENDENT\n                              |\n   IMMANENT WITNESS [6.10-17]:\n        SPIDER veiling itself in its own threads · &quot;EKO DEVAḤ ... sākṣī cetā kevalo nirguṇaḥ&quot;\n        &quot;NITYO NITYĀNĀṂ cetanaś cetanānām&quot; -&gt; attainable by SĀṂKHYA + YOGA -&gt; freed of all bonds\n        &quot;NA TATRA SŪRYO BHĀTI ... tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vibhāti&quot; (the LIGHT of all lights) [6.14]\n        Lord of matter &amp; soul · cause of BONDAGE and RELEASE alike [6.16-17]\n                              |\n   SURRENDER [6.18]:  &quot;ŚARAṆAM AHAṂ PRAPADYE&quot; (I take refuge), desiring liberation\n   THE ABSOLUTE [6.19]: niṣkala·niṣkriya·śānta·niravadya·nirañjana — the BRIDGE to immortality\n   NO OTHER WAY [6.20]: sorrow ends only by knowing God (else &quot;roll up the sky like leather&quot;)\n                              |\n   THE SEAL [6.21-23]:  won by TAPAS + DEVA-PRASĀDA (grace) · taught by sage ŚVETĀŚVATARA\n        ====&gt;  &quot;YASYA DEVE PARĀ BHAKTIḤ, YATHĀ DEVE TATHĀ GURAU&quot;\n               -&gt; to that great soul (alone) these truths SHINE FORTH (prakāśante)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Adhyāya 6","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Credit God, not blind forces</strong> (6.1). When tempted to explain your life by \"nature,\" \"time,\" or \"luck,\" recall: it is <strong>the glory of God</strong> that turns the wheel. This reorients the whole inquiry toward Him.","2. <strong>Worship within</strong> (6.5). Make your practice <strong>inner worship of the God \"seated in your own mind (sva-citta-stha)\"</strong> — not only an outer rite but the adoration of the indwelling Lord.","3. <strong>Trust His effortless, innate power</strong> (6.8). God \"has no task and no organ\" — He needs nothing, yet His <strong>knowledge, strength, and action</strong> are His very nature. Lean on a Lord who is complete, not on a needy deity.","4. <strong>See Him as the Witness within</strong> (6.11). Behind every being and your own mind is the <strong>one hidden God — the Witness, conscious, attribute-free.</strong> Rest as/with that Witness.","5. <strong>Know that every light is His</strong> (6.14). Practise: the sun, the lamp, the spark of your own awareness — all <strong>shine by His light.</strong> Trace each light to the Light of lights.","6. <strong>Take refuge</strong> (6.18). Let knowledge culminate in <strong>prapatti</strong>: \"<em>I, seeking freedom, take refuge in Thee.</em>\" Combine <strong>tapas (effort)</strong> with openness to <strong>deva-prasāda (grace)</strong> — both are named (6.21).","7. <strong>Cultivate supreme devotion to God and Guru</strong> (6.23). The Upaniṣad's last word: the deepest truths <strong>reveal themselves (prakāśante)</strong> not to cleverness but <strong>to the heart of <em>parā bhakti</em> — devotion to God, and to the Guru as to God.</strong> Make that love the ground of all study and practice.","8. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the whole Śvetāśvatara converges here — the one God is <strong>Brahman and Lord, transcendent and indwelling, wielder of māyā, Witness and Light</strong> — and He is won by <strong>knowledge ripened in devotion and grace.</strong> <em>\"Yasya deve parā bhaktiḥ…\"</em> — to the loving heart, the truth shines of itself."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Colophon","title":"","sa":["इति श्वेताश्वतरोपनिषत्समाप्ता ॥","ॐ सह नाववतु । सह नौ भुनक्तु । सह वीर्यं करवावहै । तेजस्वि नावधीतमस्तु । मा विद्विषावहै ॥ ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["iti śvetāśvataropaniṣat samāptā ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Thus the Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad is concluded.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Oṃ saha nāvavatu… Oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||</em> — May we be protected together; may our study be illumining; may we never hate. Oṃ, peace, peace, peace."]},{"ref":"The whole in one glance","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The <strong>Śvetāśvatara</strong> answers the question with which it opens — <em>what is the cause?</em> — by unfolding, across six adhyāyas, the <strong>one God</strong> who is <strong>both Brahman and the personal Lord (Rudra-Śiva-Maheśvara)</strong>:","- <strong>(1)</strong> the <strong>first cause</strong> is neither time, nature, chance, the elements, nor the soul, but the <strong>one God and His self-power (devātma-śakti)</strong>, on whose <strong>wheel</strong> the soul revolves until, by knowledge and grace, it knows the Mover;","- <strong>(2)</strong> that God is realised by <strong>Yoga</strong> — the yoking of mind and breath, in a fit place, until the dust-covered mirror of the mind reflects the <strong>Self</strong>, and one bows to the God in fire, water, herbs, and all;","- <strong>(3)</strong> He is the <strong>one Rudra/Puruṣa</strong> — transcendent (\"beyond darkness; no other path\") and immanent (the <strong>thumb-sized Person in the heart</strong>), <strong>subtler than the subtle</strong>, seen by the <strong>desireless, by grace</strong>;","- <strong>(4)</strong> He is <strong>all forms</strong> (\"Thou art woman, Thou art man\") yet <strong>imageless</strong> (\"no likeness of Him\"), the <strong>wielder of māyā = prakṛti</strong> as <strong>Maheśvara</strong>, and the <strong>watching bird</strong> beside the eating soul;","- <strong>(5)</strong> He rules <strong>knowledge and ignorance</strong>, ripens every nature, and is the deeper cause behind the <strong>transmigrating soul</strong> — which, though \"a hundredth of a hair-tip,\" is <strong>fit for infinitude</strong>;","- <strong>(6)</strong> He is <strong>Maheśvara above all</strong>, of <strong>innate knowledge-strength-action</strong>, with <strong>no master and no equal</strong>, the <strong>Witness in all</strong>, the <strong>Light by which all shines</strong>, the <strong>bridge to immortality</strong> — won by <strong>tapas, grace, knowledge, surrender, and supreme devotion to God and Guru.</strong>","Its enduring gifts to the tradition: the words <strong>māyā, bhakti, deva-prasāda (grace), svābhāvikī jñāna-bala-kriyā</strong>; the great verses <strong>\"eko hi rudraḥ,\" \"tvaṃ strī tvaṃ pumān,\" \"dvā suparṇā,\" \"māyāṃ tu prakṛtiṃ vidyāt,\" \"na tatra sūryo bhāti,\" and \"yasya deve parā bhaktiḥ\"</strong>; and the seamless <strong>synthesis of Vedānta, Sāṃkhya, Yoga, and theistic devotion</strong> that shaped all later Hindu thought, from the Bhagavad-Gītā to Śaiva Siddhānta."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Sources & method (for this volume)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>Mūla text:</strong> the standard Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda recension of the Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad (113 verses, 6 adhyāyas), taken verbatim from the <strong>ITRANS edition on sanskritdocuments.org</strong> (`shveta.itx`; transliterated by Sunder Hattangadi, proofread by John Manetta) and converted to <strong>IAST and Devanāgarī with the `indic_transliteration` (sanscript) library</strong> — so every verse's Devanāgarī and IAST is a faithful, mechanically-checked rendering of that source, not a hand-transcription.","- <strong>Translations &amp; commentaries consulted:</strong> the <em>Śvetāśvataropaniṣad-bhāṣya</em> ascribed to <strong>Śaṅkara</strong> (Eng. Swāmī Gambhīrānanda, Advaita Ashrama); <strong>Śaṅkarānanda's</strong> <em>dīpikā</em>; Swāmī <strong>Tyāgīśānanda</strong> (Sri Ramakrishna Math); Swāmī <strong>Krishnananda</strong> &amp; Swāmī <strong>Sivananda</strong> (Divine Life Society); <strong>Max Müller</strong> (<em>Sacred Books of the East</em> 15, 1884), <strong>Paul Deussen</strong> (<em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em>, 1897), and <strong>Patrick Olivelle</strong> (<em>Upaniṣads</em>, OUP 1996).","- <strong>Format:</strong> modelled on the canonical per-verse template of this document series (Devanāgarī → IAST → pada/phrase gloss → literal → paraphrase → 5-commentary digest), extended per adhyāya with <strong>Details</strong>, <strong>Visual representation</strong>, and <strong>Practical steps / observations</strong>. Shared Ṛgvedic/Atharvan/Kaṭha/Muṇḍaka mantras are noted where they occur.","- ⚠️ <strong>Recommendation:</strong> for close textual or liturgical work, spot-check each verse against the accented (<em>sasvara</em>) edition at sanskritdocuments.org and a printed critical edition (e.g. the Adyar or Ramakrishna Math texts); the commentary digests summarise representative positions and are not verbatim quotations."],"kind":"note"}]}]};
